{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '561,1098p' docs/design/dao.stream.md | nl -ba -v 561\"","aggregated_output":"   561\t\n   562\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                                                                                              | Required keys |\n   563\t|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|\n   564\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | The value is accepted at the next position in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |\n   565\t| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\n   566\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |\n   567\t| `:dao.stream/closed`          | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                                         | —             |\n   568\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append; nothing was appended.                                                                                    | —             |\n   569\t\n   570\tAcceptance is handle-relative, as close outcomes are. **Which sequence** a\n   571\thandle's writer surface is on is part of the transport's declared nature: for a\n   572\tring buffer it is the logical stream itself, so `ok` places the value in it; for\n   573\ta transport that carries values toward a stream elsewhere it is the ordered\n   574\toutbound path, and what becomes of the value at the far end is reported there,\n   575\tnot here. No remote transport can say more — under *no operation waits*, an\n   576\tanswer from the far end cannot arrive before `append!` returns.\n   577\t\n   578\tThe writer decides what to do about any non-`ok` outcome; DaoStream does not\n   579\tretry, buffer, or notify on its behalf.\n   580\t\n   581\t## Close\n   582\t\n   583\t`close!` takes a handle and returns a map whose outcome set is `{ok}` — no\n   584\tother outcome exists for it. It is idempotent: closing an already-closed scope\n   585\tis also `:dao.stream/ok`.\n   586\t\n   587\t`ok` is the local lifecycle transition, not a report on releasing host\n   588\tresources: what the handle is on is closed from that moment, irrevocably.\n   589\tA transport whose close must also flush or release something that can fail\n   590\tdeclares where that failure surfaces — an ordinary deposit on a channel it\n   591\tnames — because the outcome set has no room for it and the transition has\n   592\talready happened.\n   593\t\n   594\t`close!` closes **what the handle is on**. A handle explicitly designated by\n   595\tthe transport as the logical stream's owner closes that logical stream; a\n   596\tserver-side accepted-connection handle is an attachment and does not gain\n   597\townership merely by being on the serving host. An attached\n   598\thandle is on an attachment: closing it ends that attachment only — the\n   599\tstream persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` may rejoin\n   600\tlater. Which scope a transport's handles carry is part of its declared\n   601\tnature.\n   602\t\n   603\t`:dao.stream/closed` and `:dao.stream/end` are handle-relative: they speak of\n   604\twhat *this handle* is on. (Not every outcome is: `gap` is retention-relative\n   605\tand `cursor-mismatch` is logical-stream-relative.) After an attachment\n   606\tcloses, the handle's `append!` answers `closed`; `descriptor` still answers\n   607\t`ok` with reachability and identity — both outlive any attachment; a reader surface, where the\n   608\thandle has one, answers `end` from `next` once observation through this\n   609\thandle is exhausted, and `closed` from `cursor` — no new cursors are\n   610\tminted through a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream,\n   611\tnot to a handle, so a kept cursor resumes through a newly attached handle.\n   612\t\n   613\tClosing an attachment freezes what that handle can still observe: values\n   614\tappended to the logical stream afterwards are not observable through it, so\n   615\t`end` is reached at the position the stream had reached when the attachment\n   616\tclosed. And a logical stream that has itself been closed remains attachable —\n   617\t`attach!` answers `ok` and the new handle reads retained history to `end` —\n   618\tbecause close changes future availability, not identity or reachability.\n   619\t\n   620\tClosing the logical stream changes future availability only:\n   621\t\n   622\t- Subsequent `append!` returns `:dao.stream/closed`.\n   623\t- A reader that exhausts retained history receives `:dao.stream/end` instead\n   624\t  of `:dao.stream/blocked`.\n   625\t- Retained history remains readable. Close erases nothing; eviction is a\n   626\t  retention concern, not a lifecycle one.\n   627\t\n   628\tThere is no `closed?` predicate in the public surface. Operation results are\n   629\tauthoritative; a predicate answer is stale the moment it is returned.\n   630\t\n   631\t## Concurrency\n   632\t\n   633\tEach logical stream is one append-only sequence, and a handle's writer\n   634\tsurface is on exactly one sequence — for some transports the logical stream\n   635\titself, for others the ordered outbound path toward a stream elsewhere (see\n   636\tWriting). A successfully appended value occupies exactly one definite position\n   637\tin the sequence this handle's writer surface is on, and every operation's\n   638\toutcome reflects that sequence at one moment. DaoStream guarantees nothing\n   639\tfurther about concurrent operations.\n   640\t\n   641\tThis follows from Axiom 2. The sequence a handle's surfaces are on is the one\n   642\ttruth for that handle; each outcome is one perspective on it at one moment. A\n   643\treader that observes\n   644\t`:dao.stream/blocked` and then a value has not caught the stream in a\n   645\tcontradiction — it made two observations from its own cursor, and no global\n   646\tview exists to contradict. Whether an append landed before a close is\n   647\tanswered by the sequence the handle's writer surface is on — the value has a\n   648\tposition in it or it does not — and where that sequence is not observable from\n   649\tthis handle, the answer is read where it is.\n   650\tWhat an ordering between writers *means*, and any coordination among them —\n   651\ta merge interpreter, a one-log-per-writer discipline — is interpretation,\n   652\tand interpretation happens above the stream.\n   653\t\n   654\t## Retention and Gaps\n   655\t\n   656\tRetention and overflow behavior are transport-owned and form part of the\n   657\tstream or writer surface's declared nature; a locally created stream records\n   658\tthem through its creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts\n   659\tthe oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send\n   660\tbuffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —\n   661\t`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or\n   662\tpermanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the\n   663\toutcomes and standardizes no policy.\n   664\t\n   665\t**Eviction never waits on a reader.** Retention is the stream's own declared\n   666\tpolicy and applies whatever any reader is doing — a stream holds no reader\n   667\tpositions, so it cannot be held back by a slow one. A transport that deferred\n   668\teviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's\n   669\tabsence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\n   670\treader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\n   671\tdeliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a cursor that falls behind\n   672\treceives a `gap`.\n   673\t\n   674\tWhen bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\n   675\tgap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\n   676\tnothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\n   677\ttelemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently\n   678\tskipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader holding a cursor across an\n   679\teviction is told it missed values. This is a promise to a **cursor**, not to a\n   680\thandle: it is precisely what a kept cursor is worth, and it is why a consumer\n   681\tthat must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\n   682\t\n   683\t### Complete history\n   684\t\n   685\tSome consumers require *complete* history rather than *retained* history: an\n   686\tinterpreter that derives state by replaying a log from its beginning is wrong,\n   687\tnot merely stale, if it replays a suffix. The contract already carries what\n   688\tsuch a consumer needs, in two mechanisms that answer different questions.\n   689\t\n   690\t**A transport that excludes `gap` gives completeness.** This is the existing\n   691\tdeclaration in its existing form — the outcome's precondition is impossible by\n   692\tthe transport's nature, an unbounded log never evicts, so never reports `gap`\n   693\t(see *Surfaces*). Retention is declared through the transport's nature and, for\n   694\ta locally created stream, through its creation specification; it is\n   695\tconfiguration provenance. There is no way to ask a handle about it and none is\n   696\tadded. A consumer requiring complete history therefore requires *a transport of\n   697\tthat declared nature*, and the composition that wires it is what supplies one.\n   698\t\n   699\t**A kept origin cursor gives detection.** On a transport that can evict, an\n   700\torigin cursor turns a loss that would otherwise be invisible into a reported\n   701\t`gap`, and the consumer aborts or recovers on its own policy. It does not make\n   702\tthe history complete; it makes incompleteness observable.\n   703\t\n   704\tBoth stand, and they are not substitutes. Where completeness is a correctness\n   705\trequirement, only the declared transport delivers it, and a kept origin cursor\n   706\tis the honest failure mode that remains when completeness was not wired. Where\n   707\ta consumer can proceed on a suffix but must know that it is on one, the origin\n   708\tcursor is right and sufficient.\n   709\t\n   710\t**Wiring a log onto an evicting transport is a host assembly defect**, of the\n   711\tsame kind as wiring a deposit destination that can refuse. A durable log is not\n   712\ta window: if a consumer treats a stream as the record of everything that\n   713\thappened, the transport under it must declare that it retains everything.\n   714\tCapacity is the wrong knob for this — a larger window makes loss less likely\n   715\tand never makes it reported — so sizing never substitutes for the declaration.\n   716\t\n   717\tThe obligation a complete-history transport takes on is one sentence: **a\n   718\tcomplete-history reader begins at its logical sequence's origin and never\n   719\tevicts an element of that sequence.** Every element present at creation, and\n   720\tevery value an append places on that same sequence with `ok`, remains part of\n   721\tit for as long as the logical stream exists. If the medium cannot serve\n   722\tretained history it reports `transport-error`; it never silently substitutes a\n   723\tsuffix. Everything else follows. Its `:oldest` anchor stays at the origin, so\n   724\t`gap` is impossible and is excluded for that reason.\n   725\t\n   726\tWhat it owes on the writing side depends on what it is. A complete-history\n   727\ttransport need not be writable at all — a finite immutable history is a valid\n   728\tone, and owes no writer outcome. A writable one that declares a finite capacity\n   729\treturns `full` on reaching it, without appending and without evicting: refusing\n   730\tand evicting are the two answers to the same condition, and a transport that has\n   731\tgiven up one owes the other. A writable one with no declared capacity — a\n   732\tlogically unbounded log, however it grows or spills beneath — may exclude\n   733\t`full`. Logically unbounded is not physically infinite, and the line runs\n   734\tbetween failures an operation can observe and return from and those it cannot:\n   735\tunexpected exhaustion the transport can observe and return from is\n   736\t`transport-error`, while fatal host or runtime exhaustion, from which the\n   737\toperation produces no result at all, lies outside the outcome algebra\n   738\taltogether. Neither is ever converted into the eviction of acknowledged\n   739\thistory. A transport whose every operation either completes its state\n   740\ttransition or does not return therefore has no firing condition for\n   741\t`transport-error` and excludes it like any other outcome.\n   742\t\n   743\t## Composition\n   744\t\n   745\tDaoStream defines only streams, cursors, and the operations above. Anything\n   746\tthat transforms one stream into another is an interpreter — it reads via its\n   747\town cursor and appends to an ordinary output stream — and that lives entirely\n   748\toutside this contract, needing no support from it. As in a Unix pipeline, the\n   749\tpipe is dumb; the transforming happens in the processes on either end.\n   750\t\n   751\tA forwarder between streams is a single step (not a callback loop) over the\n   752\ttransport-agnostic stream algebra. Because the stream never blocks, cadence is\n   753\towned by the composition-supplied driver that repeatedly calls the step,\n   754\tyielding execution as needed by the host runtime.\n   755\t\n   756\tFlow control (a reader pausing a sender) is an interpreter's concern. A pause\n   757\thas to be a lease and not a switch to avoid permanently stuck states. Those\n   758\tsemantics live in `dao.lease.md`, whose facts are plain data on ordinary\n   759\tstreams. DaoStream provides the honest baseline: a cursor that falls\n   760\tbehind is evicted past and told so with a `gap`, and the reader holding it\n   761\tdecides what that means.\n   762\t\n   763\t## Explicitly Absent\n   764\t\n   765\tAbsent from the public surface, by derivation from the invariants:\n   766\t\n   767\t- **an ambient registry** — a namespace-global dispatch table, or registration\n   768\t  as a load-time side effect. Host dispatch is ordinary data the host owns.\n   769\t- **a `closed?` predicate** — a predicate answer is stale the moment it\n   770\t  returns; operation results are authoritative.\n   771\t- **destructive reads** — a read that removes what it observes makes one\n   772\t  reader's progress every other reader's data loss. It is coherent only while\n   773\t  exactly one observer exists; a forwarder, an indexer, or a DHT replica makes\n   774\t  it theft. Where the capability went, so no later plan reinvents it: **taking\n   775\t  is a write.** Removing a tuple from an append-only log is not a mutation but\n   776\t  an assertion that it no longer holds, which `dao.space` already expresses as\n   777\t  a retraction datom — Linda's `in` is observe-then-retract, not a stream\n   778\t  operation. And the hard part of a take was never the removal but the\n   779\t  *exclusion* of competing takers, which is a lease over datoms\n   780\t  (`dao.lease.md`), not something a stream can promise. v1's `drain-one!`\n   781\t  appeared to solve exclusion only because it ran in one process against a\n   782\t  local ring buffer.\n   783\t- **`seek`** — every valid cursor comes from the stream; anchored minting and\n   784\t  kept cursors cover repositioning.\n   785\t- **a blocking take** — no operation waits.\n   786\t- **waiter registration** — there is no readiness extension.\n   787\t- **throwing conveniences** — operational outcomes are data.\n   788\t- **a retention or completeness predicate** — retention is declared, through\n   789\t  the transport's nature and the creation specification. Asking a handle would\n   790\t  be the same defect as asking whether it is closed: the answer is\n   791\t  configuration the composition already holds, and reading it back at runtime\n   792\t  invites code that branches on what it should have been wired with.\n   793\t\n   794\t## Open Decisions\n   795\t\n   796\tStatus: **proposed, binding nothing.** Every other section of this document is\n   797\tcontract; this one is not. Each entry names an assumption the contract makes\n   798\twithout stating it, the use that would break it, and a proposed resolution.\n   799\tAn entry leaves this section in one of two ways: accepted, its text moves into\n   800\tthe section it amends and the entry is deleted; or rejected, the entry is\n   801\treplaced by one sentence in *Explicitly Absent* recording why. Drafted\n   802\t2026-09-19 from a review of this document against `dao.stream`, its three\n   803\ttransports (`ringbuffer`, `memory-log`, `ws`), and their consumers.\n   804\t\n   805\tOD-1 to OD-4 are ordered by cost of delay. Each is a paragraph today, while\n   806\tthree transports and a handful of consumers exist, and a migration once a\n   807\tdurable or replicated transport ships. OD-5 was added afterwards and is\n   808\tnumbered by arrival; it concerns the layers above this contract rather than the\n   809\tcontract's own operations.\n   810\t\n   811\t### OD-1. An unrecognized outcome has no defined meaning\n   812\t\n   813\t**The assumption.** The Result Convention makes each operation's outcome set\n   814\texhaustive and makes result *maps* open. The two rules are asymmetric: a future\n   815\textension may add a key, never an outcome. The contract assumes the seven\n   816\toutcome sets are complete for every transport that will ever exist.\n   817\t\n   818\t**Evidence.** Consumers are already total over the closed sets and treat\n   819\tanything else as a fault: `dao.stream.observe/step` classifies an unrecognized\n   820\tread or effect outcome as `transport-error` and reports `:defect` or `:failed`;\n   821\t`yin.vm.engine/handle-put` and `handle-next` throw. *Envelopes* declines to\n   822\treserve an outcome for gating and says gating \"arrives as an addition\", but\n   823\tunder the exhaustive rule a new outcome is not an addition.\n   824\t\n   825\t**What breaks.** Any condition the present outcomes cannot express honestly:\n   826\tattachment or append refused for lack of authority; a descriptor that is valid\n   827\tbut served elsewhere now; a transport that is throttling rather than full; an\n   828\tappend whose effect is unknown (OD-2). Each must either be squeezed into an\n   829\texisting outcome that misdescribes it (`not-found` for unauthorized, `full` for\n   830\tthrottled) or break every consumer.\n   831\t\n   832\t**Proposed resolution.** Keep each outcome set exhaustive *for this version of\n   833\tthe contract* and add one rule to the Result Convention that makes later\n   834\toutcomes safe to introduce:\n   835\t\n   836\t> A consumer that receives an outcome it does not recognize treats it as: not\n   837\t> `ok`; nothing observed and nothing appended; and not retryable, unless the\n   838\t> result map carries `:dao.stream/retry? true`. It does not throw, because an\n   839\t> unrecognized outcome is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's\n   840\t> assembly.\n   841\t\n   842\tWith that rule a newer transport degrades to \"refused, do not retry\" on an\n   843\tolder consumer instead of to an exception, and `:dao.stream/retry?` lets it\n   844\tsay when waiting is the right response. The existing `full` and `blocked` keep\n   845\ttheir meanings and need not carry the key.\n   846\t\n   847\t**Cost if deferred.** Every consumer written in the meantime hard-codes\n   848\t\"unknown means defect\", and the first new outcome is a coordinated change\n   849\tacross all of them.\n   850\t\n   851\t### OD-2. `append!` assumes its own effect is always knowable\n   852\t\n   853\t**The assumption.** *Writing* defines `transport-error` as \"the transport\n   854\tfailed to perform the append; nothing was appended\", and `ok` as acceptance at\n   855\ta definite position. Both assume the transport knows, at return time, whether\n   856\tthe value is in the sequence.\n   857\t\n   858\t**Evidence.** True of all three transports today: `ringbuffer` and `memory-log`\n   859\tare single in-memory state transitions, and `ws` sidesteps the question because\n   860\tits writer surface is the outbound path, so `ok` claims only enqueueing. No\n   861\ttransport yet has a medium that can fail *after* accepting bytes.\n   862\t\n   863\t**What breaks.** A durable file whose write succeeds and whose sync fails; a\n   864\tlog replicated to a quorum; an append forwarded to a remote sequencer. Each has\n   865\ta third state, *unknown*, that the table cannot express. A consumer written\n   866\tagainst the present text retries on `transport-error`, which is correct only if\n   867\tnothing was appended, and duplicates the value when something was.\n   868\t\n   869\tTwo related gaps compound it. `append!`'s `ok` carries no correlation value, so\n   870\twhere the real answer is displaced to another channel (*Writing*: \"what becomes\n   871\tof the value at the far end is reported there, not here\") nothing in the\n   872\tcontract ties that answer to the append that caused it. `attach!` has\n   873\t`:dao.stream/attachment` for exactly this; `append!` has no counterpart. And\n   874\t`ok` promises neither readability nor delivery nor durability, with no named\n   875\tway to learn any of the three later.\n   876\t\n   877\t**Proposed resolution.** Three sentences, no new operation:\n   878\t\n   879\t1. Reword `transport-error` on `append!`: \"The transport failed to perform the\n   880\t   append. Whether the value was appended is unknown unless the transport\n   881\t   declares that its failures are clean.\" The three existing transports declare\n   882\t   clean failure, so nothing changes for them.\n   883\t2. State the rule the RPC layer already follows: **deduplication and\n   884\t   correlation are the payload's.** A writer that must retry safely puts an\n   885\t   identity in the value; a reader that must not act twice deduplicates on it.\n   886\t   The stream never promises exactly-once.\n   887\t3. Name where later answers land: a transport whose acceptance is not its\n   888\t   final answer (durability reached, far-end refusal) declares the channel on\n   889\t   which that answer is deposited, as *Close* already requires for a failing\n   890\t   flush.\n   891\t\n   892\t**Cost if deferred.** The first durable transport either lies (reports clean\n   893\tfailure it cannot guarantee) or changes the meaning of an outcome that every\n   894\tretry loop already depends on.\n   895\t\n   896\t### OD-3. A cursor binds to the logical stream but is shaped by one transport\n   897\t\n   898\t**The assumption.** *Cursors* promises that \"any handle on the same logical\n   899\tstream accepts it\", and *Envelopes* allows that \"different descriptors may\n   900\treach the same logical stream through different endpoints\". *Cursors* also says\n   901\t\"a cursor's representation belongs to the transport\". Together these hold only\n   902\tif a logical stream lives on exactly one transport for its whole life.\n   903\t\n   904\t**Evidence.** Cursor maps are keyed in the minting transport's namespace\n   905\t(`:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity` and `/position`,\n   906\t`:dao.stream.memory-log/identity` and `/position`), so a cursor from one\n   907\ttransport is `invalid-cursor` on any other, whatever identity it names. Across\n   908\thosts the promise has never been exercised: `ws` has no reader surface, so\n   909\tinbound values are deposited on a local stream with its own identity and its\n   910\town positions. What a remote host reads is a copy, not the stream. Finally,\n   911\t\"whether a cursor survives serialization to another host is transport-owned and\n   912\tTBD\", while *Explicitly Absent* removes `seek`: a kept cursor is therefore the\n   913\tonly checkpoint a consumer has, and whether it can be written down is\n   914\tundecided.\n   915\t\n   916\t**What breaks.** Replication and DHT replicas (which replica's positions are\n   917\tthe stream's?); re-homing a stream from memory to a durable medium without\n   918\tinvalidating every kept cursor; a durable consumer resuming after restart;\n   919\tcomparing two replicas to detect a forked history.\n   920\t\n   921\t**Proposed resolution.** Two decisions, the first a choice:\n   922\t\n   923\t1. **Choose what a logical identity ranges over.** Either\n   924\t   (a) *one origin, one transport*: a logical stream is the sequence one\n   925\t   transport instance owns. A copy on another host or medium is a different\n   926\t   logical stream with its own identity; that it mirrors the first is a fact for an\n   927\t   interpreter to record. This is how the code already behaves:\n   928\t   `dao.stream.forward` copies into a destination with its own identity. The\n   929\t   sentence\n   930\t   about different descriptors reaching one stream narrows to \"different\n   931\t   endpoints of the same owning transport\". Or\n   932\t   (b) *contract-level position*: the contract defines a position within an\n   933\t   identity (an ordinal), cursors carry it under a `:dao.stream/…` key, and any\n   934\t   transport serving that identity honors it.\n   935\t   Recommended: (a). It matches Axiom 2 (sameness between sequences is an\n   936\t   interpretation), costs a paragraph, and leaves (b) available later as an\n   937\t   additive key. (b) commits every future transport, including ones with no\n   938\t   natural ordinal, now.\n   939\t2. **Settle serialization.** Replace \"TBD\" with: \"A cursor is plain data and\n   940\t   survives the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, under the same\n   941\t   rule as the envelopes. A cursor that outlives the stream it names yields\n   942\t   `not-found` at `attach!` or `cursor-mismatch` at `next`, never a defect.\"\n   943\t   Both existing transports already satisfy this.\n   944\t\n   945\t**Cost if deferred.** The first durable or replicated transport decides both\n   946\tquestions implicitly, by whatever its cursor happens to look like, and every\n   947\tconsumer that persisted a cursor inherits that accident.\n   948\t\n   949\t### OD-4. A logical stream can close but cannot end\n   950\t\n   951\t**The assumption.** *Complete history* obliges a transport to retain every\n   952\telement \"for as long as the logical stream exists\", and *Close* says close\n   953\t\"erases nothing\". Nothing says how a logical stream stops existing. The\n   954\tcontract assumes storage is either bounded by eviction or kept forever.\n   955\t\n   956\t**Evidence.** There is no destroy, truncate, compact, or redact anywhere in the\n   957\tsurface or in any transport. `memory-log` ends only when its process does. What\n   958\t`next`, `cursor`, or `attach!` answer for a stream whose medium was removed out\n   959\tof band is undefined; the nearest outcomes are `transport-error` and\n   960\t`not-found`, and the document chooses neither.\n   961\t\n   962\t**What breaks.** Reclaiming disk under a durable log; snapshot-and-truncate\n   963\tcompaction, the ordinary way a replayed log stays bounded; erasure that law or\n   964\ta user requires. All three are forbidden to a transport that declared complete\n   965\thistory, and consumers are wired to that declaration as a correctness\n   966\trequirement.\n   967\t\n   968\t**Proposed resolution.**\n   969\t\n   970\t1. Name a third retention nature beside *evicting* and *complete*:\n   971\t   **compacted**. A compacted transport may drop a prefix it has summarized. A\n   972\t   cursor into the dropped prefix receives `gap` exactly as under eviction, and\n   973\t   the result map may carry, under a transport-owned key, a descriptor or\n   974\t   content address for the summary that replaces the prefix. A consumer that\n   975\t   requires complete history is not wired to one; a consumer that can resume\n   976\t   from a summary is. No new outcome is needed.\n   977\t2. State the end of existence: **destruction is transport-owned**, like any\n   978\t   operation outside the seven. After it, `attach!` answers `not-found`; a live\n   979\t   handle's `next` and `cursor` answer `end` if the handle was closed first and\n   980\t   `transport-error` otherwise; `descriptor` still answers `ok`, because a name\n   981\t   outlives what it named. Redaction of a single element is not a stream\n   982\t   operation at all: under *taking is a write* it is a retraction datom, and\n   983\t   what must be unreadable is made so by discarding a key held elsewhere.\n   984\t\n   985\t**Cost if deferred.** The first durable transport ships as \"complete\", gains\n   986\tconsumers that depend on that word, and can then never reclaim space without\n   987\tbreaking them.\n   988\t\n   989\t### OD-5. \"No operation waits\" binds the seven operations and nothing above them\n   990\t\n   991\t**The assumption.** *The IO Model* argues that a blocking contract is\n   992\t\"unimplementable on most of the targets\" and that a callback contract \"inverts\n   993\tcontrol on all of them\", and concludes that non-blocking polling is the only\n   994\tmodel that means the same thing on clj, cljs, and cljd. The argument is about\n   995\thosts, not about streams, so it applies with equal force to any interface an\n   996\tinterpreter publishes. The contract nevertheless binds only its own seven\n   997\toperations. It assumes the layers above will inherit the model without being\n   998\ttold to.\n   999\t\n  1000\t**Evidence.** Content lookup, the one random-access service in the system, is\n  1001\tcurrently published in three shapes:\n  1002\t\n  1003\t- **Waiting.** `dao.jing/get` is `(get handle address not-found) -> value`, and\n  1004\t  `materialize!` returns an address \"only after the backend reports success\".\n  1005\t  Over a local store this is one in-memory or file read. Over a remote store it\n  1006\t  must wait, which `dao.jing.md` records honestly: `connect-content!` is \"a\n  1007\t  blocking driver as host policy… a JVM thread polls and sleeps\", and both\n  1008\t  halves of `dao.jing.remote` are JVM-only. Nine call sites depend on this\n  1009\t  shape: three in `dao.space.index` (index walks and manifest reads), four in\n  1010\t  `dao.data.btree.storage`, two in `yin.vm.content`.\n  1011\t- **Callback.** `dao.data.btree.storage/hydrate-async` takes `on-ok` and\n  1012\t  `on-err` or returns a Promise / Future / CompletableFuture, tracking\n  1013\t  completion in atoms, over an async content handle\n  1014\t  (`:get-content-async-fn`). This is the second of the three IO models, the\n  1015\t  one this document rejects by name, and it sits one layer above the contract.\n  1016\t- **Stepped.** `dao.jing.remote.step` is \"a pure function over explicit data…\n  1017\t  no socket, atom, promise, callback, or scheduler\": `request-get`, `step`,\n  1018\t  `abandon` over `dao.stream.rpc`. This is the third model, and the only one of\n  1019\t  the three shapes that is the same on every host.\n  1020\t\n  1021\t`dao.jing.md` already lists the consequences as deferred items (*The content\n  1022\twrite path as an effect stream*, *Async hydration*). What is undecided is not\n  1023\twhether the problem exists but which shape is the interface.\n  1024\t\n  1025\t**What breaks.** Any interpreter that must reach content it does not hold\n  1026\tlocally, on a host that cannot wait: a browser or Dart agent querying a remote\n  1027\t`dao.space` index; a VM loading code by address from another node\n  1028\t(`yin.vm.content`); a game client fetching meshes or textures by address.\n  1029\tToday each has two options. It can hydrate the whole reachable graph before\n  1030\treading, which turns a selective index read into a full download. Or it can\n  1031\tadopt the callback shape, which reintroduces inverted control into every\n  1032\tcaller above it, the viral cost *Why this model and not the other two*\n  1033\tdescribes.\n  1034\t\n  1035\tThe general form matters more than this instance. Every future service that\n  1036\tanswers a question (a lookup, a query against a remote peer, a lease grant, a\n  1037\tname resolution) faces the same three shapes. Without a rule each will choose\n  1038\tby local convenience, and the convenient choice on the JVM is the one that\n  1039\tdoes not port.\n  1040\t\n  1041\t**Proposed resolution.** One rule, stated here because this is where the\n  1042\targument for it lives, and one consequence, stated in `dao.jing.md`:\n  1043\t\n  1044\t1. Add to *Composition*:\n  1045\t\n  1046\t   > The IO model is inherited. An interpreter's portable interface is\n  1047\t   > expressed the way this contract's is: a step that returns what is true\n  1048\t   > now, including \"not yet\", over streams the caller supplied. A waiting\n  1049\t   > call or a callback may wrap that step as **host policy**, named as such\n  1050\t   > and confined to the hosts that can afford it, exactly as\n  1051\t   > `connect-content!` wraps the stepped client. It is never the interface\n  1052\t   > another interpreter is written against.\n  1053\t\n  1054\t2. In `dao.jing.md`, name the stepped request/response pair as the\n  1055\t   agent-facing interface to content, and reclassify the synchronous handle\n  1056\t   (`:get-content-fn`) as what it already is in practice: the store\n  1057\t   interpreter's boundary adapter onto its own medium, plus a cache readers may\n  1058\t   consult. The handle map does not change. What changes is which of the three\n  1059\t   shapes new code is allowed to depend on.\n  1060\t\n  1061\tTwo costs follow and should be accepted with open eyes. The tree walks in\n  1062\t`dao.space.index` and `dao.data.btree.storage` become steppable: fetch, receive\n  1063\t\"not yet\", return state, resume when the response is observed. Inside `yin.vm`\n  1064\tthis is the existing park and wait-set path; in library code it is the shape\n  1065\t`forward-step` and `rpc/poll!` already have. And `hydrate-async` is either\n  1066\trewritten as a step or explicitly labelled host policy. The hydration cache\n  1067\titself survives unchanged: prefetching a graph so that a walk never misses is a\n  1068\tlegitimate optimization of a stepped interface, not an alternative to one.\n  1069\t\n  1070\t**Cost if deferred.** The nine call sites grow. Every new consumer of content\n  1071\twritten on the JVM is written against the waiting shape, and each one is a\n  1072\tseparate port when the first browser or Dart agent needs to read a remote\n  1073\tindex.\n  1074\t\n  1075\t### Recorded, not proposed\n  1076\t\n  1077\tThese came out of the same review. Each is reachable by addition under the\n  1078\trules the contract already has, so none needs a decision now. They are listed\n  1079\tso that a later plan does not rediscover them as surprises.\n  1080\t\n  1081\t- **A writer does not learn where its value landed.** `append!`'s `ok` carries\n  1082\t  no cursor. Read-your-writes and causal references between streams arrive as\n  1083\t  an optional `:dao.stream/cursor` key on `ok`. Conditional append (\"only if\n  1084\t  the tail is still here\") would be an eighth operation; the design does not\n  1085\t  need it because it assumes **one writer per logical stream**, which\n  1086\t  `dao.space` practices and this document should say once in *Concurrency*.\n  1087\t- **History is not verifiable.** Identity is an opaque value nothing\n  1088\t  authenticates, and append-only is the transport's promise, not something a\n  1089\t  reader can check. Self-certifying identities and signed or hash-chained\n  1090\t  elements live in the payload, above the stream. The obligation on this layer\n  1091\t  is only the one it already meets: identity is compared structurally and never\n  1092\t  parsed.\n  1093\t- **Readiness.** Polling is O(n) in streams observed (*What it costs*). The\n  1094\t  extension is reserved, and `dao.stream.waitset.implementation-plan.md` pays\n  1095\t  the cost in the runtime without it.\n  1096\t- **Granularity.** An element is a whole value; there is no byte offset within\n  1097\t  one and no chunked element. Large media travel as content addresses in the\n  1098\t  payload, resolved through `dao.jing`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' docs/design/dao.stream.md | nl -ba\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' docs/design/dao.stream.md | nl -ba\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream Design\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: agreed design target. This document is the DaoStream contract. It does\n     4\tnot redesign Yin.VM. Yin.VM is a downstream consumer and will be addressed only\n     5\tafter this contract is implemented and tested.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Purpose\n     8\t\n     9\tThis contract derives from the foundational axioms and non-negotiable\n    10\tinvariants of [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). Two axioms bear on it\n    11\tdirectly, named here once so the sections below can cite rather than restate\n    12\tthem:\n    13\t\n    14\t1. **Everything is a Stream** — all IO and data flow through append-only\n    15\t   streams.\n    16\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics** — data is syntax; semantics emerge\n    17\t   only through interpretation. One truth, many perspectives.\n    18\t\n    19\tDaoStream is the passive substrate for IO in datom.world — passive because\n    20\tinterpretation and execution must not collapse into one layer. Axiom 1 is\n    21\twhat makes this the layer every host boundary crosses. A stream carries\n    22\tvalues and decides nothing about them:\n    23\t\n    24\t- **What the values mean** is not its business — Axiom 2. An element\n    25\t  judged, filtered, or typed at this layer destroys the perspectives nobody\n    26\t  has taken yet.\n    27\t- **Which interpreter observes them** is not recorded — a registry of who\n    28\t  is listening would be hidden global state.\n    29\t- **When an interpreter runs** is not its decision — \"do not introduce\n    30\t  callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream\", seen from the stream's\n    31\t  side: DaoStream invokes nothing, so control is never inverted and\n    32\t  causality stays explicit (see The IO Model).\n    33\t\n    34\tThe Invariants below are these denials in operational form.\n    35\t\n    36\tThe design separates five concepts:\n    37\t\n    38\t1. A **creation specification** is data for creating a new logical stream.\n    39\t2. A **handle** is a host-local operational stream value — the\n    40\t   implementation of the `dao.stream` protocols.\n    41\t3. A **logical-stream identity** is transport-independent plain data shared by\n    42\t   every handle and descriptor for one stream.\n    43\t4. A **portable descriptor** is reachability data used to attach to an existing\n    44\t   stream; it includes the logical-stream identity but is not that identity.\n    45\t5. A **cursor** is immutable interpreter-owned observation state for one\n    46\t   logical stream.\n    47\t\n    48\tCreation is not attachment. A descriptor is not a creation specification or an\n    49\tidentity. A handle is not portable identity. A cursor is not handle-owned\n    50\tstate.\n    51\t\n    52\tSeven operations range over them, each returning an outcome map (see\n    53\tResult Convention):\n    54\t\n    55\t| Operation    | Consumes               | Yields             |\n    56\t|--------------|------------------------|--------------------|\n    57\t| `create!`    | creation specification | handle outcome     |\n    58\t| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |\n    59\t| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |\n    60\t| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |\n    61\t| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |\n    62\t| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |\n    63\t| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |\n    64\t\n    65\tA handle implements the subset of the **handle surface** its transport\n    66\tdeclares (see Surfaces). `create!` and `attach!` are not part of it: they\n    67\tconsume no handle and produce one.\n    68\t\n    69\t## Invariants\n    70\t\n    71\t- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.\n    72\t- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.\n    73\t- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.\n    74\t- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.\n    75\t- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.\n    76\t- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase\n    77\t  retained history.\n    78\t- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n    79\t  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n    80\t- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.\n    81\t- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is\n    82\t  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.\n    83\t- No hidden global state. DaoStream keeps no registry of streams, handles, or\n    84\t  observers, and no operation consults ambient state.\n    85\t- A logical stream has an identity that is plain data, the same through every\n    86\t  handle on it, stable across attachment lifecycles and across serialization.\n    87\t  Projecting that identity never fails.\n    88\t\n    89\tEvery clause below must be consistent with these invariants, and most derive\n    90\tfrom one directly. Where a clause is instead an interoperability decision — the\n    91\tdescriptor's properties, the result keyword namespace, the anchor set — it is\n    92\tstated as a decision rather than dressed as a deduction. A proposed addition\n    93\tthat contradicts an invariant does not belong in this contract; one that merely\n    94\tcannot be deduced from them must say which decision it is and why.\n    95\t\n    96\t## Result Convention\n    97\t\n    98\tEvery DaoStream operation returns data.\n    99\t\n   100\t- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n   101\t  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of\n   102\t  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n   103\t  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n   104\t  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n   105\t  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n   106\t- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n   107\t  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n   108\t  addition to it.\n   109\t- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per\n   110\t  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n   111\t  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n   112\t  added without breaking existing consumers.\n   113\t- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of\n   114\t  DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an\n   115\t  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,\n   116\t  malformed input, and transport defects are all outcomes, never exceptions\n   117\t  and never callbacks.\n   118\t- All qualified keywords in results live under `:dao.stream/…`.\n   119\t\n   120\t## The IO Model\n   121\t\n   122\tThree models are available for expressing IO. DaoStream is deliberately the\n   123\tthird.\n   124\t\n   125\t**Blocking (synchronous) IO.** The operation parks until it has an answer,\n   126\tthen returns it. Control flow stays sequential and the call site reads as\n   127\tordinary code, which is the whole of its appeal. The cost is that something\n   128\tmust wait — a thread per pending operation, held for its duration — and it\n   129\trequires a host that can park at all.\n   130\t\n   131\t**Callback (asynchronous) IO.** The operation registers a continuation and\n   132\treturns at once; the runtime invokes the continuation when the answer\n   133\tarrives. Nothing waits, so one thread carries many pending operations: this\n   134\tis the model that scales. Its cost is inverted control. The caller no longer\n   135\tdecides when it observes the answer, and the inversion is viral — on a host\n   136\twithout blocking, a promise cannot be absorbed inside a function, because\n   137\tawaiting returns to the caller at the first suspension point and makes the\n   138\tcaller's own result a promise in turn. Whatever is reached from a callback\n   139\tis reached from a callback.\n   140\t\n   141\t**Non-blocking polling.** The operation returns immediately with whatever is\n   142\ttrue now, including the answer \"nothing yet, ask again.\" Nothing parks and\n   143\tnothing is inverted: the caller keeps control and decides when to ask again.\n   144\tThis is POSIX `O_NONBLOCK` without `select`, and it is DaoStream's model.\n   145\t\n   146\t`next` is total and non-blocking (see Reading): every state of the stream\n   147\thas a defined data answer, and `:dao.stream/blocked` is `EAGAIN` — this\n   148\tposition holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same\n   149\tanswer for writes. No operation in this contract parks, and none takes a\n   150\tcallback; the Result Convention's \"never exceptions and never callbacks\" is\n   151\tthis model stated as a rule about results.\n   152\t\n   153\tThe rule generalizes past `next`: **no operation waits.** An operation that\n   154\tcannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is\n   155\tcalled, and the eventual answer arrives the way every other fact does — as\n   156\tdata, observed later, through an ordinary operation or on a stream. A\n   157\tDaoStream operation is never a request for something to happen and be\n   158\treported back. It is a question about now.\n   159\t\n   160\t### Why this model and not the other two\n   161\t\n   162\tThe hosts do not offer the same choices. The JVM has both blocking and\n   163\tasynchronous IO. JavaScript and Dart have only the asynchronous kind: there\n   164\tis no blocking read to call and no way to wait for one, because the single\n   165\tevent loop that would deliver the answer is the one a waiting call would be\n   166\toccupying — waiting deadlocks rather than merely costing a thread.\n   167\t\n   168\tA blocking contract is therefore unimplementable on most of the targets. A\n   169\tcallback contract is implementable on all of them and inverts control on all\n   170\tof them, including the host that never needed it. Non-blocking polling is\n   171\tthe only one of the three that means the same thing everywhere — the same\n   172\toperations, the same outcome sets, the same control flow — which is what\n   173\tlets one surface serve clj, cljs, and cljd without the shape of the API\n   174\tdiffering by host.\n   175\t\n   176\t### What it costs\n   177\t\n   178\tPolling without readiness notification is O(n) in streams observed: a reader\n   179\tholding ten thousand idle streams must ask all ten thousand to learn which\n   180\thave data. This is `select` without `select`, and it is a real limit rather\n   181\tthan an oversight — the extension that answers it is reserved below. The\n   182\tcost also lands where it can be paid: DaoStream schedules nothing, so\n   183\tcadence belongs to the runtime driving the interpreters, and a readiness\n   184\tmechanism belongs there too.\n   185\t\n   186\t### The readiness extension\n   187\t\n   188\tThis contract has no readiness or waiter extension. A reader that receives\n   189\t`:dao.stream/blocked` retries `next` when it chooses; retry cadence is the\n   190\tconcern of the interpreter or the runtime driving it, not of the stream.\n   191\t\n   192\tA future readiness extension would be additive: an optional protocol on\n   193\ttransports that support it, plus optional keys in operation results (for\n   194\texample a wake token list on `append!`), which the open-map rule already\n   195\tpermits. Nothing in this contract needs to change for that; no consumer of\n   196\tthis contract may depend on it existing. The standing answer to *What it\n   197\tcosts* is `dao.stream.waitset`: poll-based classification over a complete\n   198\tordered sweep, with the host cadence layer deciding when the sweep runs —\n   199\twhich is why no consumer needs the reserved extension to sleep between\n   200\trounds, and the contract remains untouched by it.\n   201\t\n   202\t## Creation and Attachment\n   203\t\n   204\tCreation brings a new logical stream into existence. Attachment joins an\n   205\texisting one. They are separate operations with separate inputs.\n   206\t\n   207\t- `create!` consumes a creation specification and returns a handle on a\n   208\t  new logical stream. `descriptor` on that handle returns `{ok}` with both\n   209\t  `:dao.stream/descriptor`, the transport-specific reachability value, and\n   210\t  `:dao.stream/identity`, the transport-independent logical-stream identity.\n   211\t  The descriptor carries the same identity internally; the sibling projection\n   212\t  and its value inside `:dao.stream/descriptor` must be structurally equal.\n   213\t  Both projections are total and outlive any attachment, so `descriptor` has\n   214\t  no failure outcome.\n   215\t- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on an\n   216\t  attachment to the **same** logical stream the descriptor names. Attaching\n   217\t  never creates.\n   218\t- A creation specification says how to build a stream. A descriptor only\n   219\t  names one — never executable code, never a construction program, never a\n   220\t  request to load a plugin.\n   221\t\n   222\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams — up to a serialization\n   223\tboundary. An in-memory stream whose admission declaration permits host\n   224\treferences can carry a handle intact. A dynamically delivered handle travels\n   225\tas composition data together with its declared surface, for example\n   226\t`{:dao.stream/handle h :dao.stream/surface #{:reader :writer}}`; the receiving\n   227\tinterpreter uses only that declared surface, and attaching to what it already\n   228\tholds is meaningless. A composition may omit the wrapper only when the\n   229\treceiver's fixed contract already declares the surface. But a handle does not\n   230\tsurvive encoding, and any\n   231\ttransport that encodes its elements — a file as much as a network socket —\n   232\tis a serialization boundary. Descriptors exist for crossing those\n   233\tboundaries, where the live value cannot travel and a name must. (What a\n   234\ttransport can carry is transport-owned: handing an encoding transport a\n   235\tvalue it cannot encode is reported as data like any other outcome.)\n   236\t`attach!` therefore consumes only descriptors, and\n   237\tresolves them against the transport's inherent operational state — the\n   238\tstreams a network endpoint serves because serving them is what it is, the\n   239\tfiles a filesystem holds. There is no stream table in this contract: no\n   240\tregistry maps descriptors to live handles, so a descriptor arriving\n   241\tsomewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other\n   242\thost, or on its own host absent a directory some composition chose to keep —\n   243\thonestly resolves to `:dao.stream/not-found`.\n   244\t\n   245\t### Envelopes\n   246\t\n   247\tA **creation specification** is a map with one required key:\n   248\t\n   249\t- `:dao.stream/type` — a qualified keyword naming the transport. This is the\n   250\t  dispatch key.\n   251\t\n   252\tEvery other key is transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace\n   253\t(for example a ring buffer's capacity), and validated by the selected handler\n   254\t— generic validation checks only the envelope, and a handler that rejects its\n   255\town keys returns `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`.\n   256\t\n   257\tThe **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object\n   258\treference. It is reachability data, not the logical-stream identity itself.\n   259\tIts properties are settled:\n   260\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '261,520p' docs/design/dao.stream.md | nl -ba -v 261\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '261,520p' docs/design/dao.stream.md | nl -ba -v 261\"","aggregated_output":"   261\t- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch\n   262\t  key.\n   263\t- It carries the logical-stream identity under the contract-owned\n   264\t  `:dao.stream/identity` key. Different descriptors may reach\n   265\t  the same logical stream through different endpoints; their reachability data\n   266\t  may differ, but their logical-stream identity is structurally equal. The same\n   267\t  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.\n   268\t- It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is\n   269\t  needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is\n   270\t  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns\n   271\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.\n   272\t- A transport whose attachments are distinguishable assigns each attachment\n   273\t  an opaque, structurally serializable value, unique among the attachments\n   274\t  that transport can tell apart. Every attachment handle the transport mints\n   275\t  has such an identity, whether the handle is returned by `attach!` or\n   276\t  minted from a host connection accepted without an `attach!` call. An\n   277\t  `attach!` success map carries the value under `:dao.stream/attachment`.\n   278\t  When no `attach!` call produces the handle, the transport specification\n   279\t  names the deposited event or other stream-native mechanism by which the\n   280\t  composition receives the identity. Wherever an answer or event for that\n   281\t  attachment is displaced onto another channel, the same value correlates it\n   282\t  there. The key and correlation rule are the contract's; minting,\n   283\t  representation, and the server-side delivery mechanism are the\n   284\t  transport's. Transports that cannot distinguish attachments omit it.\n   285\t- It carries no authorization. Nothing in this contract gates who may attach:\n   286\t  a descriptor is a name, and a host that can reach what it names attaches.\n   287\t  Whether attachment should be gated at all, and by what, is a question this\n   288\t  contract does not answer and does not reserve room for — an outcome or key\n   289\t  held open for an undesigned mechanism constrains that mechanism's design\n   290\t  from a document with no business specifying it. Should gating arrive, it\n   291\t  arrives as an addition, which the open-map rule and this section's own\n   292\t  transport-owned keys already permit.\n   293\t\n   294\tFor the time being, the operative guarantee is the round trip: `descriptor`\n   295\ton a live handle returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/descriptor`,\n   296\ta value that can be sent on a stream and reconstructed on the other end, and\n   297\t`:dao.stream/identity`, equal through every handle and descriptor for the\n   298\tlogical stream,\n   299\twhere `attach!` on the reconstructed value attaches to the stream it names —\n   300\tprovided the receiving host can reach what it names at all (see Creation and\n   301\tAttachment on reachability), and allowing that a transport which cannot know\n   302\tthat at call time reports it later as data.\n   303\t\n   304\tBoth envelopes are plain data through and through: every value must survive\n   305\tthe host serialization codec structurally unchanged, so equality is\n   306\tstructural, and no key holds a function, a host object, a live handle, or\n   307\tidentity smuggled in metadata. Like every DaoStream map, envelopes are open —\n   308\ta consumer ignores qualified keys it does not understand.\n   309\t\n   310\t`create!` outcomes:\n   311\t\n   312\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                       | Required keys        |\n   313\t|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n   314\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A new logical stream exists, its identity settled at once. Implies not that every resource backing it has finished being acquired. | `:dao.stream/handle` |\n   315\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`    | The creation specification is malformed.      | —                    |\n   316\t| `:dao.stream/not-found`       | No transport here matches `:dao.stream/type`. | —                    |\n   317\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The handler failed.                           | —                    |\n   318\t\n   319\t`attach!` outcomes:\n   320\t\n   321\t| Outcome                          | Meaning                                                                             | Required keys        |\n   322\t|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n   323\t| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | A handle exists through which this attachment is operated. Implies neither that a far end has confirmed the stream it names nor that establishment has completed. Where either answer is one only the far end can give, it arrives later as data, on the channel the transport names (see Surfaces). | `:dao.stream/handle`, and `:dao.stream/attachment` where the transport distinguishes attachments |\n   324\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |\n   325\t| `:dao.stream/not-found`          | The descriptor names no stream here, or no transport matches its type.              | —                    |\n   326\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error`    | The handler failed.                                                                 | —                    |\n   327\t\n   328\tWhere a transport acquires its medium through a process rather than at once —\n   329\tbinding a port, opening a handle through an API that only answers later — `ok`\n   330\tstill means the logical stream exists: its identity is minted, its sequence is\n   331\tempty, and the handle is operable. What it does not mean is that the stream can\n   332\tyet accept values. The handle reports that through its own operations, exactly\n   333\tas any other transient condition is reported, and the result of the acquisition\n   334\tarrives as data.\n   335\t\n   336\tThis is permitted only where logical existence is genuinely independent of the\n   337\tresource. A transport that cannot mint identity and lifecycle before its\n   338\tacquisition completes is not creating a stream and deferring its medium; it is\n   339\tdeferring the creation itself, and `ok` would be false. Such a transport does\n   340\tnot implement `create!` — what it needs is a request whose result arrives\n   341\tlater, which is an ordinary interpreter over streams, not an operation here.\n   342\t\n   343\tCreation defers differently from attachment, and the difference is why this is\n   344\ta caveat rather than a new outcome. `attach!` defers a **remote confirmation**:\n   345\tan answer only a far end can give, which is why it arrives as deposited data.\n   346\t`create!` has no far end. What it defers is **local acquisition**, whose\n   347\tfailure surfaces through the handle the caller already holds. A \"not yet\"\n   348\toutcome would have to return no handle — leaving the caller an identity it\n   349\tcannot operate, and nothing to retry against.\n   350\t\n   351\t### Host Dispatch\n   352\t\n   353\tWhen a creation specification or portable descriptor arrives dynamically —\n   354\tfor example off a stream — the host looks at it and dispatches on\n   355\t`:dao.stream/type`. The type resolves to a map that has the transport's\n   356\t`:dao.stream/create` and `:dao.stream/attach` implementations:\n   357\t\n   358\t```clojure\n   359\t{:dao.stream/ringbuffer {:dao.stream/create ringbuffer/create!\n   360\t                         :dao.stream/attach ringbuffer/attach!}\n   361\t :dao.stream/ws         {:dao.stream/attach ws/attach!}}\n   362\t```\n   363\t\n   364\tThe entries are per-transport and may be partial. A transport that creates\n   365\tno streams supplies no `:dao.stream/create` — the WebSocket transport owns\n   366\tno stream, it serves streams the host composed, so nothing there creates\n   367\tone. A creation specification naming it finds no create implementation and\n   368\treturns `:dao.stream/not-found`, the same answer as an absent transport and\n   369\tthe correct one: nothing here creates that.\n   370\t\n   371\tEntry values are **host-composed**: ordinarily closures produced by a\n   372\ttransport constructor that already received whatever composition state the\n   373\ttransport needs — a boundary's deposit destination and its admission\n   374\tdeclaration, a directory some composition keeps. The bare vars above are\n   375\tschematic. A bare namespace var could reach that state only through namespace\n   376\tglobals, which is the hidden state this contract retires.\n   377\t\n   378\tThis table is ordinary data owned by the host, and the dispatch is an\n   379\tordinary map lookup done by the host — DaoStream neither performs nor\n   380\tstandardizes it. A host that cannot dispatch on `:dao.stream/type` returns\n   381\t`:dao.stream/not-found` — never creation or discovery.\n   382\t\n   383\tCode that knows its transport statically calls the transport's functions\n   384\tdirectly; no dispatch is involved. There is no ambient namespace-global\n   385\tregistry and no namespace-load registration side effect.\n   386\t\n   387\t## Surfaces\n   388\t\n   389\tA handle implements the subset of the public surface its transport can\n   390\thonor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**\n   391\t(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and\n   392\tRetention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.\n   393\t\n   394\t`descriptor` belongs to no surface: every handle answers it with both\n   395\treachability and logical-stream identity, because both outlive any attachment.\n   396\tAn operation a handle does not\n   397\tdeclare is not part of that handle at all: there is nothing to call, so there\n   398\tis no operation for this contract to give an outcome. What a host does when\n   399\tasked for a method that is not there is the host's, and no transport may\n   400\tanswer such a call with a DaoStream outcome map — an outcome would imply the\n   401\toperation exists.\n   402\t\n   403\tA transport may also offer operations of its own beside the public surface —\n   404\tanswering a question this contract does not ask, such as how far a cursor\n   405\ttrails the newest value. They are transport-owned, they are not DaoStream\n   406\toperations, and nothing here defines or constrains them. **Code written\n   407\tagainst this contract may not reach for one**: it must work on any transport\n   408\tdeclaring the surfaces it uses, and a transport-owned operation is by\n   409\tdefinition absent from some of them.\n   410\t\n   411\tAn interpreter may still use one, on the same terms as any other thing a\n   412\tcomposition supplies. What it may not do is *find* it — reach through a handle\n   413\tfor an operation the contract never promised, or dispatch on which transport\n   414\tit was given. It receives the operation as an ordinary argument from the\n   415\tcomposition that wired it, exactly as it receives the streams it reads and the\n   416\tpolicies it applies, and it then depends on a function it was handed rather\n   417\tthan on a transport it detected. Such an interpreter is composed for a\n   418\tparticular medium and says so; it is not contract-generic code, and nothing\n   419\tcontract-generic may be built on it.\n   420\t\n   421\tThe reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as\n   422\tone positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and\n   423\tre-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that\n   424\tpromise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the\n   425\thost's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary\n   426\t— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a\n   427\ttransport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the\n   428\ttransport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per\n   429\tits declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly\n   430\tdefect. Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated: the contract's\n   431\tpublic surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy. A constructor\n   432\ttakes the deposit destination and its admission declaration as data. This is\n   433\tconfiguration provenance, not runtime introspection. (`dao.stream.ws.md`\n   434\tspecifies one such transport and the admission rule in detail.)\n   435\t\n   436\tA boundary that can no longer deposit must make itself **observably gone**. Its\n   437\tone permitted action is closing the host resource it itself holds; it invokes\n   438\tnothing above itself, retries nothing, and interprets nothing. This is the sole\n   439\taction a boundary adapter takes beyond appending, and it is not a judgement\n   440\tabout any event — it is the same action for every cause. One corner is\n   441\tirreducible: when the deposit that failed was the boundary's only channel, the\n   442\trecord of its own departure has nowhere to land, and the failure is observable\n   443\tonly to whoever holds the deposit result and through subsequent operations on\n   444\tthe handle.\n   445\t\n   446\tA transport also declares **which outcomes it produces**, and for each one it\n   447\texcludes, why. There are three honest reasons: the outcome's precondition is\n   448\timpossible by the transport's nature (an unbounded log never evicts, so never\n   449\treports `gap`); another policy answers the same condition, named (a ring buffer\n   450\tevicts rather than refusing, so pressure surfaces as a `gap` to a cursor that\n   451\tspans the eviction, and never as `full`); or the answer is displaced to another channel, named (a transport\n   452\tthat cannot know a remote answer at call time reports it as data elsewhere). An\n   453\texclusion with no stated reason is an unimplemented outcome, not a declaration.\n   454\t\n   455\t### Where the asynchrony goes\n   456\t\n   457\tThe IO Model makes this contract non-blocking; hosts are asynchronous\n   458\twhether or not it is. A network message arrives when the runtime says so, in\n   459\ta callback, on a thread or event loop DaoStream does not own. DaoStream does\n   460\tnot pretend otherwise; it confines the inversion to one place. The host's\n   461\tboundary adapter *is* callback code, and its entire job is to `append!` what\n   462\tarrived. Past that append the event holds a position in a sequence, and\n   463\tevery reader above observes it with its own cursor, in its own control flow,\n   464\twhen it chooses.\n   465\t\n   466\tThis is an un-inversion. The real cost of callback IO is not performance but\n   467\tthat the inversion spreads through everything it touches; depositing into a\n   468\tretained sequence pays that cost once, at the boundary, and returns ordinary\n   469\tcode to everyone above. The reader-surface rule above is the same thing\n   470\tstated structurally: a transport that retains nothing has no reader surface,\n   471\tand its inbound events are deposited onto a stream that does.\n   472\t\n   473\t## Cursors\n   474\t\n   475\tA cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n   476\t\n   477\t- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**\n   478\t  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\n   479\t  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained\n   480\t  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended\n   481\t  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport\n   482\t  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional\n   483\t  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is\n   484\t  malformed or not honored by this transport yields\n   485\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\n   486\t  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream\n   487\t  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains\n   488\t  readable. Minting may have to consult the medium — locating the earliest\n   489\t  retained position on a durable log is itself a read — so where a\n   490\t  transport's reads can fail at runtime, minting can too, and `cursor`\n   491\t  yields `:dao.stream/transport-error` for it; a transport whose medium\n   492\t  cannot fail this way excludes the outcome like any other (see Surfaces).\n   493\t  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n   494\t  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n   495\t  `:dao.stream/closed`, and `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n   496\t- A composition that intends to observe events caused by an operation mints\n   497\t  its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n   498\t  after `attach!`, endpoint bind, handoff installation, or any other operation\n   499\t  that can deposit would intentionally skip events deposited in between.\n   500\t- **A freshly minted `:dao.stream/oldest` is a position, not a claim about\n   501\t  completeness.** The anchor names the earliest *retained* position, which on\n   502\t  an evicting transport advances as values are lost. A `gap` is reported to a\n   503\t  cursor that spans an eviction; a cursor minted after one is a valid cursor\n   504\t  onto the surviving suffix and reports nothing, because nothing is wrong with\n   505\t  the cursor. Retained history is not complete history, and no anchor can make\n   506\t  it so. A consumer that requires complete history gets it from the transport's\n   507\t  declared retention (see *Retention and Gaps*), never from an anchor.\n   508\t- A composition on a transport that **can evict**, intending to detect whether\n   509\t  history was lost, mints an **origin cursor** — `:dao.stream/oldest` before\n   510\t  the first append — and keeps it, exactly as a composition that intends to\n   511\t  observe events caused by an operation mints `:dao.stream/newest` before\n   512\t  invoking it. A kept origin cursor converts a silent loss into a reported\n   513\t  `gap`. It cannot be minted afterwards and cannot be reconstructed: a consumer\n   514\t  that arrives later holds no evidence about what preceded it. On a transport\n   515\t  that declares complete retention this is unnecessary — a fresh `:oldest` is\n   516\t  the origin there, however late the consumer arrives.\n   517\t- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as\n   518\t  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never\n   519\t  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A\n   520\t  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '521,793p' docs/design/dao.stream.md | nl -ba -v 521\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '521,793p' docs/design/dao.stream.md | nl -ba -v 521\"","aggregated_output":"   521\t- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the\n   522\t  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter\n   523\t  works with that interpreter's own handle. Whether a cursor survives\n   524\t  serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD, like the\n   525\t  descriptor envelope.\n   526\t- A cursor carries the logical-stream identity, not a reachability descriptor.\n   527\t  `cursor-mismatch` compares that identity, so handles reached through distinct\n   528\t  descriptors for the same logical stream accept the same cursor.\n   529\t- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never\n   530\t  advances a reader; the stream holds no reader positions at all. Dropping a\n   531\t  cursor is how a reader detaches — there is nothing to unregister.\n   532\t- Because cursors are immutable values, a reader may retain an old cursor and\n   533\t  re-read retained history, or hand a cursor to another interpreter. What a\n   534\t  cursor can still observe is governed only by retention.\n   535\t\n   536\t## Reading\n   537\t\n   538\t`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\n   539\tIt is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data\n   540\tanswer.\n   541\t\n   542\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |\n   543\t|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n   544\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n   545\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   546\t| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n   547\t| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n   548\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n   549\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   550\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\n   551\t\n   552\tReading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same\n   553\tposition observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the\n   554\tother. There is no destructive read anywhere in the contract — no drain, no\n   555\ttake. An operation that removes an element as it reads would make one\n   556\treader's progress every other reader's data loss.\n   557\t\n   558\t## Writing\n   559\t\n   560\t`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:\n   561\t\n   562\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                                                                                              | Required keys |\n   563\t|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|\n   564\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | The value is accepted at the next position in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |\n   565\t| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\n   566\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |\n   567\t| `:dao.stream/closed`          | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                                         | —             |\n   568\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append; nothing was appended.                                                                                    | —             |\n   569\t\n   570\tAcceptance is handle-relative, as close outcomes are. **Which sequence** a\n   571\thandle's writer surface is on is part of the transport's declared nature: for a\n   572\tring buffer it is the logical stream itself, so `ok` places the value in it; for\n   573\ta transport that carries values toward a stream elsewhere it is the ordered\n   574\toutbound path, and what becomes of the value at the far end is reported there,\n   575\tnot here. No remote transport can say more — under *no operation waits*, an\n   576\tanswer from the far end cannot arrive before `append!` returns.\n   577\t\n   578\tThe writer decides what to do about any non-`ok` outcome; DaoStream does not\n   579\tretry, buffer, or notify on its behalf.\n   580\t\n   581\t## Close\n   582\t\n   583\t`close!` takes a handle and returns a map whose outcome set is `{ok}` — no\n   584\tother outcome exists for it. It is idempotent: closing an already-closed scope\n   585\tis also `:dao.stream/ok`.\n   586\t\n   587\t`ok` is the local lifecycle transition, not a report on releasing host\n   588\tresources: what the handle is on is closed from that moment, irrevocably.\n   589\tA transport whose close must also flush or release something that can fail\n   590\tdeclares where that failure surfaces — an ordinary deposit on a channel it\n   591\tnames — because the outcome set has no room for it and the transition has\n   592\talready happened.\n   593\t\n   594\t`close!` closes **what the handle is on**. A handle explicitly designated by\n   595\tthe transport as the logical stream's owner closes that logical stream; a\n   596\tserver-side accepted-connection handle is an attachment and does not gain\n   597\townership merely by being on the serving host. An attached\n   598\thandle is on an attachment: closing it ends that attachment only — the\n   599\tstream persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` may rejoin\n   600\tlater. Which scope a transport's handles carry is part of its declared\n   601\tnature.\n   602\t\n   603\t`:dao.stream/closed` and `:dao.stream/end` are handle-relative: they speak of\n   604\twhat *this handle* is on. (Not every outcome is: `gap` is retention-relative\n   605\tand `cursor-mismatch` is logical-stream-relative.) After an attachment\n   606\tcloses, the handle's `append!` answers `closed`; `descriptor` still answers\n   607\t`ok` with reachability and identity — both outlive any attachment; a reader surface, where the\n   608\thandle has one, answers `end` from `next` once observation through this\n   609\thandle is exhausted, and `closed` from `cursor` — no new cursors are\n   610\tminted through a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream,\n   611\tnot to a handle, so a kept cursor resumes through a newly attached handle.\n   612\t\n   613\tClosing an attachment freezes what that handle can still observe: values\n   614\tappended to the logical stream afterwards are not observable through it, so\n   615\t`end` is reached at the position the stream had reached when the attachment\n   616\tclosed. And a logical stream that has itself been closed remains attachable —\n   617\t`attach!` answers `ok` and the new handle reads retained history to `end` —\n   618\tbecause close changes future availability, not identity or reachability.\n   619\t\n   620\tClosing the logical stream changes future availability only:\n   621\t\n   622\t- Subsequent `append!` returns `:dao.stream/closed`.\n   623\t- A reader that exhausts retained history receives `:dao.stream/end` instead\n   624\t  of `:dao.stream/blocked`.\n   625\t- Retained history remains readable. Close erases nothing; eviction is a\n   626\t  retention concern, not a lifecycle one.\n   627\t\n   628\tThere is no `closed?` predicate in the public surface. Operation results are\n   629\tauthoritative; a predicate answer is stale the moment it is returned.\n   630\t\n   631\t## Concurrency\n   632\t\n   633\tEach logical stream is one append-only sequence, and a handle's writer\n   634\tsurface is on exactly one sequence — for some transports the logical stream\n   635\titself, for others the ordered outbound path toward a stream elsewhere (see\n   636\tWriting). A successfully appended value occupies exactly one definite position\n   637\tin the sequence this handle's writer surface is on, and every operation's\n   638\toutcome reflects that sequence at one moment. DaoStream guarantees nothing\n   639\tfurther about concurrent operations.\n   640\t\n   641\tThis follows from Axiom 2. The sequence a handle's surfaces are on is the one\n   642\ttruth for that handle; each outcome is one perspective on it at one moment. A\n   643\treader that observes\n   644\t`:dao.stream/blocked` and then a value has not caught the stream in a\n   645\tcontradiction — it made two observations from its own cursor, and no global\n   646\tview exists to contradict. Whether an append landed before a close is\n   647\tanswered by the sequence the handle's writer surface is on — the value has a\n   648\tposition in it or it does not — and where that sequence is not observable from\n   649\tthis handle, the answer is read where it is.\n   650\tWhat an ordering between writers *means*, and any coordination among them —\n   651\ta merge interpreter, a one-log-per-writer discipline — is interpretation,\n   652\tand interpretation happens above the stream.\n   653\t\n   654\t## Retention and Gaps\n   655\t\n   656\tRetention and overflow behavior are transport-owned and form part of the\n   657\tstream or writer surface's declared nature; a locally created stream records\n   658\tthem through its creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts\n   659\tthe oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send\n   660\tbuffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —\n   661\t`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or\n   662\tpermanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the\n   663\toutcomes and standardizes no policy.\n   664\t\n   665\t**Eviction never waits on a reader.** Retention is the stream's own declared\n   666\tpolicy and applies whatever any reader is doing — a stream holds no reader\n   667\tpositions, so it cannot be held back by a slow one. A transport that deferred\n   668\teviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's\n   669\tabsence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\n   670\treader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\n   671\tdeliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a cursor that falls behind\n   672\treceives a `gap`.\n   673\t\n   674\tWhen bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\n   675\tgap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\n   676\tnothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\n   677\ttelemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently\n   678\tskipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader holding a cursor across an\n   679\teviction is told it missed values. This is a promise to a **cursor**, not to a\n   680\thandle: it is precisely what a kept cursor is worth, and it is why a consumer\n   681\tthat must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\n   682\t\n   683\t### Complete history\n   684\t\n   685\tSome consumers require *complete* history rather than *retained* history: an\n   686\tinterpreter that derives state by replaying a log from its beginning is wrong,\n   687\tnot merely stale, if it replays a suffix. The contract already carries what\n   688\tsuch a consumer needs, in two mechanisms that answer different questions.\n   689\t\n   690\t**A transport that excludes `gap` gives completeness.** This is the existing\n   691\tdeclaration in its existing form — the outcome's precondition is impossible by\n   692\tthe transport's nature, an unbounded log never evicts, so never reports `gap`\n   693\t(see *Surfaces*). Retention is declared through the transport's nature and, for\n   694\ta locally created stream, through its creation specification; it is\n   695\tconfiguration provenance. There is no way to ask a handle about it and none is\n   696\tadded. A consumer requiring complete history therefore requires *a transport of\n   697\tthat declared nature*, and the composition that wires it is what supplies one.\n   698\t\n   699\t**A kept origin cursor gives detection.** On a transport that can evict, an\n   700\torigin cursor turns a loss that would otherwise be invisible into a reported\n   701\t`gap`, and the consumer aborts or recovers on its own policy. It does not make\n   702\tthe history complete; it makes incompleteness observable.\n   703\t\n   704\tBoth stand, and they are not substitutes. Where completeness is a correctness\n   705\trequirement, only the declared transport delivers it, and a kept origin cursor\n   706\tis the honest failure mode that remains when completeness was not wired. Where\n   707\ta consumer can proceed on a suffix but must know that it is on one, the origin\n   708\tcursor is right and sufficient.\n   709\t\n   710\t**Wiring a log onto an evicting transport is a host assembly defect**, of the\n   711\tsame kind as wiring a deposit destination that can refuse. A durable log is not\n   712\ta window: if a consumer treats a stream as the record of everything that\n   713\thappened, the transport under it must declare that it retains everything.\n   714\tCapacity is the wrong knob for this — a larger window makes loss less likely\n   715\tand never makes it reported — so sizing never substitutes for the declaration.\n   716\t\n   717\tThe obligation a complete-history transport takes on is one sentence: **a\n   718\tcomplete-history reader begins at its logical sequence's origin and never\n   719\tevicts an element of that sequence.** Every element present at creation, and\n   720\tevery value an append places on that same sequence with `ok`, remains part of\n   721\tit for as long as the logical stream exists. If the medium cannot serve\n   722\tretained history it reports `transport-error`; it never silently substitutes a\n   723\tsuffix. Everything else follows. Its `:oldest` anchor stays at the origin, so\n   724\t`gap` is impossible and is excluded for that reason.\n   725\t\n   726\tWhat it owes on the writing side depends on what it is. A complete-history\n   727\ttransport need not be writable at all — a finite immutable history is a valid\n   728\tone, and owes no writer outcome. A writable one that declares a finite capacity\n   729\treturns `full` on reaching it, without appending and without evicting: refusing\n   730\tand evicting are the two answers to the same condition, and a transport that has\n   731\tgiven up one owes the other. A writable one with no declared capacity — a\n   732\tlogically unbounded log, however it grows or spills beneath — may exclude\n   733\t`full`. Logically unbounded is not physically infinite, and the line runs\n   734\tbetween failures an operation can observe and return from and those it cannot:\n   735\tunexpected exhaustion the transport can observe and return from is\n   736\t`transport-error`, while fatal host or runtime exhaustion, from which the\n   737\toperation produces no result at all, lies outside the outcome algebra\n   738\taltogether. Neither is ever converted into the eviction of acknowledged\n   739\thistory. A transport whose every operation either completes its state\n   740\ttransition or does not return therefore has no firing condition for\n   741\t`transport-error` and excludes it like any other outcome.\n   742\t\n   743\t## Composition\n   744\t\n   745\tDaoStream defines only streams, cursors, and the operations above. Anything\n   746\tthat transforms one stream into another is an interpreter — it reads via its\n   747\town cursor and appends to an ordinary output stream — and that lives entirely\n   748\toutside this contract, needing no support from it. As in a Unix pipeline, the\n   749\tpipe is dumb; the transforming happens in the processes on either end.\n   750\t\n   751\tA forwarder between streams is a single step (not a callback loop) over the\n   752\ttransport-agnostic stream algebra. Because the stream never blocks, cadence is\n   753\towned by the composition-supplied driver that repeatedly calls the step,\n   754\tyielding execution as needed by the host runtime.\n   755\t\n   756\tFlow control (a reader pausing a sender) is an interpreter's concern. A pause\n   757\thas to be a lease and not a switch to avoid permanently stuck states. Those\n   758\tsemantics live in `dao.lease.md`, whose facts are plain data on ordinary\n   759\tstreams. DaoStream provides the honest baseline: a cursor that falls\n   760\tbehind is evicted past and told so with a `gap`, and the reader holding it\n   761\tdecides what that means.\n   762\t\n   763\t## Explicitly Absent\n   764\t\n   765\tAbsent from the public surface, by derivation from the invariants:\n   766\t\n   767\t- **an ambient registry** — a namespace-global dispatch table, or registration\n   768\t  as a load-time side effect. Host dispatch is ordinary data the host owns.\n   769\t- **a `closed?` predicate** — a predicate answer is stale the moment it\n   770\t  returns; operation results are authoritative.\n   771\t- **destructive reads** — a read that removes what it observes makes one\n   772\t  reader's progress every other reader's data loss. It is coherent only while\n   773\t  exactly one observer exists; a forwarder, an indexer, or a DHT replica makes\n   774\t  it theft. Where the capability went, so no later plan reinvents it: **taking\n   775\t  is a write.** Removing a tuple from an append-only log is not a mutation but\n   776\t  an assertion that it no longer holds, which `dao.space` already expresses as\n   777\t  a retraction datom — Linda's `in` is observe-then-retract, not a stream\n   778\t  operation. And the hard part of a take was never the removal but the\n   779\t  *exclusion* of competing takers, which is a lease over datoms\n   780\t  (`dao.lease.md`), not something a stream can promise. v1's `drain-one!`\n   781\t  appeared to solve exclusion only because it ran in one process against a\n   782\t  local ring buffer.\n   783\t- **`seek`** — every valid cursor comes from the stream; anchored minting and\n   784\t  kept cursors cover repositioning.\n   785\t- **a blocking take** — no operation waits.\n   786\t- **waiter registration** — there is no readiness extension.\n   787\t- **throwing conveniences** — operational outcomes are data.\n   788\t- **a retention or completeness predicate** — retention is declared, through\n   789\t  the transport's nature and the creation specification. Asking a handle would\n   790\t  be the same defect as asking whether it is closed: the answer is\n   791\t  configuration the composition already holds, and reading it back at runtime\n   792\t  invites code that branches on what it should have been wired with.\n   793\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"The baseline makes the key review criterion sharper: portable interfaces must be stepped and non-blocking, while any waiting call or callback is host policy only. I’m now checking whether the new linker’s lookup path actually satisfies that contract rather than merely describing it as a stream effect."}}
