{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,230p' docs/design/dao.jing.md | nl -ba\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoJing: The Content-Addressed Storage Observer\n     2\t\n     3\tNamed for 井 (jǐng), the well: shared storage everyone draws from, holding whatever is poured in and giving it back unchanged. Not 经, the canon, and not a reference to any scripture.\n     4\t\n     5\tStatus: implemented. The observer (`observer-state` / `observe-step!`) and the\n     6\tplain-data content-store handles described here are the current\n     7\t`src/cljc/dao/jing*.cljc` code. What remains open — the final canonical\n     8\tencoding, byte-array addressing, metadata-carrying backends and transports,\n     9\tdurable observer checkpoints, explicit materialization acknowledgement, the\n    10\tcontent write path as an effect stream, garbage collection, and async\n    11\thydration — is listed under *Open items and current limitations*.\n    12\t\n    13\t**Related documents:**\n    14\t\n    15\t- `docs/design/dao.space.md` — the tuple space built by interpreters above\n    16\t  storage\n    17\t- `docs/design/dao.space.index.md` — the owner-side covered-index publisher\n    18\t- `docs/design/dao.space.query.md` — the reader-side index consumer\n    19\t- `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md` — the DHT distribution backend\n    20\t- `docs/design/dao.stream.md` — the append-only stream primitive\n    21\t- `docs/design/dao.data.btree.md` — the covered-index node format\n    22\t- `docs/design/datom.md` — the datom and tuple specification\n    23\t- `docs/design/adr/0001-dao-space-as-storage-boundary.md` — the storage-boundary\n    24\t  decision\n    25\t- `docs/datomic.md` — the Datomic storage architecture that informs the\n    26\t  separation of storage and interpretation\n    27\t\n    28\t## Definition\n    29\t\n    30\tA `dao.jing` observes an explicit pool of intake `dao.stream` values and\n    31\tmaterializes their elements into content-addressed key-value storage.\n    32\t\n    33\tFor every opaque payload `x` arriving through any stream in the pool, DaoJing\n    34\tperforms the same storage operation:\n    35\t\n    36\t```text\n    37\tbytes = canonical-encode(x)\n    38\tkey   = content-hash(bytes)\n    39\tKV[key] = x                 ; insert if absent\n    40\t```\n    41\t\n    42\tDaoJing knows how to:\n    43\t\n    44\t- observe the streams in its configured pool;\n    45\t- retain the operational cursor needed to continue reading each stream;\n    46\t- canonically encode an element;\n    47\t- derive its content address;\n    48\t- insert it idempotently into the materialization target; and\n    49\t- retrieve content by its address.\n    50\t\n    51\tDaoJing does not know whether an element is a datom, B-tree node, covered\n    52\tindex, manifest, program, image, or any other kind of value. Meaning belongs\n    53\tto the interpreter that produced or consumes the content.\n    54\t\n    55\tThis makes DaoJing suitable storage for **any point in Datom.world's moduli\n    56\tspace of database interpreters**. DaoSpace can store covered indexes and\n    57\tindexed snapshots in it; DaoField can store metric indexes; another interpreter\n    58\tcan store graph, document, columnar, model, or other materializations. All are\n    59\topaque values at this boundary.\n    60\t\n    61\tDaoJing is itself an interpretation of DaoStream, but only at the\n    62\trepresentation level: it interprets each emitted value as content to encode,\n    63\thash, and materialize in a key/value store. That operation assigns content\n    64\tidentity and retrieval semantics while remaining devoid of domain semantics.\n    65\tDaoJing is therefore shared storage beneath the database moduli space, not a\n    66\tsemantic database point within it.\n    67\t\n    68\tDaoJing maintains no membership registry, no mutable roots, no CAS records,\n    69\tand no delete operation. Its only object of discourse is the strict content\n    70\taddress `:segment/<algorithm>-<digest>`, and its only semantics are\n    71\tinsert-if-absent materialization plus content reads.\n    72\t\n    73\t## The intake pool\n    74\t\n    75\tThe pool contains the streams through which content is submitted for\n    76\tmaterialization. It is supplied explicitly when the observer state is\n    77\tconstructed. DaoJing performs no stream registration or discovery and\n    78\tmaintains no shared membership record.\n    79\t\n    80\tThe pool is an intake topology, not a semantic namespace. DaoJing does not use\n    81\tthe identity of the source stream when deriving an element's key, and it does\n    82\tnot attach source identity to the stored value:\n    83\t\n    84\t```text\n    85\tstream A emits x ─┐\n    86\t                  ├── hash(canonical-encode(x)) ──► the same KV entry\n    87\tstream B emits x ─┘\n    88\t```\n    89\t\n    90\tIdentical content therefore converges on the same address regardless of which\n    91\tpool member carried it. Pool order, source identity, and arrival order do not\n    92\tchange content identity.\n    93\t\n    94\tDaoJing must distinguish pool members operationally long enough to maintain a\n    95\tcursor for each one. That association is observer state only. It is not\n    96\twritten into the content address or materialized payload and has no semantic\n    97\tmeaning. In the implemented observer each member entry is plain data —\n    98\t`{:stream <v2 reader handle>, :cursor <opaque>, :status s}` — an operational\n    99\trecord, nothing more. `observer-state` rejects a member that lacks a cursor\n   100\tor a v2 reader before any operation. Besides successful observation, the only\n   101\tway a member's cursor changes is through `adopt-cursor`.\n   102\t\n   103\tIf a storage backend partitions content into physical buckets or shards, that\n   104\tplacement is a backend concern, normally derived from the content hash. It is\n   105\tnot derived from the source stream.\n   106\t\n   107\t## Publication from an agent\n   108\t\n   109\tAn agent writes ordinary working data to its local `dao.stream`. That local\n   110\tstream is not automatically a member of DaoJing's intake pool.\n   111\t\n   112\tWhen the agent calls `dao.space.index/publish-index!`, the index publisher:\n   113\t\n   114\t1. reads the agent's local stream;\n   115\t2. constructs the covered indexes;\n   116\t3. selects an intake stream from the DaoJing pool;\n   117\t4. appends the immutable index payloads, including the top-level manifest, to\n   118\t   that intake stream; and\n   119\t5. returns the manifest and its content address required by the publishing\n   120\t   layer.\n   121\t\n   122\t```text\n   123\tagent-local dao.stream\n   124\t        │\n   125\t        │ dao.space.index/publish-index!\n   126\t        ▼\n   127\tselected DaoJing intake stream\n   128\t        │\n   129\t        │ opaque payloads\n   130\t        ▼\n   131\tDaoJing observer ── canonical encode + hash ──► content-addressed KV\n   132\t```\n   133\t\n   134\tSelecting an intake stream is an operational routing decision. It does not\n   135\tbecome part of an index payload's identity. The same index payload published\n   136\tthrough a different intake stream receives the same address.\n   137\t\n   138\tPublication acknowledges that every payload was appended to the selected\n   139\tintake stream. It does not acknowledge that an asynchronous observer has yet\n   140\tmaterialized those payloads; see *Open items and current limitations*.\n   141\t\n   142\tPublication changes representation and access cost, not the meaning of the\n   143\tcovered data. Consumers whose explicit read coordinates name the published\n   144\tmanifest address can consume the persisted covered indexes.\n   145\t\n   146\t## Materialization rule\n   147\t\n   148\tThe materialization is an idempotent union of content-addressed values. If\n   149\t`H(x)` is the address of the canonical encoding of `x`, observing `x` means:\n   150\t\n   151\t```clojure\n   152\t(assoc-if-absent target (H x) x)\n   153\t```\n   154\t\n   155\tRe-observing the same value is a no-op. Observing equal values through\n   156\tdifferent pool streams is also a no-op after the first insertion. Consequently:\n   157\t\n   158\t- replay is safe;\n   159\t- pool streams may be consumed in any interleaving;\n   160\t- duplicate publication is harmless;\n   161\t- immutable content writes do not require cross-stream coordination; and\n   162\t- a materialization can be reconstructed by replaying the available pool\n   163\t  streams.\n   164\t\n   165\tThe implemented write is `dao.jing/materialize!`: it derives the address from\n   166\tthe payload alone (`segment-key`) and asks the backend's `:put-content-fn`\n   167\tfor an explicit verdict. A backend validates before it writes: the address must\n   168\tbe a segment address and must hash to the payload, else it throws and stores\n   169\tnothing. `:inserted` means the value is durably stored now;\n   170\t`:present` means an equal value is already stored there, in which case the\n   171\tstored value is read back and verified. A collision in which an existing\n   172\taddress contains different canonical bytes is an integrity failure, not an\n   173\toverwrite, and throws loudly. `nil` is a legal payload distinct from absence:\n   174\t`get` takes a caller-supplied not-found and returns stored `nil` as `nil`.\n   175\t\n   176\t### Canonical encoding\n   177\t\n   178\tContent addressing is meaningful only when equal supported values produce the\n   179\tsame bytes on every participating platform. Distinct values must address\n   180\tdistinctly, both across types (`42` and `\"42\"` do not collide) and within one\n   181\ttype (`{:a 1}` and `{:a 2}` do not). Minted addresses are readable EDN: the\n   182\tname never starts with a digit, and a key survives `pr-str` → `read-string`.\n   183\tThe canonical encoder is therefore part of the storage contract even though the\n   184\tmeaning of the encoded value is not.\n   185\t\n   186\tCanonicalization may understand representation-level structure such as maps,\n   187\tsets, numbers, strings, and byte arrays. It must not understand domain concepts\n   188\tsuch as datoms, index orders, manifests, or any notion of a root.\n   189\t\n   190\tThe target encoding is a canonical flat byte representation suitable for\n   191\tcross-platform hashing and in-place reading. The current implementation uses\n   192\tan order-normalized, metadata-aware hand printer (`dao.jing/order-normalize`\n   193\tand `canonical-print`) as a transitional encoder — deterministic and\n   194\torder-insensitive, but not yet the pinned canonical byte encoding. This is the\n   195\tfirst open item under *Open items and current limitations*.\n   196\t\n   197\tThe transitional encoder's current contract, precisely: collection metadata\n   198\t(on maps, sets, vectors, lists, and seqs) is address-significant, except\n   199\treader-position keys (`:line`, `:column`, `:end-line`, `:end-column`), which\n   200\tare stripped before hashing, and empty metadata, which is dropped rather than\n   201\ttreated as distinct from no metadata. Scalar metadata (on symbols — no\n   202\tportable host lets a keyword carry metadata) is not address-significant\n   203\t— it is silently ignored, a known\n   204\tresidual pending the pinned canonical byte encoding. Lists and seqs of equal\n   205\tcontent share one address (`=` calls them equal and both print the same way);\n   206\tvectors, sets, and maps are each their own type and never collide with\n   207\tanother, for any non-pathological scalar (see the pathological-symbol\n   208\tresidual under *Open items and current limitations*). Records are not a\n   209\tsupported payload: `content-hash` throws rather\n   210\tthan silently addressing a record as its equal plain map, since the\n   211\tparticipating hosts cannot agree on how to print one. `materialize!`'s\n   212\t`:present` read-back is verified by `segment-matches?`, ensuring that\n   213\ta metadata-only mismatch is caught as a real collision.\n   214\t\n   215\t## Multihash Content Addressing & Algorithm Registry\n   216\t\n   217\tDaoJing implements a closed, immutable algorithm registry providing\n   218\tmulti-algorithm content-addressing across JVM, Node/CLJS, and ClojureDart.\n   219\t\n   220\tSix core concepts are strictly distinguished:\n   221\t\n   222\t1. **Algorithm:** A registered cryptographic hash function identified by a\n   223\t   keyword (`:blake3` or `:sha256`). The registry is closed and immutable:\n   224\t   - `:blake3`: default minting algorithm; produces 32-byte (256-bit) digests.\n   225\t   - `:sha256`: permanent first-class selectable algorithm; produces 32-byte\n   226\t     digests.\n   227\t2. **Digest:** The lowercase hexadecimal representation of the raw digest\n   228\t   bytes computed over `canonical-bytes(payload)`.\n   229\t3. **Address:** The namespace-qualified keyword\n   230\t   `:segment/<algorithm-id>-<digest-hex>` (81-character keyword\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
