{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '231,460p' docs/design/dao.jing.md | nl -ba -v 231\"","aggregated_output":"   231\t   beginning with `:segment/blake3-` or `:segment/sha256-`).\n   232\t   Validated by `dao.jing/segment-address?` and parsed by\n   233\t   `dao.jing/parse-segment-address`.\n   234\t4. **Minting:** Deriving a new address for content (`dao.jing/content-hash`,\n   235\t   `dao.jing/segment-key`, `dao.jing/materialize!`). Implicit minting defaults\n   236\t   to `:blake3`. Explicit minting supports `{:algorithm :sha256}` across public\n   237\t   APIs.\n   238\t5. **Verification:** Validating that an opaque payload matches an existing\n   239\t   address using `dao.jing/segment-matches?`.\n   240\t   - **Verification must be address-directed; minting primitives are not\n   241\t     validators.**\n   242\t   - The address carries its own algorithm identifier. `segment-matches?` parses\n   243\t     the address, computes canonical bytes, digests them using the algorithm\n   244\t     indicated by the address, and compares. It never consults\n   245\t     `default-hash-algorithm` and never throws on encoder refusal or\n   246\t     malformed addresses.\n   247\t6. **Copying:** Transferring or caching addressed content across layers (such as\n   248\t   DHT caching, B-tree hydration, and `store-tree-async` flushing).\n   249\t   - **Copying addressed content preserves the address-carried algorithm.** An\n   250\t     address minted under `:sha256` remains under `:sha256` when cached or\n   251\t     hydrated.\n   252\t\n   253\t### Host Dependencies and Licenses\n   254\t\n   255\tPinned BLAKE3 provider dependencies across all three hosts:\n   256\t- **JVM:** `io.github.rctcwyvrn/blake3 1.3` (MIT License)\n   257\t- **Node/CLJS:** `@noble/hashes 2.4.0` (MIT License)\n   258\t- **ClojureDart:** `blake3_dart 1.0.0` (MIT License)\n   259\t\n   260\tSHA-256 uses native host implementations on JVM (`java.security.MessageDigest`),\n   261\tNode/CLJS (`goog.crypt.Sha256`), and pure ClojureDart.\n   262\t\n   263\t### Maintenance Obligations: Call-Site Classification & AST Lint Guard\n   264\t\n   265\tFuture maintenance across the repository carries two explicit obligations\n   266\tgoverned by `docs/design/dao.jing.call-site-classification.md`:\n   267\t\n   268\t1. **Call-Site Classification:** Any new call site dealing with content\n   269\t   addresses must be classified into one of the four established classes:\n   270\t   - **Class 1 (Minting):** Fresh content addressing via `content-hash` or\n   271\t     `segment-key` (defaulting to `:blake3` or explicit `{:algorithm ...}`).\n   272\t   - **Class 2 (Wire / Handshake):** Protocol framing or manifest candidate\n   273\t     entries where schema addresses are stored.\n   274\t   - **Class 3 (Validation):** Must ALWAYS use `dao.jing/segment-matches?`\n   275\t     directed by the existing address. Never re-mint with default minting\n   276\t     primitives and compare with `=`.\n   277\t   - **Class 4 (Copy / Hydration):** Must ALWAYS preserve the address-carried\n   278\t     algorithm when copying, caching, or flushing content.\n   279\t2. **Architectural AST Lint Guard:** The active test\n   280\t   `architectural-lint-sweeps-production-sources` in\n   281\t   `test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc` sweeps all production\n   282\t   `.cljc` sources to forbid equality validation (`=`) against\n   283\t   `dao.jing/content-hash` or `dao.jing/segment-key`. This test runs as part of\n   284\t   the standard suite (`bb test:clj`) to prevent regressions to implicit\n   285\t   mint-and-compare validation.\n   286\t\n   287\t## Storage ignorance\n   288\t\n   289\tDaoJing assigns identity to opaque content but does not interpret the data\n   290\tstructure stored at that identity. In particular, it does not:\n   291\t\n   292\t- traverse B-trees;\n   293\t- distinguish EAVT, AEVT, AVET, or VAET nodes;\n   294\t- merge indexes;\n   295\t- evaluate Datalog;\n   296\t- infer authorship or provenance from the intake stream;\n   297\t- discover which streams or published indexes a query should read;\n   298\t- construct a tuple space; or\n   299\t- maintain a root, CAS, or delete surface.\n   300\t\n   301\tThose responsibilities remain above the storage boundary. This allows a new\n   302\timmutable data structure to be introduced without changing DaoJing.\n   303\t\n   304\t## Physical intake versus semantic composition\n   305\t\n   306\tTwo forms of multi-stream work must remain separate:\n   307\t\n   308\t- **Physical intake:** DaoJing observes every stream in its configured pool and\n   309\t  content-addresses their opaque elements into the KV materialization.\n   310\t- **Semantic composition:** `dao.space.query` receives an explicit collection\n   311\t  of published sources and decides how their covered datoms are folded and\n   312\t  interpreted.\n   313\t\n   314\tDaoJing owns the first operation. It has no knowledge of the second. Observing\n   315\tmany intake streams does not mean that DaoJing semantically merges their\n   316\tcontents.\n   317\t\n   318\t## Reads\n   319\t\n   320\tThe storage read operation resolves a strict content address to the exact\n   321\topaque value stored at that address. `dao.jing/get` accepts only\n   322\tregistered `:segment/<algorithm>-<digest>` addresses; arbitrary keys and mutable\n   323\troots are outside DaoJing and throw before a backend is consulted.\n   324\t\n   325\tA reader may access the target locally or through a remote transport\n   326\t(`dao.jing.remote`, `dao.jing.dht`). Location changes how bytes are obtained,\n   327\tnot how their identity or meaning is determined.\n   328\t\n   329\tHigher layers expose the semantic compositions the stream participates in. A\n   330\tquery reads a published index descriptor containing a serializable\n   331\tcontent-store coordinate plus an immutable manifest address.\n   332\t`dao.jing.coordinate/open!` interprets coordinates such as\n   333\t`{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/file :path ...}` or,\n   334\ton the JVM, `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :url ...}` into local handles;\n   335\tunsupported coordinates fail closed. These coordinates are caller-supplied\n   336\tvalues, not state inferred from storage. DaoJing does not infer a source or\n   337\tcoordinate from the stream that carried a payload, and no content address is\n   338\tever treated as a mutable root.\n   339\t\n   340\t## Cursor tracking and recovery\n   341\t\n   342\tThe observation step is `dao.stream.observe`, shared with `forward` and\n   343\tthe VM. `dao.jing/observe-step!` polls the pool round-robin and processes at\n   344\tmost one payload per pool walk.\n   345\t\n   346\tThe `dao.stream.observe/step` core runs the materialization effect before\n   347\tadvancing the cursor. If materialization throws, the exception propagates\n   348\tbefore the cursor is advanced, so the caller's state is untouched and the\n   349\tsame payload is reprocessed from the same cursor once the backend succeeds.\n   350\t\n   351\tThe pool's signals and cursor disciplines are:\n   352\t\n   353\t| signal                                                    | when                                                       | extra keys                                      | member cursor            |\n   354\t| --------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- | ------------------------ |\n   355\t| `:dao.stream/ok`                                          | a payload was materialized                                 | `:address`                                      | successor, from the step |\n   356\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`                                     | empty pool, or every non-ended member blocked              | —                                               | unchanged                |\n   357\t| `:dao.stream/end`                                         | every member has ended                                     | —                                               | unchanged                |\n   358\t| `:dao.stream/gap`                                         | member `i`'s position was evicted                          | `:member i`, `:cursor` (the step's `:recovery`) | unchanged                |\n   359\t| `/cursor-mismatch`, `/invalid-cursor`, `/transport-error` | member `i`'s read failed, or answered outside the contract | `:member i`, `:result` (the step's `:read`)     | unchanged                |\n   360\t\n   361\tAn unrecognized transport answer is reported as a defect signal under\n   362\t`:dao.stream/transport-error`, carrying the classified read under `:result`\n   363\twith the raw answer retained inside it, and no action is taken\n   364\tbeyond declining to advance. Recovery is the caller's decision, made by\n   365\t`adopt-cursor` with the reported `:cursor`.\n   366\t\n   367\tThe `:next` scheduling index keeps a continuously ready member from\n   368\tstarving another. Scheduling does not affect the resulting content set,\n   369\tbecause materialization is content-addressed, commutative, and idempotent.\n   370\t\n   371\t## Resource lifecycle\n   372\t\n   373\tThe observer itself is a value: `observe-step!` owns no resources and has\n   374\tnothing to close — streams and storage handles retain responsibility for\n   375\treleasing their transport, file, database, or network resources.\n   376\t`dao.jing/close!` delegates to a handle's optional `:close-fn`; a handle\n   377\twithout one has nothing to release. A backend's close is idempotent; after\n   378\tclose, every entry point throws, and stored content is neither cleared nor\n   379\trewritten. A long-running runner that drives the pool loop, and its resource\n   380\tpolicy, are open items (see below).\n   381\t\n   382\t## Implemented surface\n   383\t\n   384\tThe current `src/cljc/dao/jing*.cljc` code implements the architecture above\n   385\tdirectly.\n   386\t\n   387\t**Content-store handles are plain data.** A handle is a map\n   388\t`{:put-content-fn f, :get-content-fn g, :close-fn c?}`; the backend effects\n   389\tare explicit functions, not a protocol or hidden state. `dao.jing/materialize!`\n   390\tand `dao.jing/get` dispatch through the handle, and `close!` through its\n   391\toptional `:close-fn`.\n   392\t\n   393\t**Content-store coordinates are transportable data.**\n   394\t`dao.jing.coordinate/open!` is the explicit interpretation boundary from a\n   395\tcoordinate to a live handle. Its closed dispatch recognizes\n   396\t`:dao.jing/file` on every supported platform and `:dao.jing/remote` on the\n   397\tJVM. There is no name-to-handle registry; adding a backend is an explicit code\n   398\tchange to the coordinate interpreter.\n   399\t\n   400\tImplemented backends:\n   401\t\n   402\t- `dao.jing.mem/create-content-mem` — an ephemeral, thread-safe,\n   403\t  content-addressed in-memory store. Put is an atomic insert-if-absent; an\n   404\t  address already holding the same payload reports `:present` and is never\n   405\t  overwritten.\n   406\t- `dao.jing.file/create-content-file` — a content-addressed store backed by a\n   407\t  private framed append-only file. Each log record is `[address payload]`, written\n   408\t  through a write lock, acknowledged only after the log is flushed, and\n   409\t  replayed on open to rebuild the in-memory content map. The framing layer\n   410\t  truncates an incomplete tail before replay; Jing then fails closed on any\n   411\t  complete record that cannot be decoded, validated, or matched to its content\n   412\t  address. The store guarantees idempotent close, throws after close, and\n   413\t  serializes concurrent puts with exactly one record written.\n   414\t- `dao.jing.remote` — over DaoStream v2, both halves JVM-only: the\n   415\t  constructor `connect-content!` returns a content handle over a live v2\n   416\t  attachment, and `serve-content!` serves `default-handlers` — or any\n   417\t  `{op fn}` map — at a WebSocket endpoint. The synchronous client is a\n   418\t  blocking driver as host policy over the portable non-waiting call step:\n   419\t  a JVM thread polls and sleeps between advances, and the cadence\n   420\t  (`:poll-interval-ms`), the connect deadline (`:connect-timeout-ms`) and\n   421\t  the request deadline (`:request-timeout-ms`) are options of the\n   422\t  constructor, not constants of the step. The constructor returns only\n   423\t  after the attachment reports `/established`; a failed open — a terminal\n   424\t  lifecycle, the deadline, or an interruption of the establishment loop's\n   425\t  sleep — guarantees only that the handle is closed and nothing escapes to\n   426\t  the caller — on\n   427\t  the JVM a close before the socket opens does not tear down the JDK's\n   428\t  establishment, so a peer that accepts TCP and never completes the\n   429\t  upgrade costs one held connection per attempt for the process lifetime\n   430\t  (the establishment-cancel gap `dao.stream.ws.md` records). The traffic\n   431\t  cursor is minted at `:dao.stream/newest` **before** `attach!`, because\n   432\t  the host may deposit the establishment event at any moment after\n   433\t  attaching and a cursor minted later would sit past it, hanging every\n   434\t  connect to its timeout. A URL is `ws://host[:port][/path]`, port\n   435\t  defaulting to 80 and an absent path naming `/jing`; `wss://`, a missing\n   436\t  host, a port that is not a positive integer, and a bracketed IPv6\n   437\t  authority throw before any socket. The wire vocabulary is\n   438\t  `:jing/put-content` answering `:inserted`/`:present` and\n   439\t  `:jing/get-content` answering exactly `{:found? boolean :value v}`, so\n   440\t  a stored `nil` is distinguishable from absence. One client carries one\n   441\t  locally awaited call — calls serialize under the client's lock — while\n   442\t  `close!` runs outside that lock and is safe during an in-flight call,\n   443\t  which then throws the terminal reason. A timed-out call retires its\n   444\t  bookkeeping (the id leaves the outstanding table and its late response\n   445\t  is dropped as unsolicited) but not the remote execution, which the\n   446\t  server may still be running. Bookkeeping is bounded by the one call in\n   447\t  flight: every exit of the blocking driver — return, timeout, terminal,\n   448\t  an immediate refusal at the writer, or an interruption of its poll sleep\n   449\t  — drains its completions and diagnostics before storing state, because an\n   450\t  exit that stored nothing would let the next call reuse the interrupted\n   451\t  call's request id and take its late response as an answer. An interrupted\n   452\t  call retires the same way a timed-out one does, and the thread's interrupt\n   453\t  flag is re-asserted before the throw. The timing options themselves are\n   454\t  validated at the driver's entry, before anything is sent: an argument\n   455\t  defect must throw before the wire, or it strands a request whose id the\n   456\t  next call would reuse. The portable value domain binds in\n   457\t  both directions: a payload outside it is refused before anything is\n   458\t  sent, and a handler result outside it is answered as a correlated\n   459\t  `:dao.jing.remote/non-portable-result` error rather than a timeout.\n   460\t  After a terminal lifecycle every call throws with that reason and the\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
