{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '461,619p' docs/design/dao.jing.md | nl -ba -v 461\"","aggregated_output":"   461\t  handle is never rebound; reattachment is the caller's, by opening a new\n   462\t  coordinate. Diagnostics have no outlet under a blocking driver and are\n   463\t  drained and dropped at every step. Server `stop!` detaches every\n   464\t  session, releases the listener, and is idempotent; handlers run in the\n   465\t  server's single driver thread, so a handler that never returns stalls\n   466\t  every session.\n   467\t- `dao.jing.dht/create-content-dht` and\n   468\t  `dao.jing.dht.node/create-content-dht-udp` — the distributed backend over an\n   469\t  `IDhtNet` transport; see `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md`.\n   470\t\n   471\t**The observer is implemented.** `observer-state` and `observe-step!` provide\n   472\tthe explicit intake-pool walk described in *Cursor tracking and recovery*,\n   473\twith no atoms, globals, registration, or discovery. The source stream never\n   474\tenters an address or a stored value.\n   475\t\n   476\t**Content addressing is implemented via the closed multihash registry.**\n   477\t`content-hash` digests canonical bytes under the selected algorithm (:blake3\n   478\tdefault, :sha256 selectable); `segment-key` mints\n   479\t`:segment/<algorithm>-<digest>` addresses; `segment-address?` is the strict\n   480\taddress test the backend layer enforces; `segment-matches?` is the total\n   481\taddress-directed verification predicate. As recorded in *Canonical encoding*,\n   482\tthe encoder is transitional until the pinned canonical CBOR encoding lands.\n   483\t\n   484\t## Open items and current limitations\n   485\t\n   486\t- **Canonical encoding.** The order-normalized hand-printer encoder must be\n   487\t  replaced by a pinned, cross-platform canonical byte encoding. Until then,\n   488\t  content addresses are portable only between implementations sharing the\n   489\t  exact print rule; when the encoding lands, `content-hash`, `segment-key`,\n   490\t  and every minted address change together. Three residuals of the\n   491\t  transitional encoder are deferred to that landing: scalar (symbol)\n   492\t  metadata is not address-significant; pathological symbols whose print text\n   493\t  mimics another value's print (e.g. `(symbol \"42\")` vs `42`) can collide;\n   494\t  and ambient print-var bindings (`*print-readably*` and similar) still\n   495\t  reach scalar bytes, since `canonical-print` delegates scalars to `pr-str`\n   496\t  — collection structure and order are rendered by `canonical-print`\n   497\t  itself, so only scalar leaves reach the host printer.\n   498\t- **ClojureDart's `list` mints metadata.** On ClojureDart, `(list ...)` and\n   499\t  `(apply list ...)` return a list carrying `cljd.core`'s own reader metadata\n   500\t  (`{:line … :column … :end-line … :end-column … :tag PersistentList}`).\n   501\t  `order-normalize`, `yin.vm`'s semantic-bytecode projection, and the\n   502\t  ClojureDart Transit decoders (`dao.stream.transit.cljd` behind\n   503\t  `dao.stream.ws`'s incoming frames, and the older `dao.stream.transit`)\n   504\t  clear metadata on the lists they mint, so neither normalization nor a list\n   505\t  decoded off the wire fabricates it. Any other Dart code that builds a\n   506\t  payload with `list` still hands `dao.jing` that metadata, and its `:tag`\n   507\t  survives the reader-position strip, so the payload addresses differently\n   508\t  from the equal list built on another host. Each such constructor must clear\n   509\t  it the same way (`(with-meta (apply list xs) nil)`) until the ClojureDart\n   510\t  defect is fixed upstream or the pinned canonical encoding decides the fate\n   511\t  of metadata.\n   512\t- **Byte arrays are hashed by identity, not content.** `dao.jing.md` lists\n   513\t  byte arrays as a supported representation-level type, but the transitional\n   514\t  encoder's scalar branch falls through to `pr-str`, which on the JVM prints\n   515\t  a byte array as an identity-bearing object literal (`#object[\"[B\" 0x...\n   516\t  \"...\"]`); other hosts print their own identity-bearing form. Two\n   517\t  content-equal byte arrays currently mint different addresses. No\n   518\t  current producer emits byte-array payloads, so this is latent; it must be\n   519\t  fixed (a proper byte-array print rule, or promotion into the pinned\n   520\t  canonical byte encoding) before any producer relies on byte-array content\n   521\t  addressing.\n   522\t- **Backends and transports must fail closed on metadata they cannot carry.**\n   523\t  Metadata is now address-significant in the transitional encoder, but no\n   524\t  durable backend or wire codec in the system carries metadata today: the\n   525\t  file backend (`dao/jing/file.cljc`) writes payloads with plain `pr-str`,\n   526\t  and the transit codec (`dao/stream/transit.cljc`) states metadata is not\n   527\t  on the wire and its portable-value check admits metadata-bearing\n   528\t  collections without complaint. A metadata-bearing payload therefore passes\n   529\t  `materialize!`'s put validation, is written with its metadata silently\n   530\t  dropped, and fails loudly — the whole store becomes unopenable — on replay,\n   531\t  because the replayed frame no longer hashes to its claimed address. This is\n   532\t  not reachable today (no producer emits collection metadata yet), and the\n   533\t  failure mode is loud rather than silently corrupting, so it is not a\n   534\t  blocker for the encoder fix itself. It becomes blocking the moment any\n   535\t  producer (the code-as-tuples pipeline's row/metadata-bearing content, once\n   536\t  that work starts emitting metadata-bearing literals) begins emitting\n   537\t  metadata-bearing payloads. Every backend and transport must, before that\n   538\t  point, either carry metadata through or explicitly refuse a payload whose\n   539\t  round trip through its own codec would not hash back to its address.\n   540\t\n   541\t  **Status 2026-09-18 (U10):** the storage half is closed. The file\n   542\t  backend's put now applies exactly this rule — a payload whose\n   543\t  `pr-str`/EDN round trip does not hash back to its address (any\n   544\t  metadata-bearing payload, since `pr-str` drops collection metadata the\n   545\t  address keeps) is refused before a byte is written; the memory backend\n   546\t  carries values verbatim, so metadata round-trips there. The transport\n   547\t  half is answered differently: Jing content never crosses a transport as\n   548\t  a bare payload value. `dao.jing.stream`'s boundary adapter wraps the\n   549\t  canonical bytes (`dao.jing/canonical-bytes`, the exact bytes the address\n   550\t  digests) as a CBOR byte string on the `dao.stream.cbor` profile and as a\n   551\t  named vector of octets on `dao.stream.transit-json`, so no transport's\n   552\t  value domain — metadata-blind or not — re-encodes the content.\n   553\t- **Durable observer checkpoints / long-running runner.** `observer-state`\n   554\t  and `observe-step!` are single-step and in-process. The checkpoint records,\n   555\t  per member, the stream coordinate plus the transport-minted cursor; the\n   556\t  coordinate is operational and never enters an address (serializability TBD in\n   557\t  the contract). A runner that drives the loop over time remains to be built.\n   558\t- **Explicit materialization acknowledgement.** A publisher observes only\n   559\t  that its payloads were appended to an intake stream. The mechanism by which\n   560\t  it observes that those payloads have been materialized must be expressed\n   561\t  explicitly, potentially as a response stream.\n   562\t- **The content write path as an effect stream.** A synchronous handle that\n   563\t  accepts a write and blocks until durable keeps the storage coupling. The\n   564\t  content write path as an effect stream (durability as data, `materialize!`\n   565\t  no longer returning an address synchronously) is deferred to a larger\n   566\t  write-path redesign.\n   567\t- **Garbage collection.** Content reachability and reclamation belong to a\n   568\t  higher-level retention policy; immutable content otherwise accumulates\n   569\t  forever.\n   570\t- **Async hydration.** Readers of remote or async B-tree content use the\n   571\t  hydration adapter (`dao.data.btree.storage/hydration-storage` and\n   572\t  `hydrate!`), but the async variants (`hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`)\n   573\t  are deferred until an async DaoJing backend exists. See\n   574\t  `docs/design/dao.data.btree.md` §5.4.\n   575\t\n   576\t  The remote half of that deferral is the **stepped client**: a\n   577\t  non-blocking remote handle with the `request-put` / `request-get` /\n   578\t  `request-materialize` / `step` / `abandon` shape — a client the caller\n   579\t  steps on a host that cannot wait. It is owed to the async hydration\n   580\t  work (`docs/design/dao.data.btree.md` §5.4), not to the v1 deletion: it\n   581\t  needs multi-id dispatch and per-materialization records rather than the\n   582\t  blocking driver's per-id step, and it forces a consumer change on\n   583\t  B-tree hydration.\n   584\t\n   585\t  **Status 2026-09-18 (stepped client):** the client itself is built —\n   586\t  `dao.jing.remote.step` wraps one `dao.stream.rpc` client state with\n   587\t  per-materialization records and id routes, `step` advancing in the\n   588\t  recorded fixed order (retry unsent, poll, terminal-abandon, issue\n   589\t  verify reads in put-id order, drain-and-route), the `:present` verify\n   590\t  hop hashing the read-back against the address exactly as\n   591\t  `materialize!` does, one published completion per materialization\n   592\t  carrying its put id, and no payload retained or published anywhere.\n   593\t  What remains owed to `dao.data.btree.md` §5.4 is the consumer side:\n   594\t  an async backend over this client, `hydrate-async`, and\n   595\t  `store-tree-async`.\n   596\t\n   597\t  **Status 2026-09-18 (consumer side):** built.\n   598\t  `dao.jing.remote.async/async-content` is the async backend: it owns one\n   599\t  stepped state as its single step owner, queues requests from any\n   600\t  caller, and drives `step` with a self-rescheduling pump (setTimeout /\n   601\t  Dart `Timer` / the JVM delayed executor, or an injected `:schedule`),\n   602\t  answering each request's callback exactly once with its published\n   603\t  completion. `dao.data.btree.storage/hydrate-async` and\n   604\t  `store-tree-async` consume it through a `hydration-storage` whose\n   605\t  source is that handle; see `dao.data.btree.md` §6 Phase 4 notes.\n   606\t\n   607\t## Lineage\n   608\t\n   609\tDaoJing combines two constraints:\n   610\t\n   611\t- **Datomic:** storage retains immutable content while an embeddable reader\n   612\t  interprets indexes and queries above it.\n   613\t- **Plan 9:** location and transport are properties of streams and handles,\n   614\t  not of the values carried through them.\n   615\t\n   616\tThe result is a deliberately restricted observer: a pool of streams carries\n   617\topaque values in, canonical content addresses identify them, and a KV\n   618\tmaterialization makes them retrievable. All semantic structure remains in the\n   619\tlayers that publish and consume those values.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
