{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,210p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md | nl -ba\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# yin.vm de Bruijn linker over dao.stream (Phase B6)\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design, revised; not implemented\n     4\t\n     5\t## 1. Objective and architectural position\n     6\t\n     7\tThis document specifies the closed-image code linker for `yin.vm` over\n     8\t`dao.stream` (backed by `dao.jing` content addressing). It is Phase B6 of\n     9\tthe de Bruijn compilation and VM architecture.\n    10\t\n    11\tGoverning rule: **B6 is used by both the stack VM and the register VM for\n    12\tlinking code over `dao.stream`.**\n    13\t\n    14\tFoundational principle: **The linker fetches code over `dao.stream`, but the\n    15\tcode itself is stored in `dao.jing`.**\n    16\t\n    17\tIn datom.world, code and continuations flow through streams: one universal\n    18\tAST, multiple execution backends (stack VM and register VM), peer witnesses\n    19\tto the same truth. Executable code is content-addressed data stored in\n    20\t`dao.jing`, while linking, resolution, and fetching occur strictly over\n    21\tappend-only `dao.stream` boundaries. Upstream stages -- Yang, the resolver\n    22\t(B2), the stack lowerer (B2), and the register compiler (R1, R2) -- produce\n    23\tcontent-addressed executable images:\n    24\t- Stack images, identified by H (`:yin.debruijn.code`)\n    25\t- Register images, identified by R (`:yin.debruijn.register`)\n    26\t\n    27\tThe linker is the component that enables either VM to resolve, fetch,\n    28\tverify, and link those executable images over `dao.stream`. A host holding\n    29\tonly an identity (H or R) and an index can obtain the image over a stream,\n    30\tprove it matches the storage address, prove it matches the requested\n    31\tcontract identity, validate its structural and liveness invariants, verify\n    32\tfree-variable closure for the receiving environment, and deliver it for\n    33\texecution.\n    34\t\n    35\tPhase B6 is implemented alone as the dedicated linker phase. It establishes\n    36\tthe format-parameterized fetch function (`yin.vm.debruijn-linker/fetch`) and\n    37\tprovides the concrete format records for both the stack and register VMs.\n    38\t\n    39\t## 2. Invariants: storage in dao.jing, fetching over dao.stream\n    40\t\n    41\tThe linker embodies the separation between storage and stream transport:\n    42\t\n    43\t1. **Storage in `dao.jing`**: The executable code itself (the canonical\n    44\t   instruction vector or register image map) is stored as an immutable,\n    45\t   content-addressed value in `dao.jing`, located at its `segment-key`.\n    46\t2. **Fetching over `dao.stream`**: Linking is an append-only stream process.\n    47\t   When code is resolved, requested, or transferred across execution\n    48\t   contexts or remote nodes, all exchanges travel strictly over\n    49\t   `dao.stream`. No direct function calls cross process or host boundaries.\n    50\t3. **Reused DaoJing substrate**: The linker builds directly on DaoJing:\n    51\t   - `dao.jing.dht` handles local cache retrieval and Kademlia peer lookup.\n    52\t   - `dao.jing.remote` handles DHT requests over `dao.stream` using\n    53\t     `content-client` and `default-handlers`.\n    54\t\n    55\tB6 reuses this substrate wholesale. The linker writes no transport, no peer\n    56\tlookup, no cache, and no responder of its own.\n    57\t\n    58\t### 2.1 What B6 adds\n    59\t\n    60\tB6 contributes:\n    61\t\n    62\t1. The format record protocol (section 5).\n    63\t2. The format-neutral six-step fetch function (section 4).\n    64\t3. Stack format record (`:yin.debruijn.code`) for stack VM code.\n    65\t4. Register format record (`:yin.debruijn.register`) for register VM code.\n    66\t5. The H index and R index mapping identities to Jing storage addresses.\n    67\t6. Same-root pairing (H and R) for cross-VM fallback over streams.\n    68\t7. The qualified refusal vocabulary (section 4.3).\n    69\t8. Tri-host tests exercising local and remote fetch across JVM, CLJS,\n    70\t   and ClojureDart.\n    71\t\n    72\t### 2.2 What B6 does not add\n    73\t\n    74\t- No new transport: `dao.stream` is the only medium; `dao.jing.remote`\n    75\t  provides the RPC client and server.\n    76\t- No peer routing or caching: handled entirely by DaoJing DHT.\n    77\t- No global registry: handles, indexes, and format records are explicit\n    78\t  arguments.\n    79\t- No callbacks: every outcome is a returned, qualified data value.\n    80\t- No execution coupling: the linker fetches and verifies; running the\n    81\t  image belongs to the VM kernels or lifting.\n    82\t\n    83\t## 3. The address and identity distinction\n    84\t\n    85\tNeither H nor R is the `dao.jing` storage address. This follows governing\n    86\tdecision D9: Jing addresses are storage locations, not VM identities.\n    87\t\n    88\tThe distinction is:\n    89\t\n    90\t    address = (jing/segment-key image-payload)\n    91\t    H       = (image-hash stack-instruction-vector)\n    92\t    R       = (register-hash register-image-map)\n    93\t\n    94\tThey differ in preimage and purpose:\n    95\t\n    96\t- `segment-key` is computed by the storage layer over the order-normalized\n    97\t  print of the value, using the address-carried algorithm (BLAKE3 by default\n    98\t  in DaoJing today, with SHA-256 registered). It changes whenever the\n    99\t  storage serialization format changes (e.g. when DaoJing CBOR lands).\n   100\t- H and R are pinned to explicit SHA-256 by VM contract freeze and embed the\n   101\t  lowering contract version. They must never fork when the storage encoder\n   102\t  changes.\n   103\t\n   104\tBecause the storage address and the VM identity have different preimages,\n   105\tthe linker must execute both checks:\n   106\t\n   107\t- Step 2 proves the retrieved value is authentic content at that storage\n   108\t  address.\n   109\t- Step 3 proves that authentic content is the exact program requested.\n   110\t\n   111\tNeither check subsumes the other.\n   112\t\n   113\t## 4. The fetch function\n   114\t\n   115\t### 4.1 Interface\n   116\t\n   117\tThe public entry point in `yin.vm.debruijn-linker` is:\n   118\t\n   119\t```clojure\n   120\t(defn fetch\n   121\t  \"Fetches and verifies an image by its identity using handle, index,\n   122\t   and format record. Used by stack and register VMs alike. Returns the\n   123\t   verified image or a qualified defect.\"\n   124\t  [handle index format identity]\n   125\t  ...)\n   126\t```\n   127\t\n   128\t`handle` is any DaoJing store handle satisfying `dao.jing/get`: local store,\n   129\t`create-content-dht` DHT handle, or `content-client` remote stream handle.\n   130\t\n   131\t`index` is a lookup function, plain map, or queryable store resolving\n   132\t`identity` (H or R) to a Jing storage address.\n   133\t\n   134\t`format` is a format record (section 5).\n   135\t\n   136\t`identity` is the format identity: H for stack images, R for register\n   137\timages.\n   138\t\n   139\t### 4.2 The six steps\n   140\t\n   141\tFetch executes six steps in strict order:\n   142\t\n   143\t```clojure\n   144\t;; (fetch handle index format identity)\n   145\t;; 1. address  <- (index identity)\n   146\t;;               absent entry            -> :absent\n   147\t;; 2. value    <- (jing/get handle address absent)\n   148\t;;               absent value            -> :absent\n   149\t;;               (not (jing/segment-matches? address value))\n   150\t;;                                       -> :address-mismatch\n   151\t;; 3.           (not= identity ((:hash-fn format) value))\n   152\t;;                                       -> :hash-mismatch\n   153\t;; 4.           ((:validate-fn format) value) returns a defect\n   154\t;;                                       -> that qualified defect\n   155\t;; 5.           ((:free-names-fn format) value) against receiver environment\n   156\t;;               unresolved name         -> :unresolved-free\n   157\t;;               name shadowed by store  -> :shadowed-free\n   158\t;; 6. return the verified image\n   159\t```\n   160\t\n   161\tStep order is load-bearing:\n   162\t\n   163\t1. Address verification (step 2) precedes identity verification (step 3)\n   164\t   because the storage address was requested from the store. Crucially,\n   165\t   `(jing/segment-matches? address value)` verifies the value against the\n   166\t   specific hash algorithm carried by `address`, rather than assuming\n   167\t   DaoJing's current minting default.\n   168\t2. Validation (step 4) precedes the closure check (step 5) so that\n   169\t   `:free-names-fn` only ever encounters structurally valid images. For\n   170\t   register images, step 4 includes the verified in-band live-set rules.\n   171\t3. The closure check (step 5) precedes return so that an unclosed image is\n   172\t   never handed to the caller. D11 requires refusal, not execution, for an\n   173\t   image the receiver cannot bind identically.\n   174\t\n   175\tThere is no separate descriptor check. The descriptor hash and contract\n   176\tversion are embedded inside H and R. A descriptor or version disagreement\n   177\tfails at step 3 as `:hash-mismatch`.\n   178\t\n   179\t### 4.3 Refusal vocabulary\n   180\t\n   181\tEvery refusal is qualified plain data:\n   182\t\n   183\t    +---------------------+--------+----------------------------------------+\n   184\t    | Outcome             | Step   | Meaning                                |\n   185\t    +---------------------+--------+----------------------------------------+\n   186\t    | :absent             | 1 or 2 | The index has no entry for identity,   |\n   187\t    |                     |        | or address has no payload on any store |\n   188\t    | :address-mismatch   | 2      | The received value does not match the  |\n   189\t    |                     |        | address algorithm and digest           |\n   190\t    | :hash-mismatch      | 3      | The value at that address does not     |\n   191\t    |                     |        | hash to identity (H or R)              |\n   192\t    | :descriptor-defect  | 4      | The image fails structural/liveness    |\n   193\t    |                     |        | validation                             |\n   194\t    | :unresolved-free    | 5      | A :load-free name cannot be resolved   |\n   195\t    |                     |        | in receiver's primitive/module env     |\n   196\t    | :shadowed-free      | 5      | A :load-free name is shadowed by the   |\n   197\t    |                     |        | receiver's free env or store           |\n   198\t    | :pairing-mismatch   | verify | Re-lowered H and R do not match the    |\n   199\t    |                     |        | claimed root pairing                   |\n   200\t    +---------------------+--------+----------------------------------------+\n   201\t\n   202\t## 5. Format records: stack and register\n   203\t\n   204\tB6 provides the format records for both execution backends:\n   205\t\n   206\t### 5.1 Stack format record (`:yin.debruijn.code`)\n   207\t\n   208\t```clojure\n   209\t{:format        :yin.debruijn.code\n   210\t :hash-fn       yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-hash\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
