{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '211,398p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md | nl -ba -v 211\"","aggregated_output":"   211\t :validate-fn   yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-defect\n   212\t :free-names-fn yin.vm.debruijn-linker/stack-free-names}\n   213\t```\n   214\t\n   215\tThe payload stored in Jing is the canonical instruction vector value itself,\n   216\tat its own `segment-key`.\n   217\t- `image-hash` is B1's hash over the descriptor hash and canonical vector.\n   218\t- `image-defect` validates opcodes, operands, bounds, body arity chains,\n   219\t  and host scalar support.\n   220\t- `stack-free-names` is defined by the linker module (scanning `:load-free`\n   221\t  operands in the canonical vector), preserving \"Existing edits: none\".\n   222\t- The lift to `:yin.code/*` is `yin.vm.debruijn-linearize/lift`.\n   223\t\n   224\t### 5.2 Register format record (`:yin.debruijn.register`)\n   225\t\n   226\t```clojure\n   227\t{:format        :yin.debruijn.register\n   228\t :hash-fn       yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-hash\n   229\t :validate-fn   yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-image-defect\n   230\t :free-names-fn yin.vm.debruijn-linker/register-free-names}\n   231\t```\n   232\t\n   233\tThe payload stored in Jing is the register image map `{:bodies ...\n   234\t:instructions ...}`, at its own `segment-key`.\n   235\t- `register-hash` is R1's hash over the descriptor hash and register vector.\n   236\t- `register-image-defect` validates opcodes, register bounds, body ranges,\n   237\t  and the section 4.5 ascending, bounded, exact live sets.\n   238\t- `register-free-names` is defined by the linker module (scanning\n   239\t  `:load-free` operands across all bodies in the instruction vector),\n   240\t  preserving \"Existing edits: none\".\n   241\t- The lift to `:yin.code/*` is `yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile/lift`.\n   242\t\n   243\tBecause `fetch` is parameterized by this record, adding future VM formats\n   244\trequires only defining a new format map.\n   245\t\n   246\t## 6. The H and R indexes\n   247\t\n   248\tThe index maps an identity to a Jing address:\n   249\t\n   250\t    H index:  H -> address   as a datom  [H :yin.debruijn.code/address address]\n   251\t    R index:  R -> address   as a datom\n   252\t              [R :yin.debruijn.register/address address]\n   253\t\n   254\tThe index is composition data, never DaoJing's: DaoJing has no roots and no\n   255\tnames by design (stack design D16). The linker takes the index as an\n   256\targument, either a plain Clojure map or a datom collection read through the\n   257\tcomposition's store.\n   258\t\n   259\tAn index entry is a claim, not a proof. Step 3 protects against stale or\n   260\tswapped entries (refusing `:hash-mismatch`). When the DaoJing storage encoder\n   261\tchanges (e.g. CBOR), the indexes are re-minted, while the underlying code\n   262\timages and their identities (H and R) remain unchanged.\n   263\t\n   264\tPhase B7 will later make the index a full name environment with ledger,\n   265\ttrust, and provenance. Phase B6 requires only a plain data value.\n   266\t\n   267\t## 7. Same-root pairing (H and R)\n   268\t\n   269\tA host without a register kernel may refuse R and instead resolve, by H, a\n   270\tstack image published for the same named root. Because H and R have disjoint\n   271\tpreimages, a bare H-to-R map has no verification law.\n   272\t\n   273\tThe pairing is recorded at mint time beside the named root as datoms:\n   274\t\n   275\t    [root :yin.debruijn.code/hash     H]\n   276\t    [root :yin.debruijn.register/hash R]\n   277\t\n   278\tTwo fallback paths exist:\n   279\t\n   280\t1. Trusted fallback: The receiver accepts the pairing on composition trust\n   281\t   and states so in its outcome.\n   282\t2. Verifying fallback: The receiver fetches the named datoms by the root,\n   283\t   re-lowers them locally via `adapt` and `lower-register`, and accepts\n   284\t   the fallback only when the recomputed H and R both match. Otherwise it\n   285\t   refuses with `:pairing-mismatch`.\n   286\t\n   287\t## 8. Dependency risk: transitional content hash\n   288\t\n   289\t`segment-key` hashes the order-normalized print of a value. Every Jing\n   290\taddress will change when DaoJing's canonical CBOR encoding lands. H and R\n   291\twill not change, but every H and R index must be re-minted then.\n   292\t\n   293\tConsequences:\n   294\t- Tests pin H and R values only. No test ever pins a Jing address as a\n   295\t  golden; addresses are always computed dynamically.\n   296\t- Until CBOR lands, Jing addresses are portable only between hosts whose\n   297\t  print of the value agrees. Cross-host test corpora are restricted to\n   298\t  print-stable scalars.\n   299\t- A print divergence fails closed: `:absent` or `:address-mismatch`, never\n   300\t  execution of wrong code.\n   301\t\n   302\t## 9. Stream topology: fetching over dao.stream\n   303\t\n   304\tReference-by-hash is a stream process, not hidden VM machinery:\n   305\t\n   306\t1. **Stream boundary**: The linker consumes image requests over a stream and\n   307\t   emits linked code or explicit refusals onto a stream. Local and remote\n   308\t   resolution follow the exact same stream protocol.\n   309\t2. **Storage decoupling**: The linker fetches code over `dao.stream`, but the\n   310\t   code itself is stored in `dao.jing`. DaoStream transports the requests and\n   311\t   the retrieved byte payloads; DaoJing content-addresses and stores the\n   312\t   underlying immutable data.\n   313\t3. **Parked requests**: A VM encountering an unlinked `:call-hash` (in B7)\n   314\t   parks its continuation and emits a request carrying the identity (H or R).\n   315\t   The composition resolves the identity through its index and initiates\n   316\t   `fetch`. All request tokens, continuations, and response outcomes are pure\n   317\t   data on the stream.\n   318\t\n   319\t## 10. File box: Phase B6\n   320\t\n   321\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc\n   322\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_test.cljc\n   323\t    Existing edits: none\n   324\t    Depends on: dao.jing (segment-matches?, segment-key, materialize!, get),\n   325\t                dao.jing.dht (create-content-dht, IDhtNet),\n   326\t                dao.jing.remote (content-client, default-handlers),\n   327\t                B1 (image-hash, image-defect, descriptor),\n   328\t                B2 (lift),\n   329\t                R1 (register-hash, register-image-defect, descriptor, lift)\n   330\t    Must not change: merged projection namespace, image-hash, register-hash,\n   331\t                     dao.stream, dao.jing, dao.jing.dht, dao.jing.remote,\n   332\t                     named VM semantics\n   333\t\n   334\t`yin.vm.debruijn-linker` exports:\n   335\t- `fetch`: the parameterized fetch function.\n   336\t- `stack-format`: the `:yin.debruijn.code` format record.\n   337\t- `register-format`: the `:yin.debruijn.register` format record.\n   338\t- `stack-free-names`: scans free names from stack instruction vectors.\n   339\t- `register-free-names`: scans free names from register image maps.\n   340\t- `verify-same-root-pairing`: verifies an H and R pair against source datoms.\n   341\t- Qualified refusal constructors and predicates.\n   342\t\n   343\t## 11. Completion criteria: Phase B6\n   344\t\n   345\tCross-host transfer over `dao.stream`:\n   346\t\n   347\t1. (H, R) JVM to Dart transfer over `dao.jing.remote`'s DaoStream transport,\n   348\t   where the receiver initially knows only the identity (H or R) and an\n   349\t   index. Includes the Dart-side client harness for that path.\n   350\t2. (H, R) Equal normalized results under the B0 normalizer when the fetched\n   351\t   image is lifted and executed through the existing semantic VM, compared\n   352\t   to local execution.\n   353\t3. (H, R) The cross-host corpus is restricted to print-stable scalars until\n   354\t   CBOR lands; no golden test pins a Jing address.\n   355\t\n   356\tRefusals:\n   357\t\n   358\t4. (H, R) `:absent` for an identity the index cannot resolve.\n   359\t5. (H, R) `:address-mismatch` when the received value does not match the\n   360\t   address algorithm and digest, tested via:\n   361\t   a. Local storage corruption.\n   362\t   b. Corrupt client RPC response over DaoStream.\n   363\t6. (shared) A DHT peer serving mismatched content is rejected before load;\n   364\t   `make-get` filters invalid peer payloads, returning `:absent` if no peer\n   365\t   has valid data.\n   366\t7. (H, R) `:hash-mismatch` when a well-stored value at the indexed address\n   367\t   does not hash to the requested identity, including descriptor or contract\n   368\t   version disagreement.\n   369\t8. (H, R) Structural validator defect in a fetched image refused before load.\n   370\t9. (R) Live-set defect in a fetched register image refused before load.\n   371\t10. (H, R) `:unresolved-free` for a `:load-free` name the receiver cannot\n   372\t    resolve through primitives or modules.\n   373\t11. (H, R) `:shadowed-free` for a `:load-free` name shadowed by the\n   374\t    receiver's free environment or store.\n   375\t12. (R) Trusted fallback from R to H that names its trust in the outcome.\n   376\t13. (R) Verifying fallback that re-lowers named datoms, refuses a swapped\n   377\t    pairing with `:pairing-mismatch`, and accepts an authentic pairing.\n   378\t\n   379\tStructure:\n   380\t\n   381\t14. One fetch function serves both stack and register formats without\n   382\t    format-specific branching in `fetch`.\n   383\t15. Tests use an explicit `fetch` call; no `:call-hash` instruction is\n   384\t    emitted or consumed (deferred to B7).\n   385\t16. No global loader, registry, callback, or cache is introduced; handle\n   386\t    and index are explicit arguments.\n   387\t17. Tri-host parity: JVM, Node/CLJS, and ClojureDart pass all tests cleanly,\n   388\t    with 0 kondo errors and clean cljstyle formatting.\n   389\t\n   390\t## 12. Non-goals\n   391\t\n   392\tThe B6 linker does not:\n   393\t\n   394\t- Add transport, peer lookup, caching, or responders (owned by DaoJing).\n   395\t- Emit or interpret `:call-hash` (deferred to B7).\n   396\t- Supply a name environment with ledger or provenance (deferred to B7).\n   397\t- Provide cross-model continuation transport.\n   398\t- Alter `image-hash`, `register-hash`, `dao.stream`, or existing VM kernels.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | nl -ba\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | nl -ba\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t## §7. The Universal Continuation Format (Proposed)\n     2\t\n     3\t> [!WARNING]  \n     4\t> **Status: Proposed / Deferred.** The Semantic VM's linear CESK state is theoretically sound as an architecture-agnostic continuation format, but true heterogeneous network migration requires addressing several critical defects identified in the 2026-09-14 architectural review. This revision (r3) incorporates r2's answers to that review's findings 1–19 and the subsequent owner ruling on §7.3's canonical form; §7.11 lists the acceptance blockers that keep this warning standing until an implementation phase closes each of them with tests.\n     5\t\n     6\tThe Semantic VM's linear CESK state (`{:segment id, :pc n, :env E, :stack S, :k K}`) is proposed as the canonical exchange format for network-transparent continuations. Because this lowered representation resolves execution-order ambiguity inherent in the Universal AST, it provides a simpler target for specialized execution engines (e.g., WebAssembly, LLVM, hardware FPGA) to participate in the `datom.world` ecosystem.\n     7\t\n     8\tTo safely \"lift on park\" and \"lower on resume\" across heterogeneous boundaries without violating host isolation or concurrency invariants, the following contracts must be fully specified before this feature is accepted:\n     9\t\n    10\t1. **Safepoint Reconstruction Metadata:** `:yin.code/source` mappings are insufficient for state reconstruction. The safepoint set must be exactly the machine's parking transitions — including effect-producing `:call`s — and reconstruction metadata must separate what is derivable from the canonical code alone (stack *effects*, lexical *requirements*) from what is per-activation state only the frame can carry (absolute depths, captured environments). Foreign engines publish per-segment safepoint maps derived from the canonical stream so arbitrary hardware states can be predictably lifted into the canonical CESK format.\n    11\t2. **Recursive Portable Encoding:** Section 1.1 permits host functions and local stream handles in the environment. A recursive encoding over a **disjoint tagged grammar** is required — one a program's own map literals cannot counterfeit — to encode these local references into portable descriptors, together with per-primitive **semantic profiles** (a name recovers a binding, not a meaning) and explicit, total failure modes for un-serializable resources. Cursor aliasing is observable and must survive the round trip.\n    12\t3. **Dependency Closure & Context:** A parked fragment is not a complete configuration. The format must carry its complete scheduler context — required primitives *with profiles*, loaded modules, referenced parked records, the fresh-name counter, and the reachable store slice — discovered by a conservative fixed point that includes explicit store operands, and must restore into an **isolated execution store** so the receiver's own state cannot change resolution.\n    13\t4. **Ownership Arbitration:** Emitting a continuation does not transfer ownership. Exclusion requires a **grounded authority** — a resource the grantor's boundary actually possesses, named in the value, independent of carriers — plus an **exporting state** that fences the source before publication, and **effect fencing** with authority epochs and stable operation ids, because absence of a lapse record is never evidence of tenure.\n    14\t5. **Code Identity (Content Addressing):** Because tempids (`:segment 123`) are local to a single machine's database, continuations must refer to code via immutable, versioned semantic profiles — a **canonical instruction vector** (positional tuples, not EAV) whose address is computed over the loader's resolved interpretation, whose addressed payload is the vector itself, and whose interpretation is pinned by a contract stamp versioning the *complete* execution contract.\n    15\t\n    16\t---\n    17\t\n    18\t# Universal Continuation Format — protocol draft\n    19\t\n    20\tStatus: Phase 5 design draft, 2026-09-14; revised 2026-09-14 (r2) per the\n    21\tarchitectural review `collab/1789387292000-architect-ucf-review.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`;\n    22\tamended 2026-09-14 (r3) by owner ruling — §7.3's canonical form is the\n    23\tpositional instruction tuple vector, not EAV (§7.3.2).\n    24\tSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md); builds on the Phase 0\n    25\tcontract in [`yin.vm.semantic.md`](./yin.vm.semantic.md) (§1–§4), the storage\n    26\tcontract in [`dao.jing.md`](./dao.jing.md), the stream contract in\n    27\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), and the lease vocabulary in\n    28\t[`dao.lease.md`](./dao.lease.md). The five blockers above are answered in\n    29\t§7.3–§7.7, one section each, in dependency order: code identity first, because\n    30\tevery other part names code.\n    31\t\n    32\tEvery sentence in §7.2–§7.9 is a rule of the proposed protocol. §7.11 records\n    33\twhat is deliberately left open, as acceptance blockers. Nothing below is\n    34\timplemented; the warning at the top stands until an implementation phase\n    35\tresolves each blocker with tests.\n    36\t\n    37\t## §7.1 Stance: a continuation is a value, not a session\n    38\t\n    39\tThe semantic VM already parks as pure data (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.5): a\n    40\twait entry or a `:parked-continuation` record is `{segment pc env stack k}`\n    41\tplus a reason and resource ids, with no handle and no closure attached. That\n    42\trecord is *locally* complete and *globally* meaningless: its segment is a\n    43\ttempid, its stream ids are keys into this VM's store, its environment may hold\n    44\thost functions, and nothing says which primitives it assumed.\n    45\t\n    46\tThe Universal Continuation Format (UCF) is the **lift** of that local record\n    47\tinto a self-describing value that any conforming engine can **lower** back\n    48\tinto its own registers, and the discipline that governs who may lower it.\n    49\tThree roles participate, and they are stream roles, not parties to a call:\n    50\t\n    51\t| Role | Does | Holds |\n    52\t|---|---|---|\n    53\t| **Emitter** | lifts a parked configuration into a UCF value and appends it to a medium | nothing, once appended |\n    54\t| **Carrier** | any stream, file, DHT, or socket that moves the value; sees opaque data | nothing |\n    55\t| **Resumer** | reads the value, satisfies its declared context, obtains custody, lowers, runs | custody, for the duration of a lease |\n    56\t\n    57\tThree consequences are load-bearing. First, **a UCF value is content**: it has\n    58\ta content address under `dao.jing`, equal values converge on one address, and\n    59\tcarrying it twice is harmless (`dao.jing.md`, *Materialization rule*). Second,\n    60\t**a UCF value on a stream is not a claim**: a stream has no privileged reader\n    61\t(`datom.world.md`, *Streams*), so emission transfers nothing. Custody is\n    62\tarbitrated above the stream, by leases over datoms (§7.7), exactly where\n    63\t`dao.stream.md` *Explicitly Absent* says a take belongs. Third, **content is\n    64\tnot occurrence**: the same parked computation can be encoded more than once\n    65\t(sharing thresholds differ, §7.5.3), so the encodings address differently,\n    66\twhile remaining one *checkpoint occurrence*. Content identity is for\n    67\tdeduplication and materialization; occurrence identity is what custody names\n    68\t(§7.3.4, §7.7). Conflating them was a defect of the first draft; this draft\n    69\tnever does.\n    70\t\n    71\tThe reference engine for every rule below is `yin.vm.semantic`. Its\n    72\tconfiguration ⟨C, E, S, K⟩ with C = ⟨seg, pc, val, St⟩ (`yin.vm.semantic.md`\n    73\t§4.1) is the **canonical CESK**. A foreign engine conforms by lifting into and\n    74\tlowering from this configuration, never by extending it.\n    75\t\n    76\t## §7.2 Vocabulary and envelope\n    77\t\n    78\tUCF facts live under `:yin.k/*` (K for the continuation component of CESK).\n    79\tA UCF value is one open map dispatching on `:yin.k/type`; a consumer ignores\n    80\tqualified keys it does not understand, as for every DaoStream envelope. The\n    81\tvalue must survive the host codec structurally unchanged: no function, host\n    82\tobject, or live handle anywhere inside it (`dao.stream.md`, *Envelopes*).\n    83\tEvery value it contains is encoded in the disjoint tagged grammar of §7.5 —\n    84\tnever a bare marker map.\n    85\t\n    86\t```clojure\n    87\t{:yin.k/type        :yin.k/continuation\n    88\t :yin.k/contract    {:yin.code/contract \"v2\" :yin.k/version 0}   ; §7.3.3\n    89\t :yin.k/id          :segment/sha256-…          ; §7.3.4  content address of this map minus :yin.k/id\n    90\t :yin.k/occurrence  :yin.k/o-…                  ; §7.7    the checkpoint occurrence — the lease subject\n    91\t :yin.k/origin      {:yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/o-…                        ; predecessor, or absent on a first park\n    92\t                    :dao.lease/lease   :dao.lease/l-…                     ; the grant it ran under\n    93\t                    :yin.k/emitter     <attribution>}\n    94\t :yin.k/arbitration {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}     ; §7.7.3  the authority medium, named in the value\n    95\t :yin.k/frame       {…}                        ; §7.4  the canonical CESK at a safepoint\n    96\t :yin.k/values      {…}                        ; §7.5.3  shared subvalues, by content address\n    97\t :yin.k/cells       {…}                        ; §7.5.3  cursor cells: logical identity + position\n    98\t :yin.k/scheduler   {…}                        ; §7.6.3  fresh-name state and referenced parked records\n    99\t :yin.k/requires    {…}                        ; §7.6  dependency closure, with discovery status\n   100\t :yin.k/store       {…}                        ; §7.6.2  the store slice restored as an isolated store\n   101\t :yin.k/carried     [ … ]                      ; §7.3.4  canonical instruction vectors shipped inline\n   102\t :yin.k/policy      :yin.k/exclusive}          ; §7.7  or :yin.k/fork\n   103\t```\n   104\t\n   105\tThe parts answer four questions a resumer must be able to ask of the data\n   106\talone: *what code* (`:yin.k/requires`, `:yin.k/carried`, and every segment\n   107\taddress inside the frame), *what state* (`:yin.k/frame`, `:yin.k/values`,\n   108\t`:yin.k/cells`, `:yin.k/store`, `:yin.k/scheduler`), *what world*\n   109\t(`:yin.k/requires`, `:yin.k/arbitration`), and *who may run it*\n   110\t(`:yin.k/policy`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, with the custody facts of §7.7 on\n   111\ttheir own medium).\n   112\t\n   113\tA halted computation travels as the sibling value:\n   114\t\n   115\t```clojure\n   116\t{:yin.k/type      :yin.k/result\n   117\t :yin.k/contract  {…}\n   118\t :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/o-…     ; the occurrence that halted\n   119\t :yin.k/value     <encoded>}      ; the final accumulator, in the §7.5 grammar\n   120\t```\n   121\t\n   122\tBoth envelope kinds take their identity by the same rule: `:yin.k/id` is the\n   123\t`dao.jing` segment address of the map with `:yin.k/id` removed (§7.3.4).\n   124\t\n   125\tOutcomes of every UCF operation are data: one map dispatching on\n   126\t`:yin.k/status`, in the DaoStream result convention. Where an underlying\n   127\tstream outcome is involved, it is preserved unchanged under its own dispatch\n   128\tkey `:dao.stream/outcome` — UCF never renames or nests it under a private\n   129\tkey. The full algebra is in §7.9.\n   130\t\n   131\t## §7.3 Code identity — content-addressed semantic profiles (blocker 5)\n   132\t\n   133\t### 7.3.1 The problem restated as data\n   134\t\n   135\t`:segment 123` is an entity id minted by one transactor; another machine's\n   136\t`123` is different code or no code. A continuation naming code by tempid is\n   137\ttherefore not a value, it is a pointer. UCF names code by **what it is**.\n   138\t\n   139\t### 7.3.2 The canonical instruction vector and its address\n   140\t\n   141\tThe segment attribute `:yin.code/hash`, reserved in `yin.vm.semantic.md`\n   142\t§2.2, becomes required on any segment a continuation may reference. Its\n   143\tvalue is the `dao.jing` segment address of the segment's **canonical\n   144\tinstruction vector**. The canonical form has had two predecessors, each\n   145\tnamed so no later plan reinvents it: the first draft's sorted `[e a v]`\n   146\ttriples, which were neither total nor execution-faithful; and the r2\n   147\trevision's attributed-map record (`{pc → {attr → value}}`), which was sound\n   148\tbut carried datom vocabulary into the identity layer — attribute names and\n   149\tadmissibility rules where position and the opcode table suffice. The r2\n   150\tform is superseded by two owner rulings: tuples are the correct way to\n   151\trepresent a semantic AST, though the datom `[e a v t m]` need not be the\n   152\ttuple shape when content-hashing code; and `dao.space.query` assumes no\n   153\tEAV — its `relation` is an arbitrary mixed-dimensional tuple collection\n   154\t(`src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:153-158`) — so Datalog-over-code does not\n   155\trequire `[e a v t m]` and UCF must not assume it anywhere for code. The\n   156\tcanonical form is therefore the **positional instruction tuple vector**:\n   157\tone tuple per instruction, pc is the index, and the §2.4 opcode table fixes\n   158\teach mnemonic's tuple arity and operand kinds. Nothing else names a slot.\n   159\t\n   160\t**Input.** The vector is computed from a well-formed batch (§2.6) by first\n   161\tapplying the loader's *resolved interpretation* — the same\n   162\t`index-batch` collapse the loader and the validator perform, where a repeated\n   163\tsingle-valued attribute keeps its **last** value. This is the load-bearing\n   164\tchoice. Two batches whose constants arrive as `1, 2` versus `2, 1` resolve to\n   165\tindexed values `2` and `1`; they *execute differently*, and over the resolved\n   166\tinterpretation they canonicalize differently and address differently. Over\n   167\traw triples they sorted identically — the collision the review demonstrated.\n   168\tCanonicalization and execution now read the same value by construction; there\n   169\tis nothing left to collide. The last-value-wins rule itself is part of the\n   170\tcontract stamp (§7.3.3) and is versioned with it.\n   171\t\n   172\t**The vector.** With the resolution applied, the canonical instruction\n   173\tvector of a segment is (the §2.7 worked segment, excerpted):\n   174\t\n   175\t```clojure\n   176\t[[:closure [x] 6]           ; mnemonic, params, resolved body pc\n   177\t [:push]\n   178\t [:const 10]                ; §2.5 literal: data only\n   179\t [:call 1 false]            ; argc, tail?\n   180\t …]\n   181\t```\n   182\t\n   183\tRules, each replacing heavier machinery from the attributed-map record:\n   184\t\n   185\t- **pc is the index.** No entity ids and no attribute names appear anywhere\n   186\t  in the form: position fixes meaning, the §2.4 table fixes shape, and a\n   187\t  query over code is a query over tuples of the vector, not over EAV.\n   188\t- **Saturation.** Defaults the loader applies are materialized in the tuple —\n   189\t  `:gensym` absent prefix becomes `\"id\"`, `:ffi-call` absent argc becomes\n   190\t  `0`, `:stream-make` absent buffer becomes the default capacity — so a batch\n   191\t  that omitted a defaulted operand and one that stated it canonicalize\n   192\t  identically, because they execute identically.\n   193\t- **Refs are resolved pcs**, exactly as the loader resolves them.\n   194\t- **The header folds away.** `:yin.code/length` is `(count vector)`,\n   195\t  `:yin.code/type :segment` is implied by the form. Provenance\n   196\t  (`:yin.code/derived-from`, every `:yin.code/source`), entity ids, batch\n   197\t  order, `t`, `m`, and `:yin.code/hash` itself are excluded — the address is\n   198\t  computed *over* the vector, which therefore cannot contain it without\n   199\t  hashing the hash being verified.\n   200\t- **Admissible slots.** Each tuple is its mnemonic plus exactly the operands\n   201\t  the §2.4 table gives it, in the table's order. A batch that resolves to\n   202\t  anything else — a mnemonic outside the table, an operand the mnemonic's\n   203\t  tuple has no slot for, a segment-entity attribute beyond the folded and\n   204\t  excluded set — is **non-canonicalizable**, and canonicalization refuses\n   205\t  naming the instruction (the lifted value is never minted; the refusal is\n   206\t  `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :non-canonicalizable`).\n   207\t- **Ordering: none is imposed.** The vector's order is its positions;\n   208\t  canonicalization sorts nothing and compares no operand against another.\n   209\t  Literal operands are values and hash under the same encoder as everything\n   210\t  else, so equal literals address equally however their maps were built.\n   211\t  The first draft's `[:segment …]`-versus-`[0 …]` triple sort — which throws\n   212\t  `Keyword cannot be cast to Number` in the very host it must run on — has\n   213\t  no successor here because there is no sort at all.\n   214\t\n   215\t**The address** is `(dao.jing/segment-key vector)` — a\n   216\t`:segment/sha256-<hex>` keyword. The vector *is* the resolved\n   217\tinterpretation, so r2's collision-freedom argument carries over unchanged\n   218\tand needs only restating: canonicalization and execution read the same\n   219\tvalue by construction; there is nothing left to collide. The address fixes\n   220\tthe resolved instruction stream — mnemonics, operands, control graph,\n   221\tsaturations — and nothing else.\n   222\t\n   223\t**What equal addresses do and do not mean.** Two segments address equally\n   224\t**iff** their loader-resolved interpretations are equal; this includes\n   225\tsegments that differ only in entity ids, batch order, provenance, or omitted\n   226\tdefaulted operands. No equivalence beyond that is claimed: different pc\n   227\tlayouts, different `:gensym` prefixes, different but *equivalent* instruction\n   228\tsequences, and compiler-renamed artifacts address **differently**. The\n   229\taddress is a fingerprint of one instruction stream, not a quotient of a\n   230\tsemantics.\n   231\t\n   232\tThe canonical encoding beneath `segment-key` is transitional\n   233\t(`dao.jing.md`, *Open items*). UCF inherits that limit unchanged: addresses\n   234\tare portable exactly as far as `dao.jing` addresses are, and when the pinned\n   235\tbyte encoding lands, `:yin.code/hash` values change with every other minted\n   236\taddress. This is the correct dependency; UCF must not mint a second\n   237\taddressing scheme.\n   238\t\n   239\t### 7.3.3 The contract stamp\n   240\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '241,500p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | nl -ba -v 241\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '241,500p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | nl -ba -v 241\"","aggregated_output":"   241\tAn address fixes *which* instructions; it does not fix what `:call` *means*.\n   242\tInterpretation is supplied by the loader and the step loop (§1.1,\n   243\t*Interpretation Creates Semantics*), and the first draft pinned only the\n   244\topcode table — leaving resolution precedence, effect outcomes, call\n   245\trestoration, and scheduling outside the stamp. UCF therefore versions the\n   246\t**complete execution contract**: the stamp names one published revision of\n   247\t\n   248\t- the tuple grammar of §7.3.2 — the mnemonic set, each mnemonic's tuple\n   249\t  arity and operand kinds, and the saturation/defaults table — plus the\n   250\t  §2.6 well-formedness rules and the resolved-interpretation\n   251\t  (last-value-wins) rule;\n   252\t- the §2.4 opcode table and the §4.2 transitions;\n   253\t- the resolution precedence of `resolve-var` (env → store → primitives →\n   254\t  modules);\n   255\t- the effect→outcome mapping of §3.3 and `engine/handle-effect`;\n   256\t- call restoration and the scheduler semantics of §3.5 — wait-entry shapes,\n   257\t  `check-wait-set`'s round order, `semantic-restore`.\n   258\t\n   259\t```clojure\n   260\t:yin.k/contract {:yin.code/contract \"v2\"    ; the revision above, by name\n   261\t                 :yin.k/version    0}       ; this envelope's own version\n   262\t```\n   263\t\n   264\tA resumer whose loader implements a different revision does not guess: it\n   265\treturns `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` (§7.9) *before* lowering. Adding an opcode,\n   266\tchanging a transition, or changing the resolution order is a new revision; a\n   267\tresumer on the old one cannot run code that observes the difference, and the\n   268\tstamp is how it finds out before lowering rather than at an unknown integer\n   269\tin `case`. Two revisions may be declared compatible only by a published\n   270\tstatement in `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4, never by an engine's own judgment.\n   271\tBecause interpretation is part of identity, an engine's code index is keyed\n   272\t**per stamp revision**: lookup is `(get-in index [stamp address])`, and the\n   273\tsame address under different stamps is a different image the same way the\n   274\tsame datoms under different loaders are a different program.\n   275\t\n   276\t### 7.3.4 References to code inside a continuation\n   277\t\n   278\tEvery place the local record says `:segment <id>` — the control, each\n   279\t`:return` frame, each effect frame, each closure's `:segment` — the UCF frame\n   280\tsays `:yin.k/segment <address>`. Entry pcs and frame pcs are unchanged: a pc\n   281\tis meaningful relative to the resolved stream and is stable under everything\n   282\t§7.3.2 normalizes away.\n   283\t\n   284\t**The addressed object is the vector.** `dao.jing` materializes the exact\n   285\tpayload it is handed and verifies address-equals-hash on every write and read\n   286\t(`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc`, `materialize!`); it supplies no aliases. So the payload\n   287\tstored at a code address **is the canonical instruction vector** — nothing\n   288\telse hashes to that address. A raw segment batch, with tempids and\n   289\tprovenance, materializes under its *own* different address and is not a\n   290\tresolution mechanism for UCF; `:yin.k/carried` carries vectors, not batches.\n   291\t\n   292\tLoading a fetched vector has two paths, and both are specified. The\n   293\t**projection path** converts the vector to loadable datoms deterministically\n   294\t— segment entity eid `0`, instruction of pc `p` eid `(inc p)`, each tuple\n   295\temitted with `:yin.code/segment 0` and its saturated operands as attribute\n   296\tdatoms (so the round trip vector → datoms → loader is the identity), each\n   297\tresolved-pc operand emitted as a ref to that eid — and hands the batch to\n   298\tthe ordinary §2.6 loader, so every well-formedness check runs on the way in.\n   299\tThe **direct path** decodes the canonical vector into an image without\n   300\tdatoms, as a foreign engine would. The two paths are a conformance\n   301\tobligation: both must yield the same image, and a test in the harness (§7.11)\n   302\tasserts it for every corpus segment. `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` §7.2\n   303\tsupersedes this section's original designation of the projection path as the\n   304\treference path: the direct path is primary and is the reference; the\n   305\tprojection path is derived from it. Both invoke the same validator, and\n   306\tnothing may depend on which path a conforming engine takes.\n   307\t\n   308\tOn lowering, the resumer resolves each address in this order and stops at the\n   309\tfirst hit:\n   310\t\n   311\t1. its own code index for the value's stamp, keyed by address, each entry\n   312\t   verified once by hashing and grammar-validating its vector;\n   313\t2. `:yin.k/carried` in the value itself, verified the same way before load;\n   314\t3. a `dao.jing` read by address, verified the same way.\n   315\t\n   316\tVerification is two checks, always both: the vector must hash to the address\n   317\tit claims (`:yin.k/hash-mismatch` otherwise), and it must satisfy the stamp's\n   318\ttuple grammar — mnemonic set, arity, operand kinds, saturations in place\n   319\t(`:yin.k/undecodable` naming the pc otherwise; a correct hash is not\n   320\tstructural validation, the same rule the value decoder applies, §7.5.1). A\n   321\tmiss is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the address (§7.6.5); nothing from a\n   322\tvalue that fails either check is loaded.\n   323\t\n   324\t**The address index is additive; local-id immutability stands.** The §3.1\n   325\trule — an id already holding a different image is a load error, an identical\n   326\treload accepted — is unchanged by UCF, and the first draft's claim that a\n   327\thash check *replaces* it was wrong: two different, correctly hashed segments\n   328\tcan claim the same local id, and redirecting an existing id would silently\n   329\trewire every frame that names it. A lowering engine therefore keeps two\n   330\tmaps: `:code {local-id image}` under the §3.1 rule, and a separate\n   331\t`{stamp {address image}}` index with an `address → local-id` alias column.\n   332\tSegments loaded *by address* may mint fresh local ids; they may never reuse a\n   333\tlive one, and the alias column is checked so one address never lands under\n   334\ttwo local ids in one VM.\n   335\t\n   336\t**The continuation's own identity and the occurrence.** `:yin.k/id` is the\n   337\t`dao.jing` segment address of the UCF map with `:yin.k/id` removed — and by\n   338\tthe same no-alias argument, the payload published under `:yin.k/id` is the\n   339\t**id-less body**; a reader reconstructs the envelope with\n   340\t`(assoc body :yin.k/id (dao.jing/segment-key body))`. Equal continuations\n   341\tconverge; an idempotent re-publication of one encoding is a no-op. But\n   342\tencodings legitimately differ (§7.5.3), so `:yin.k/id` is *not* what custody,\n   343\tchains, or grants name. They name `:yin.k/occurrence` — the checkpoint\n   344\toccurrence id, minted exactly once, when a park is first exported, unique\n   345\tunder the emitter's attribution, and carried unchanged by every authorized\n   346\tsnapshot variant and every retry of the same park (§7.7.2). A re-emission\n   347\tafter a failed publication reuses the occurrence; it never mints a second\n   348\trunnable checkpoint.\n   349\t\n   350\t## §7.4 Safepoints — where a canonical configuration exists (blocker 1)\n   351\t\n   352\t### 7.4.1 Only safepoints lift, and the table is the machine's\n   353\t\n   354\tThe linear machine's registers are canonical only *between* instructions.\n   355\tInside a `:call` that has popped its operands but not yet pushed a frame, or\n   356\tinside a superinstruction the loader fused (§6.4), no ⟨C, E, S, K⟩ exists\n   357\tthat both describes the state and is expressible in the §2.4 vocabulary.\n   358\tUCF resolves this by rule rather than by metadata: **a continuation may be\n   359\tlifted only at a safepoint**, and a safepoint is a transition of the\n   360\treference machine at which the machine parks. The first draft's table\n   361\tpresented itself as complete while omitting the parks that arrive through\n   362\t`:call` — a called host function that returns an effect blocks exactly as an\n   363\tinstruction effect does, through `apply-call` → `handle-effect` →\n   364\t`call-park-entries`, and both ordinary and tail calls reach that path\n   365\t(`yin.vm.semantic`, `apply-call`). The complete set:\n   366\t\n   367\t| Safepoint kind | Raised by | Canonical resume pc | Stack in frame (St) | Accumulator on resume |\n   368\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   369\t| explicit park | `:park` | `pc+1` | St at park | the resume value |\n   370\t| blocked read | `:stream-next` → `blocked` | `pc+1` | St (cursor ref was in `val`) | the read value; `nil` on `end`, `:dao.stream/gap` on gap |\n   371\t| blocked write | `:stream-put` → `full` | `pc+1` | St minus the popped target ref | the written value, on retry `ok` |\n   372\t| FFI call, sent | `:ffi-call` → `:dao.stream/ok` | `pc+1` | St minus the popped args | the correlated response's `ok` value; its `error` raises |\n   373\t| FFI call, retained | `:ffi-call` → `:dao.stream/full` | `pc+1` | St minus the popped args | as sent, once the request is appended and the response correlates |\n   374\t| effectful call | `:call`/`:tailcall` whose operator yields a blocking effect (`require`, stream-module functions, effect handlers) | `pc+1` | St minus the popped operator and args | the effect's resume value, per its kind as above |\n   375\t| halt | `:halt`, `:return` with empty K | — | — | not resumable; a `:yin.k/type :yin.k/result` travels instead |\n   376\t\n   377\tTwo corrections to the first draft's table stand beside the addition.\n   378\t`:current-continuation` is **not** a safepoint: it does not park — it\n   379\treifies `val ← {seg, pc+1, E, St, K}` and continues, so a reified\n   380\tcontinuation is a *value class* of the encoding (§7.5.1), never a lift\n   381\tpoint. And `pc+1` as the resume pc is verified sound for every row above:\n   382\t§2.6 requires the final pc to be a terminator, so every listed instruction\n   383\thas a successor in its own segment.\n   384\t\n   385\tThe `:yin.k/frame` is exactly the wait-entry shape of `yin.vm.semantic.md`\n   386\t§3.5, with segments replaced by addresses and values by their portable\n   387\tencodings (§7.5):\n   388\t\n   389\t```clojure\n   390\t:yin.k/frame\n   391\t{:yin.k/segment  :segment/sha256-3f1a…\n   392\t :yin.k/pc       5                         ; the resume pc, already pc+1\n   393\t :yin.k/reason   :next                     ; :park | :next | :put | :ffi | :ffi-request | :call-effect\n   394\t :yin.k/val      nil                       ; accumulator; nil except where §7.4.3 pre-fills it\n   395\t :yin.k/stack    [ … ]                     ; St per the table above, encoded\n   396\t :yin.k/env      { sym → encoded value }   ; E\n   397\t :yin.k/k        [ {:yin.k/frame-type :return                    ; or :eval-call / :request-sent,\n   398\t                    :yin.k/segment :segment/sha256-…             ; which also carry :yin.k/call-id\n   399\t                    :yin.k/pc 5 :yin.k/env {…} :yin.k/stack-base 0} … ]   ; K, innermost last\n   400\t :yin.k/pending  {…}}                      ; the wait it was in, §7.4.3 — one variant per reason\n   401\t```\n   402\t\n   403\t**Quiescence.** The migration unit is one task: the whole blocked machine\n   404\t(§7.6.3). Lifting additionally requires the machine's ready-queue to be\n   405\tempty — a machine with runnable work queued is mid-scheduling, its next\n   406\tconfiguration is not the parked one, and lift answers `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n   407\t\n   408\t### 7.4.2 Safepoint maps for foreign engines: static facts only\n   409\t\n   410\tThe reference machine needs no metadata because its registers *are* the\n   411\tcanonical configuration. A foreign engine — a register allocator over the\n   412\timage, a WebAssembly lowering, a JIT — keeps its state in physical locations\n   413\tof its own choosing, and the first draft promised it reconstruction data\n   414\tthat is not a function of the code: absolute stack depth and environment\n   415\tkey sets at a pc depend on the *activation* — a closure enters with the\n   416\tcaller's residual operand stack and an environment formed from its captured\n   417\tenvironment plus arguments (`apply-call`), and §2.6 enforces no equal stack\n   418\theights at joins, so the same pc is legitimately reached at many depths.\n   419\tNone of that is derivable, and none of it needs to be: **the frame carries\n   420\tit.** E, St, and every K frame's `:stack-base` and env travel in the value;\n   421\ta foreign engine lowers the canonical frame directly.\n   422\t\n   423\tWhat *is* a function of the canonical vector alone, and what such an engine\n   424\tmust publish — **per segment and per engine profile**, derived from the\n   425\tcanonical stream at its own load time — is the static half:\n   426\t\n   427\t```clojure\n   428\t[[sp :yin.safepoint/segment      :segment/sha256-3f1a…]  ; the code, by address\n   429\t [sp :yin.safepoint/engine       :wasm32/v1]             ; the engine profile that produced this layout\n   430\t [sp :yin.safepoint/pc           5]                      ; canonical resume pc\n   431\t [sp :yin.safepoint/stack-effect -1]                     ; Δ|St| across the parking instruction (static)\n   432\t [sp :yin.safepoint/lexically-required [x conn]]         ; names this pc can read from E (static)\n   433\t [sp :yin.safepoint/layout       {…}]]                   ; engine-owned: physical slot → canonical slot\n   434\t```\n   435\t\n   436\tRules:\n   437\t\n   438\t- `stack-effect` and `lexically-required` are **derived from the canonical\n   439\t  vector alone**, by the same static walk the linearizer performs (§5.3).\n   440\t  They are identical for every engine and checkable against the reference\n   441\t  machine by a conformance test that runs the segment to each safepoint and\n   442\t  compares the observed deltas and E-reads. Absolute depth, activation\n   443\t  bases, captured environments, and physical layouts are **not** claimed,\n   444\t  not derived, and not in this table; they are the frame's business.\n   445\t- `:yin.safepoint/layout` is opaque to the protocol. It is a memo the engine\n   446\t  keeps so that its `lift` is a table lookup. It never travels in a UCF\n   447\t  value; a resumer that is a different engine has its own.\n   448\t- `lift(engine-state, layout) → canonical frame` and\n   449\t  `lower(canonical frame, layout) → engine-state` are total on safepoints and\n   450\t  undefined elsewhere. The conformance obligation is `lift ∘ lower = id` on\n   451\t  canonical frames, tested against the reference machine's own frames —\n   452\t  which is possible precisely because the dynamic half rides in the frame.\n   453\t- A request to lift at a pc that is not a safepoint of the segment returns\n   454\t  `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` naming the pc. An engine that wants finer\n   455\t  interruption granularity must lower the program to a segment that\n   456\t  *contains* a `:park` where it wants one: that is a different instruction\n   457\t  record, a different address, and a different profile. An engine may not\n   458\t  invent safepoints a record does not have, and two engines that disagree\n   459\t  about the safepoint set of one address are not both conforming.\n   460\t\n   461\tSafepoints are datoms, so a query can ask \"where can this program be\n   462\tmigrated?\" without running it, and a debugger's breakpoint set is a subset of\n   463\tthe safepoint set by construction.\n   464\t\n   465\t### 7.4.3 The pending wait travels as data — complete, and resumable elsewhere\n   466\t\n   467\tA parked machine was waiting for something. That something is a resource of\n   468\tthe emitter's host, and the resumer cannot poll it. `:yin.k/pending` records\n   469\tthe wait in resource-independent terms so the resumer can re-establish an\n   470\t*equivalent* wait, or decide it cannot. The first draft carried sketches;\n   471\tthe set here is exhaustive over §7.4.1's reasons, and each variant carries\n   472\teverything a *foreign* resumer needs — including the state the first draft\n   473\tleft implicit on the emitter's heap:\n   474\t\n   475\t```clojure\n   476\t;; blocked read (:stream-next)\n   477\t{:yin.k/reason   :next\n   478\t :yin.k/cell     :yin.k/c-17}          ; the cursor CELL id, §7.5.3 — not a bare position\n   479\t\n   480\t;; blocked write (:stream-put, or an effectful call's :stream/put)\n   481\t{:yin.k/reason   :put\n   482\t :yin.k/stream   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; the target\n   483\t :yin.k/value    <encoded>}            ; the retained value — a retry appends THIS, and\n   484\t                                       ; without it the wait is not re-establishable\n   485\t\n   486\t;; FFI call, sent (:ffi-call → ok)\n   487\t{:yin.k/reason     :ffi\n   488\t :yin.k/call-id    :call-7             ; the :dao.stream.apply/id correlation\n   489\t :yin.k/op         :op/add\n   490\t :yin.k/request    {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the request went\n   491\t :yin.k/response   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the answer lands\n   492\t :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23         ; the response cursor cell, §7.5.3, at its KEPT position}\n   493\t\n   494\t;; FFI call, retained (:ffi-call → full): the request is still in hand\n   495\t{:yin.k/reason     :ffi-request\n   496\t :yin.k/call-id    :call-7\n   497\t :yin.k/request-op :op/add\n   498\t :yin.k/request-args [<encoded> …]     ; enough to rebuild the envelope verbatim\n   499\t :yin.k/request    {identity/descriptor as above}\n   500\t :yin.k/response   {identity/descriptor as above}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '501,760p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | nl -ba -v 501\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '501,760p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | nl -ba -v 501\"","aggregated_output":"   501\t :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23}        ; response cell at its kept position\n   502\t```\n   503\t\n   504\tThree rules make these resumable somewhere other than where they were minted:\n   505\t\n   506\t- **The response position is a kept cursor, never an anchor.** Reattaching\n   507\t  at `:oldest` replays responses that belong to other calls; attaching at\n   508\t  `:newest` skips past this call's own. The response cell therefore travels\n   509\t  at its *kept* position, and the whole task's waiters on that cell travel\n   510\t  with it (§7.6.3), so the round-order semantics of `check-wait-set` —\n   511\t  waiters sharing a cell consume successive values — are preserved on the\n   512\t  resumer exactly. A first poll that observes `gap` is the honest outcome\n   513\t  the contract already promises a kept cursor (`dao.stream.md`, *Retention\n   514\t  and Gaps*). This requires the response transport to accept a kept cursor\n   515\t  from another host, which `dao.stream.md` leaves transport-owned and TBD;\n   516\t  UCF therefore declares, per stream, a **portable cursor profile** in\n   517\t  `:yin.k/requires` (`:yin.k/cursor-profiles`), and a transport that does\n   518\t  not declare one cannot be a pending-response endpoint — the lift refuses\n   519\t  it as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before the value is ever minted.\n   520\t- **Outstanding calls route to the emitter's pair; the resumer's pair is its\n   521\t  own.** The reference machine restores a `:request-sent` entry into a\n   522\t  response wait through its fixed local store keys (`vm/call-out-stream-key`\n   523\t  and friends). A resumer must *not* do that: the outstanding call's\n   524\t  response will arrive on the **emitter's** response stream, because that is\n   525\t  where the request was sent. The resumer re-establishes the wait against\n   526\t  the carried response descriptor and the cell's fresh local key (the cell\n   527\t  id remapped per §7.5.3), while calls the resumed program makes *after*\n   528\t  lowering use the resumer's own pair under its own keys. Pending-call\n   529\t  routing and future-call routing are separate stores of state; conflating\n   530\t  them strands the migrated call exactly as the review described.\n   531\t- **On resume, the resumer attaches and re-mints.** Attach to every\n   532\t  descriptor in the frame, pending, and cells; an attachment resolving to\n   533\t  `not-found` is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream identity, never a\n   534\t  silent `nil`; a `:put` wait retries its retained value and resumes with it\n   535\t  on `ok` (the engine, a woken writer's retry appends and stamps the\n   536\t  value); a `:next` wait polls its cell. No wait is silently dropped and no\n   537\t  retained value is recomputed — what parks is what resumes.\n   538\t\n   539\t## §7.5 Recursive portable encoding (blocker 2)\n   540\t\n   541\t### 7.5.1 A disjoint tagged grammar\n   542\t\n   543\tEncoding is one total function `encode : value × path → outcome`, applied\n   544\trecursively to every value reachable from the frame: accumulator, operand\n   545\tstack, environment values, every frame in K, parked records, and the store\n   546\tslice of §7.6.2. The outcome is either the portable form or a refusal naming\n   547\tthe path.\n   548\t\n   549\tThe first draft encoded markers as ordinary maps — `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`,\n   550\t`{:yin.k/ref addr}` — while passing program map literals through in their\n   551\tnatural shape. That is ambiguous to everyone: §2.5 admits map literals,\n   552\tso a program can *contain* `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`, and a decoder cannot tell\n   553\tan encoder's marker from a program's data. The grammar here is disjoint by\n   554\tconstruction: **every map at an encoded-value position is a UCF marker**, and\n   555\ta program's own maps are always wrapped.\n   556\t\n   557\t| Encoded form | Tag | Contents | Decodes to |\n   558\t|---|---|---|---|\n   559\t| scalars: nil, boolean, number, string, keyword, symbol | — | themselves (never a map; cannot forge a marker) | themselves |\n   560\t| vector, list, set | — | same shape, elements recursively encoded (never a map) | same shape, elements decoded |\n   561\t| literal map | `:yin.k/literal` | `:yin.k/entries [k₁ v₁ k₂ v₂ …]`, keys and values recursively encoded | the map, keys and values decoded |\n   562\t| closure | `:yin.k/closure` | `:yin.k/segment addr :yin.k/entry pc :yin.k/params […] :yin.k/env {encoded}` | `{:type :closure …}` (§2.4) |\n   563\t| reified continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame (§7.4.1), recursively | `{:type :reified-continuation …}` |\n   564\t| parked continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame plus `:yin.k/parked-id` | `{:type :parked-continuation …}` |\n   565\t| primitive | `:yin.k/primitive` | `:yin.k/name sym` | the named function, after profile check (§7.5.2) |\n   566\t| stream reference | `:yin.k/stream` | `:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …` | an attached handle, under a fresh store key |\n   567\t| cursor reference | `:yin.k/cursor-ref` | `:yin.k/cell cell-id` | a store cursor entry for that cell, §7.5.3 |\n   568\t| shared subvalue | `:yin.k/ref` | `:yin.k/id addr` | the table entry at `addr`, §7.5.3 |\n   569\t| anything else | — | — | **refused**: `:yin.k/non-portable` |\n   570\t\n   571\tWhy the wrapping is total rather than an \"escaping rule\" for suspicious\n   572\tshapes: a literal map is wrapped *wherever it occurs and whatever it\n   573\tcontains* — including one that happens to read `{:yin.k/tag …}` — and the\n   574\tentries inside a wrapper are themselves encoded, so a forged marker inside a\n   575\tliteral is re-wrapped one level down and peeled back exactly one level on\n   576\tdecode. No rule asks whether a map \"looks like\" data; no decoder decision\n   577\tdepends on program content. Scalar and collection values pass through only\n   578\tbecause no marker is ever a non-map, which the closed tag table fixes.\n   579\t\n   580\tMap keys are values and are encoded as such — inside `:yin.k/entries`\n   581\talternately with their values, and nowhere else; the frame's own `:yin.k/env`\n   582\tkeys are the program's symbols and are structural, not encoded values.\n   583\t\n   584\t**Decoding validates, independent of hashing.** A hash check confirms what\n   585\twas encoded; it does not confirm the encoding is well formed. The decoder\n   586\twalks the grammar and rejects, as `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the path: a map\n   587\twith no `:yin.k/tag` at an encoded-value position (impossible from a\n   588\tconforming encoder); a tag outside the closed set; a marker missing its\n   589\trequired keys; a `:yin.k/ref` whose address is absent from the value table; a\n   590\tref graph that is cyclic *through ref data* (content-addressed table entries\n   591\tcannot mint such a cycle — a cycle can only be written by hand, and it is\n   592\trejected); and a value table entry not referenced by anything reachable.\n   593\tMalformed-but-correctly-hashed input fails here, before any lowering.\n   594\t\n   595\t### 7.5.2 Primitives: a name is a binding, not a meaning\n   596\t\n   597\t§1.1 admits host functions into the environment because `:var` resolves a\n   598\tprimitive symbol to the function value and a program may then bind it\n   599\t(`(def f +)`). At lift time the emitter recognizes such a value by reverse\n   600\tlookup in its own `:primitives` map — identity on the function object. The\n   601\tfirst draft treated the recovered symbol as the whole story. It is not:\n   602\t\n   603\t- one function object may sit under **several names**, making reverse lookup\n   604\t  ambiguous;\n   605\t- two composition-supplied primitive maps may bind the **same symbol to\n   606\t  different functions** — name presence at the resumer proves nothing about\n   607\t  behavior;\n   608\t- identity stability says nothing about **purity or captured host state**.\n   609\t\n   610\tUCF therefore binds every portable function to a **primitive profile**,\n   611\tpublished data, and the binding is checked on both ends:\n   612\t\n   613\t```clojure\n   614\t;; in :yin.k/requires — computed, not hand-written (§7.6.1)\n   615\t:yin.k/primitives {+\n   616\t                   {:yin.k/profile :yin.k.pp/sha256-…   ; content address of the profile record\n   617\t                    :yin.k/class    :pure               ; :pure | :effectful | :host\n   618\t                    :yin.k/arities  [2]\n   619\t                    :yin.k/effects  #{}                 ; for :effectful — declared effect kinds\n   620\t                    :yin.k/host-state :none}}           ; :none, or refused\n   621\t```\n   622\t\n   623\tThe profile record — `{name class arities effects host-state}` under a\n   624\tcontent address — is what a name must be *checked against*, not merely\n   625\tpresent as. Rules:\n   626\t\n   627\t- **Ambiguity refuses the lift.** A function object that reverse-lookups to\n   628\t  more than one name in the emitter's `:primitives` is refused\n   629\t  (`:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:ambiguous-primitive`) unless the\n   630\t  composition declares a canonical-name table resolving it to exactly one.\n   631\t- **A host function under no name is refused** (kind `:unnamed-function`) —\n   632\t  a bridge handler's return value, a module-constructed function: it has no\n   633\t  declared semantics to travel under.\n   634\t- **`:host`-class functions are refused** (kind `:host-state-primitive`). A\n   635\t  function whose behavior depends on undeclared host state is not a value\n   636\t  any other host can honor; profiles that declare host state may not be\n   637\t  lifted, and a profile claiming `:host-state :none` is a publication's\n   638\t  warranty, versioned with the contract stamp's primitive-profile registry.\n   639\t- **On lowering, the resumer's binding is checked by profile, not by\n   640\t  presence.** For each required name, the resumer must hold a primitive\n   641\t  whose profile address is equal; a same-named function of different profile\n   642\t  is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the symbol and the expected profile — it is\n   643\t  a routing decision for whoever chose this resumer, not a silent\n   644\t  substitution.\n   645\t- The standard map `yin.vm/primitives` must be published with profiles\n   646\t  (note `yin/def` is `:effectful` with effect `:vm/store-put`, and `require`\n   647\t  is `:effectful` with `:module/require`); that publication is an acceptance\n   648\t  blocker (§7.11).\n   649\t\n   650\t### 7.5.3 Sharing, cells, and cycles\n   651\t\n   652\t**The value table.** A configuration is a DAG, not a tree: a closure's\n   653\tcaptured environment is the frame's environment; a value on the stack is\n   654\talso bound in `E`. Encoding naively duplicates, and a deep K makes\n   655\tduplication quadratic. The encoder therefore builds a **value table**:\n   656\t\n   657\t- On encountering a collection, closure, or continuation it has already\n   658\t  encoded (host identity during one lift), it emits\n   659\t  `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/ref :yin.k/id addr}` where `addr` is the `dao.jing`\n   660\t  address of the encoded subvalue, and places the subvalue in\n   661\t  `:yin.k/values {addr → encoded}`.\n   662\t- A subvalue is placed in the table when it is referenced more than once or\n   663\t  its encoded size exceeds a composition-supplied threshold; otherwise it\n   664\t  stays inline. Both choices yield equal semantics; only the table is needed\n   665\t  for correctness of sharing.\n   666\t- A cycle (a value reachable from itself through host identity) is refused\n   667\t  as `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :cyclic` naming the path. The\n   668\t  reference machine cannot produce one — `E` is extended by `merge`, never\n   669\t  mutated — so the refusal guards against foreign engines and bridge-returned\n   670\t  host structures. Cycles *through ref data* cannot be minted at all and are\n   671\t  rejected at decode (§7.5.1); this single representation retires the first\n   672\t  draft's two competing ones.\n   673\t\n   674\tBecause table entries are content-addressed, a carrier that already holds an\n   675\tentry (a `dao.jing` intake pool, a DHT replica) need not carry it again, and\n   676\ttwo continuations parked from the same closure share its encoding. This is\n   677\t`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.3's checkpoint-as-published-fold applied to\n   678\ta single frame. It is also why §7.3.4 separates `:yin.k/id` (which varies\n   679\twith these choices, harmlessly) from `:yin.k/occurrence` (which does not).\n   680\t\n   681\t**Cursor cells.** A cursor is *observable state with aliasing*: two distinct\n   682\tcursor refs may sit at the same position and yet be independently\n   683\tadvanceable store entries, while repeated refs to one cursor ref must\n   684\tadvance together — `check-wait-set` advances a shared cursor ref before\n   685\tpolling the next waiter, so waiters on one ref read successive values, and\n   686\twaiters on two refs at the same position read the same next value\n   687\t(`yin.vm.engine`, `check-wait-set`). A position-only encoding erases\n   688\texactly this distinction: two cells at one position would collapse, and\n   689\ttheir independent advancement would be lost. The encoding therefore carries\n   690\t**logical cursor cells** separately from position content:\n   691\t\n   692\t```clojure\n   693\t:yin.k/cells {:yin.k/c-17 {:yin.k/stream   {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}\n   694\t                           :yin.k/position <opaque, exactly as the transport minted it>}}\n   695\t;; values reference cells, never positions:\n   696\t{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/cursor-ref :yin.k/cell :yin.k/c-17}\n   697\t```\n   698\t\n   699\tCell ids are minted by the emitter (stable within one lift; remapped on\n   700\tlowering) and are *not* content-addressed — a cell is a local identity, like\n   701\ta tempid, and deduplicating cells by their contents is precisely the aliasing\n   702\terasure this structure exists to prevent. On lowering, each cell becomes a\n   703\tfresh store cursor entry seeded with its carried position; every\n   704\t`:yin.k/cursor-ref` to it remaps to that entry; two refs to one cell share\n   705\tone entry and one ref per cell keeps its own. The FFI response cell of\n   706\t§7.4.3 is a cell like any other.\n   707\t\n   708\tA stream reference is portable iff its transport produces a descriptor whose\n   709\t`attach!` can succeed somewhere other than the emitter's host. The encoder\n   710\tcannot know that; it encodes the descriptor and lets the resumer's `attach!`\n   711\treport `not-found` as data. What the encoder *can* refuse is a handle whose\n   712\t`descriptor` call fails, which cannot happen under the contract, and a raw\n   713\tin-memory handle admitted by an in-process stream's admission declaration\n   714\t(`dao.stream.md`, *Creation and Attachment*), which it treats as a host\n   715\tobject.\n   716\t\n   717\t### 7.5.4 Failure is total and names its place\n   718\t\n   719\tEncoding a continuation with one refused leaf refuses the whole\n   720\tcontinuation:\n   721\t\n   722\t```clojure\n   723\t{:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable\n   724\t :yin.k/path   [:yin.k/env 'conn]         ; where in the frame\n   725\t :yin.k/kind   :host-object               ; the closed kind set below\n   726\t :yin.k/hint   \"java.net.Socket\"}         ; classified, never the object\n   727\t```\n   728\t\n   729\tThe kind set is closed:\n   730\t`:host-object | :unnamed-function | :ambiguous-primitive | :host-state-primitive\n   731\t| :cyclic | :in-memory-handle | :non-canonicalizable | :foreign-parked-ref`\n   732\t(the last two from §7.3.2 and §7.6.3). There is no partial UCF value and no\n   733\tplaceholder for a missing leaf. A program that wishes to be migratable keeps\n   734\thost resources behind stream descriptors and named, profiled primitives,\n   735\twhich is the same discipline the host boundary rules already impose on\n   736\tportable code (`datom.world.md`, *Host Boundaries*). The refusal is the\n   737\temitter's outcome, returned as data to whatever asked it to lift; the\n   738\tmachine itself is unaffected and remains parked locally.\n   739\t\n   740\t## §7.6 Dependency closure and context (blocker 3)\n   741\t\n   742\t### 7.6.1 Declared, not hand-written — and discovered conservatively\n   743\t\n   744\tA resumer must be able to decide *before lowering* whether it can run the\n   745\tvalue. `:yin.k/requires` carries that decision's inputs, and every set in it\n   746\tis **computed by a conservative fixed point**, not hand-written and not\n   747\tguessed from the current environment:\n   748\t\n   749\t```clojure\n   750\t:yin.k/requires\n   751\t{:yin.k/segments        #{:segment/sha256-3f1a… :segment/sha256-9c02…}  ; every address in frame, k, closures, values, parked\n   752\t :yin.k/primitives      {sym → profile, §7.5.2}                        ; every {:yin.k/primitive …} encoded, plus every free :var\n   753\t :yin.k/modules         {my.mod :segment/sha256-…}                     ; module name → manifest address\n   754\t :yin.k/effects         #{:stream/next :stream/put :module/require}    ; effect kinds the segments can raise\n   755\t :yin.k/ffi-ops         #{:op/add}                                     ; :yin.code/ffi-op values present\n   756\t :yin.k/streams         #{<identity> …}                                ; logical-stream identities referenced anywhere\n   757\t :yin.k/cursor-profiles #{:dao.stream/file-v1}                         ; transports that must accept kept cursors, §7.4.3\n   758\t :yin.k/discovery       :complete}                                     ; :complete | :incomplete | :blocked — computed, §7.6.5\n   759\t```\n   760\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
