{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '761,1020p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | nl -ba -v 761\"","aggregated_output":"   761\tThe fixed point starts from the frame, its K frames, the parked records of\n   762\t§7.6.3, the store slice, and the pending wait. Its unit of analysis is a work\n   763\titem `[code-address context]`, not an address: `context` is the finite\n   764\tabstraction of the captured environment with which a closure enters that\n   765\tcode. Code is fetched, validated, and projected once per address, but every\n   766\tnewly discovered address-context pair is analyzed. Two closures over the\n   767\tsame address with different captured environments are therefore distinct\n   768\twork items. A full pass repeats whenever it adds a work item or any dependency\n   769\tfact, and convergence is reached only when a full pass adds neither:\n   770\t\n   771\t- **Values:** every encoded closure contributes its segment address and its\n   772\t  captured env's values and the work item\n   773\t  `[segment-address captured-env-context]`; every stream and cursor marker\n   774\t  contributes a stream identity; every `:yin.k/primitive` marker contributes\n   775\t  a name. A captured environment contributes reachable values, never name\n   776\t  discharge.\n   777\t- **Code:** every reachable segment's instructions are walked — as datoms\n   778\t  where the emitter holds the batch, as canonical tuples where only a vector\n   779\t  was fetched (§7.3.4) — including **`:store-get` and `:store-put`\n   780\t  operands**, which name store\n   781\t  keys directly and which the first draft missed entirely; every `:var`\n   782\t  name; every effect op (`:stream-*`, `:ffi-call`, and the effects of\n   783\t  `:effectful`-class primitives the walk finds); every `:resume` operand\n   784\t  (a parked id, §7.6.3). Discovering a new work item continues the fixed point\n   785\t  with that address and context even when the address was already walked for\n   786\t  another context.\n   787\t- **Names:** only a free `:var` name is an obligation. An obligation is\n   788\t  discharged by a matching key in the reachable store slice, or retained as\n   789\t  a required primitive or module export and checked by profile (§7.5.2). An\n   790\t  environment never discharges a name: captured environments contribute\n   791\t  values to their work item's reachability analysis, while lexical binding\n   792\t  determines whether a `:var` is free before it becomes an obligation. If no\n   793\t  store key or profiled primitive/module requirement can discharge an\n   794\t  obligation, discovery is `:incomplete`; after lowering, an unavailable\n   795\t  retained requirement is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` (§7.6.5).\n   796\t- **Modules:** a module's manifest declares its exported primitive profiles\n   797\t  and its **store footprint** (store keys and effect kinds its handlers may\n   798\t  touch). Until manifests carry footprints, any required module whose\n   799\t  footprint is undeclared sets `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete` — the honest\n   800\t  statement that closure could not be computed, never a silent\n   801\t  under-approximation. A module effect handler may read store keys the\n   802\t  static walk cannot see; a composition may supply extra slice keys\n   803\t  explicitly, and the value records that it did.\n   804\t\n   805\tBecause the computation is over data, the same query answers \"what does this\n   806\tprogram need?\" for a segment that has never run. And because discovery can\n   807\t*fail to terminate successfully* — a missing segment cannot be walked, an\n   808\tundeclared footprint cannot be closed — the result distinguishes\n   809\t`:complete` from `:incomplete` from `:blocked` (§7.6.5); a fixed point that\n   810\tcould not finish is reported as such, not passed off as a satisfied closure.\n   811\t\n   812\t### 7.6.2 The store slice, restored as an isolated store\n   813\t\n   814\tThe store S holds `def` results, stream handles, cursor entries, and the FFI\n   815\tpair (§4.1). None of it is global in the UCF sense; it is the emitter's. A\n   816\tcontinuation carries the **reachable slice**: every store key the fixed\n   817\tpoint of §7.6.1 can name — every free `:var` obligation for which the\n   818\temitter's store has a matching key, every `:store-get`/`:store-put` operand,\n   819\tevery stream/cursor cell — with values encoded by §7.5:\n   820\t\n   821\t```clojure\n   822\t:yin.k/store {counter 41\n   823\t              'log {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}}\n   824\t```\n   825\t\n   826\tThe FFI pair is never in the slice — a program that names the pair's store\n   827\tkeys directly holds handles, and the lift refuses it (§7.5.4). A resumer has\n   828\tits own pair; outstanding calls route per §7.4.3.\n   829\t\n   830\t**There is no merge.** The first draft said, in two places, both that the\n   831\tslice wins on collision and that the slice merges *under* the local store —\n   832\ta contradiction, and beneath it a real defect: resolution is env → store →\n   833\tprimitives (`resolve-var`), so a resumer whose local store happens to bind\n   834\t`+` would change what the migrated program resolves, without any collision\n   835\tat all. Merging in either order is the wrong shape. **The resumed task runs\n   836\tin an isolated execution store**: a fresh store whose contents are exactly\n   837\tthe carried slice, the remapped cells of §7.5.3, and the resumer's FFI pair\n   838\t— nothing else of the resumer's, nothing of any other task's. A name absent\n   839\tfrom the slice resolves through primitives and modules at the resumer\n   840\texactly as it did at the emitter, because there is no third store to\n   841\tintervene; a slice key cannot collide with a receiver task's key because no\n   842\treceiver key participates; resource entries are remapped to fresh local\n   843\tkeys (cursor cells per §7.5.3, stream handles per §7.4.3), and non-resource\n   844\tentries keep their keys verbatim. \"Unreachable store entries do not travel\"\n   845\tsurvives unchanged — anything the program did not observe is not the\n   846\tprogram's state — and nothing else shares state with the resumed task by\n   847\taccident.\n   848\t\n   849\t### 7.6.3 The migration unit is one task\n   850\t\n   851\tThe reference machine's scheduler state does not live in the store, and the\n   852\tfirst draft transported none of it. Both omissions are defects: the\n   853\tmachine's fresh identifiers come from `:id-counter`, and `:resume` resolves\n   854\tits operand against the `:parked` map — a fresh receiver could reuse an\n   855\texisting generated identifier, or fail to resolve a parked continuation a\n   856\tmigrated segment is about to name (`engine/park-continuation`,\n   857\t`engine/resume-continuation`, §3.5). UCF therefore states the unit and\n   858\tcarries the state:\n   859\t\n   860\t- **The unit is the whole blocked machine**: registers (the frame), the\n   861\t  wait-set (as pending variants, §7.4.3), the referenced `:parked` records,\n   862\t  and the fresh-name counter. The ready-queue must be empty at lift\n   863\t  (`:yin.k/not-quiescent` otherwise, §7.4.1) — a task with queued runnable\n   864\t  work has a next configuration that is not the parked one.\n   865\t- **Fresh-name state travels.** `:yin.k/scheduler {:yin.k/id-counter n\n   866\t  …}` carries the counter verbatim; the lowered machine adopts\n   867\t  `max(local, carried)` so generated ids never repeat, and ids already\n   868\t  minted (gensym results in env, stack, or store) travel as ordinary values,\n   869\t  unaffected by the counter.\n   870\t- **Referenced parked records travel.** The fixed point\n   871\t  of §7.6.1 collects every `:resume` operand in reachable code and every\n   872\t  `[:parked id]` value from reachable environments. Each must resolve\n   873\t  within this task to a parked record carried under\n   874\t  `:yin.k/scheduler :yin.k/parked {pid → frame}`. A missing id referenced\n   875\t  as a code operand is reported as an unsupplied requirement\n   876\t  (`:yin.k/unsatisfied`). A missing id referenced by an active value\n   877\t  refuses the lift (`:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:foreign-parked-ref`),\n   878\t  as it represents corrupted state. Parked records not named by the\n   879\t  reachable graph do not travel.\n   880\t\n   881\t### 7.6.4 Modules\n   882\t\n   883\t`:yin.k/modules` names each module by the content address of its published\n   884\tmanifest, on the model of `dao.space.index/publish-index!` (`dao.jing.md`,\n   885\t*Publication from an agent*). A module is satisfied when the resumer holds a\n   886\tmodule registry entry (`yin.vm.module` — a value, supplied by the\n   887\tcomposition) whose manifest address is equal; it is not satisfied by a\n   888\tsame-named module of different content. Module *code* is segments and is\n   889\tcovered by `:yin.k/segments` and the manifest's own addressing; the manifest\n   890\tpins the effect handlers, the exported primitive profiles (§7.5.2), and —\n   891\tper §7.6.1 — the store footprint the fixed point needs to claim\n   892\t`:complete`.\n   893\t\n   894\t### 7.6.5 The satisfaction check\n   895\t\n   896\tBefore lowering, the resumer evaluates `:yin.k/requires` against what it\n   897\tholds and answers with one outcome:\n   898\t\n   899\t```clojure\n   900\t{:yin.k/status    :yin.k/unsatisfied\n   901\t :yin.k/discovery :incomplete                    ; or :blocked, §7.6.1 — the computed status of the closure itself\n   902\t :yin.k/missing   {:yin.k/segments       #{:segment/sha256-9c02…}\n   903\t                   :yin.k/primitives     {println <expected profile>}\n   904\t                   :yin.k/modules        {my.mod …}\n   905\t                   :yin.k/streams        #{<identity>}\n   906\t                   :yin.k/cursor-profiles #{:dao.stream/file-v1}}}  ; keys present only where something is missing\n   907\t```\n   908\t\n   909\tThe check reports everything missing in one pass *that the fixed point could\n   910\tsee*, and says which kind of pass it was: `:complete` (the closure is fully\n   911\tcomputed; the missing list is exhaustive), `:incomplete` (a module footprint\n   912\twas undeclared; the missing list is a lower bound), or `:blocked` (a missing\n   913\tsegment stopped discovery; satisfying `:yin.k/segments` and re-running the\n   914\tcheck is the only way forward). A missing segment may be resolved by\n   915\t`dao.jing` fetch (§7.3.4); a missing primitive, module, or cursor profile\n   916\tcannot be resolved by the resumer and is a routing decision for whoever\n   917\tchose this resumer. A resumer never lowers a partially satisfied value; a\n   918\thost with no implementation for something answers with the qualified\n   919\tunsupported outcome, which `datom.world.md` calls a correct outcome, not a\n   920\tgap to be filled.\n   921\t\n   922\t## §7.7 Ownership arbitration — custody as a lease over datoms (blocker 4)\n   923\t\n   924\t### 7.7.1 The race, named\n   925\t\n   926\tThe emitter parks, lifts, appends the UCF value to a medium, and keeps its\n   927\tlocal `:parked` record. Three readers see the value. The emitter's own wait\n   928\tset wakes because the stream it was waiting on delivered. Now four machines\n   929\tbelieve they may continue one computation. Nothing in DaoStream can stop\n   930\tthem: a stream has no privileged reader, there is no destructive read, and\n   931\t\"the hard part of a take was never the removal but the *exclusion* of\n   932\tcompeting takers, which is a lease over datoms\" (`dao.stream.md`,\n   933\t*Explicitly Absent*).\n   934\t\n   935\tUCF adopts that sentence as its design. **Resumption is a take. A take is a\n   936\twrite. Exclusion is a lease.** Nothing new is added to the stream contract,\n   937\tand no key is added to any result map.\n   938\t\n   939\t### 7.7.2 Custody facts, and their dispatch keys\n   940\t\n   941\tCustody is negotiated on an **arbitration medium** — a `dao.space` whose\n   942\tfacts are ordinary datoms — using `dao.lease.md`'s vocabulary unchanged,\n   943\twith the checkpoint **occurrence** as the leased subject. The UCF-authored\n   944\tfacts are dispatch-keyed exactly as lease facts are (`dao.lease.md`,\n   945\t*Vocabulary*: a reader switches on `:dao.lease/status`; a fact carrying it\n   946\tis a lease fact, one carrying neither is ignored):\n   947\t\n   948\t| Fact | Dispatch | Author | Required keys |\n   949\t|---|---|---|---|\n   950\t| offer | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/offered` | emitter | `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:yin.k/id` (this snapshot variant), `:yin.k/policy`, `:yin.k/medium` (the carrier identity) |\n   951\t| checkpoint done | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/resumed` | holder | `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:yin.k/result` — the address of the successor's occurrence value or of a `:yin.k/result` |\n   952\t\n   953\tLease facts themselves keep their own table verbatim — proposal\n   954\t(`:dao.lease/proposed`, with `:dao.lease/subject = {:yin.k/occurrence O}`),\n   955\tgrant (`:dao.lease/accepted`, with `:dao.lease/lease :dao.lease/holder\n   956\t:dao.lease/subject :dao.lease/duration`, and `:dao.lease/max` optional),\n   957\trefusal, release, renewal, lapse — each dispatched on `:dao.lease/status`\n   958\tor `:dao.lease/event`, each with `dao.lease.md`'s required keys, unchanged.\n   959\tA lease judge reading the same medium switches on the lease keys and\n   960\tignores the `:yin.k/custody` facts; a UCF reader switches on\n   961\t`:yin.k/custody` and reads lease facts for authority. Both `:yin.k/custody`\n   962\tfacts are **evidence, not authority**: an offer creates no claim, and a\n   963\t`:resumed` record is a holder's report — completion is the grantor's ledger\n   964\ttransition, not the report (§7.7.6). Authority is exactly `dao.lease.md`'s:\n   965\t**only grantor-authored facts establish terms.**\n   966\t\n   967\tThe subject of every grant is the **occurrence**, never `:yin.k/id`:\n   968\tsnapshot variants and retries of one park share one occurrence, so no pair\n   969\tof encodings can hold independent grants, and an equal-content copy minted\n   970\tby coincidence is a different occurrence and a different computation\n   971\t(§7.3.4).\n   972\t\n   973\t### 7.7.3 The authority: what the grantor possesses\n   974\t\n   975\t`dao.lease.md` is explicit that a judge must be composed **inside the\n   976\tboundary that possesses the resource** and must reclaim *by acting on what\n   977\tit itself holds*; a judge that possesses nothing is outside the contract.\n   978\tThe first draft designated \"the composition-designated arbiter for the\n   979\tcarrier medium,\" defaulting to the emitter — an arbiter relative to a\n   980\tcarrier, possessing an offer, which is a copy of content: the same\n   981\toccurrence carried on a second medium would acquire a second arbiter, and\n   982\tneither arbiter controls execution of anything. This section replaces that\n   983\twith a grounded design.\n   984\t\n   985\t**The possessed resource is the occurrence's execution ledger**: the set of\n   986\tdatoms about one occurrence id on the arbitration `dao.space` — its offer,\n   987\tgrants, renewals, lapses, and resumed reports, plus the occurrence's\n   988\t**admission epoch** (§7.7.5). The **grantor is the boundary that transacts\n   989\tthat space** — its transactor. Possession is exact and of the right kind:\n   990\t\n   991\t- the ledger's datom base lives inside the transactor's boundary, and\n   992\t  admission to it is the transactor's alone — no other boundary can write\n   993\t  those datoms, which is why grantor-authored facts are worth more than\n   994\t  holder reports;\n   995\t- the **reclaim** is the grantor acting on what it holds: recording the\n   996\t  lapse on its own base and advancing the occurrence's epoch datom. It\n   997\t  needs no cooperation from any holder and no reach into any carrier;\n   998\t- the arbiter is **not medium-relative**: the binding\n   999\t  occurrence → arbitration space is minted at first export and travels *in\n  1000\t  the value* (`:yin.k/arbitration`, §7.2), so every carrier, every snapshot\n  1001\t  variant, and every copy names the same one authority. The same content on\n  1002\t  two media does not create a second arbiter, because the authority is named\n  1003\t  by the occurrence, not discovered from wherever a value happened to land;\n  1004\t- **one holder per occurrence is the grantor's policy, enforced where\n  1005\t  policy lives**: `dao.lease` does not impose it, and UCF does not add to\n  1006\t  the vocabulary — the grantor's ledger admits at most one live lease per\n  1007\t  occurrence (a second grant requires the prior lease reclaimed first,\n  1008\t  which `dao.lease.md`'s own validity rules make the grantor the sole\n  1009\t  author of). Exclusive custody is thus a property of the ledger the\n  1010\t  grantor possesses, not an aspiration over streams it does not.\n  1011\t\n  1012\tWhat is possessed, concretely, is the *right to admit effects as this\n  1013\toccurrence* — the ledger is where epochs and dedup-stamped admissions are\n  1014\trecorded, which is what §7.7.5's fencing consults. A composition that\n  1015\tcannot wire a `dao.space` transactor as authority cannot offer\n  1016\t`:yin.k/exclusive` custody at all; it composes `:yin.k/fork` only. That is\n  1017\tan honest capability limit, not a gap to paper over.\n  1018\t\n  1019\t### 7.7.4 The exporting state: fencing the source before publication\n  1020\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1021,1240p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | nl -ba -v 1021\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1021,1240p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | nl -ba -v 1021\"","aggregated_output":"  1021\tEmitter-as-candidate was the first draft's whole answer to the\n  1022\tsource-wakeup race, and it does not close the publication window. The\n  1023\tlifecycle published first and made the emitter a candidate afterward, but\n  1024\tnothing ever removed the emitter's *local* eligibility: its driver may\n  1025\tstill poll the wait set — and polling a blocked writer **performs the\n  1026\tappend** (the engine, a `:put` entry retries `append!` on every\n  1027\tpoll) — may restore a woken entry from the ready queue, may satisfy a\n  1028\tdirect `:resume`, and may retry a blocked write, all after the value is in\n  1029\tother machines' hands. Delaying register restoration is too late; the\n  1030\tappend is the effect.\n  1031\t\n  1032\tUCF therefore defines an explicit **exporting** transition that precedes\n  1033\tany publication, and the lift driver performs it as one step over the\n  1034\tmachine value:\n  1035\t\n  1036\t1. **Quiesce**: the ready-queue must be empty (§7.6.3).\n  1037\t2. **Detach the wait set**: every wait entry moves from the machine's\n  1038\t   wait-set into the export record. The machine's wait-set is now empty,\n  1039\t   so *there is nothing to poll* — no poll, no append, no effect. The\n  1040\t   entries are not lost: the export record retains them verbatim; they are\n  1041\t   the source of the `:yin.k/pending` variants of §7.4.3.\n  1042\t3. **Neutralize control**: the record's control and K are already absent\n  1043\t   (the machine is parked or blocked); direct `:resume` resolves against\n  1044\t   `:parked`, and the parked records referenced by reachable code travel\n  1045\t   inside the export record — a local `:resume` against them is refused\n  1046\t   while exporting (the restore function observes the exporting machine\n  1047\t   and declines), because the parked id now names a checkpoint that may be\n  1048\t   granted elsewhere.\n  1049\t4. **Publish, then offer**: append segments and the value body; record\n  1050\t   `:yin.k/offered` on the arbitration medium. Only now do readers exist,\n  1051\t   and the source was fenced before any of them did.\n  1052\t\n  1053\tThe exporting machine is pure data with an empty wait-set and a retained\n  1054\texport record — no engine change, no callback, no timer; the drivers of the\n  1055\tworld observe the state as they observe `ready-for-ingress?` (§3.1), and a\n  1056\tdriver that polls an exporting machine's wait set anyway is a host assembly\n  1057\tdefect of the same kind as wiring a refusing deposit destination.\n  1058\t\n  1059\t**Failure recovery and idempotent retry.** If publication fails — a full\n  1060\tcarrier, an unreachable arbitration medium — the machine stays exporting\n  1061\tand the composition retries; a retry re-encodes or reuses the encoded value\n  1062\tand **reuses the occurrence** (§7.3.4), so no retry mints a second runnable\n  1063\tcheckpoint. Abandoning the export reinstates the export record into the\n  1064\tmachine (wait entries return to the wait-set, parked records to `:parked`)\n  1065\tand the task resumes as a purely local one — the sole paths back to local\n  1066\texecution are *abort export* (before the offer is recorded) and *a grant to\n  1067\tthe emitter itself* (after); there is no third.\n  1068\t\n  1069\t### 7.7.5 Fencing effects: epochs, operation ids, and what exactly-once costs\n  1070\t\n  1071\t`dao.lease.md` states it plainly: **the absence of a `:lapsed` fact is\n  1072\tnever evidence of tenure.** The first draft's fencing — stamp effects with\n  1073\tthe lease id, let consumers consult the medium and drop effects whose\n  1074\tincarnation has a `:lapsed` record — fails in every direction the review\n  1075\tnamed: a partitioned consumer accepts stale effects (it cannot see the\n  1076\tlapse); a delayed legitimate effect is discarded after release; repeated\n  1077\teffects within one lease share one incarnation and are indistinguishable; a\n  1078\treplacement holder repeats an effect its predecessor committed; and apply\n  1079\tids correlate requests with responses, they do not suppress duplicates.\n  1080\tLapse-record filtering is correlation, not fencing. What UCF requires\n  1081\tinstead:\n  1082\t\n  1083\t**Authority epochs, checked atomically with commitment.** The occurrence's\n  1084\tledger carries an epoch datom, advanced by the grantor on every reclaim\n  1085\t(§7.7.3) — a monotone number the possessing boundary alone can write. A\n  1086\tconsumer that must be exactly-once (a `dao.space` transactor, an FFI callee\n  1087\twith side effects) admits a fenced effect only in the same act that reads\n  1088\tthe epoch: the check and the commit are one transition against the\n  1089\tadmission resource, never check-then-act. An effect whose incarnation's\n  1090\tepoch is not the current one is refused at commitment. This is\n  1091\t`dao.lease.md`'s \"fencing from the resource\" for durable resources, and it\n  1092\tis a composition duty that composition owes exactly once.\n  1093\t\n  1094\t**Stable operation ids, and durable dedup.** Every fenced effect carries\n  1095\t`:yin.k/op-id {:yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/seq n}` — assigned by the fenced\n  1096\twriter in emission order. Op-ids are deterministic functions of the\n  1097\tcheckpoint and the operation sequence: a replacement holder resuming the\n  1098\t*same* occurrence replays the same op-ids, which is what makes\n  1099\tacross-incarnation dedup possible at all. Exactly-once consumers keep a\n  1100\tdurable `{op-id → committed result}` record, written in the same atomic\n  1101\ttransition as the epoch check; a replayed op-id returns the recorded result\n  1102\tand commits nothing. Within one incarnation and across incarnations, the\n  1103\tsame op-id is the same logical operation — the room for exactly-once\n  1104\tsemantics that lease identity alone cannot provide.\n  1105\t\n  1106\t**Where the stamp rides.** Program values are program data; UCF never\n  1107\tedits them. A resumed incarnation running under a lease emits through a\n  1108\t**fenced writer** the composition wires: a writer that wraps each appended\n  1109\tvalue in an envelope `{:yin.k/envelope … :yin.k/incarnation <lease>\n  1110\t:yin.k/op-id … :yin.k/value <the program value, verbatim>}`. Exactly-once\n  1111\tconsumers read stamped envelopes; a stream wired bare — no fence — is\n  1112\t*declared* unprotected, and a composition that needs exactly-once delivery\n  1113\tthrough it has wired the wrong thing. Fencing is opt-in per stream, exactly\n  1114\tas `dao.lease.md` places the burden: \"a holder needing exclusion obtains it\n  1115\tfrom the resource.\" No callback tells a lapsed holder to stop; it stops at\n  1116\tits own lease bound (`dao.lease.md`, *The holder*), and what it did past\n  1117\tthe bound is distinguishable after the fact by epoch and op-id.\n  1118\t\n  1119\t**Durable completion of a checkpoint.** Publication of a successor is not\n  1120\tcompletion. The holder's exit sequence is: append the successor value to\n  1121\tthe carrier, append `:yin.k/resumed` (evidence) to the arbitration medium,\n  1122\tappend `:dao.lease/released`. Completion is the **grantor's ledger\n  1123\ttransition** — observing the release (or the `:resumed` evidence followed\n  1124\tby release) and closing the occurrence's tenure. The crash windows are then\n  1125\tbounded and harmless: a crash after the successor append but before the\n  1126\trecords leaves an orphan successor — content-addressed, idempotent,\n  1127\tre-readable as evidence but granting nothing; the lease lapses by silence;\n  1128\tthe grantor re-grants the *last recorded* occurrence, and the orphan chain\n  1129\tis queryable history (§7.7.6). A crash mid-sequence never requires the\n  1130\tgrantor to trust a holder's report as proof of durable completion, because\n  1131\tthe report is evidence and the ledger is authority.\n  1132\t\n  1133\t**Partition policy, stated honestly.** A fenced consumer that cannot reach\n  1134\tthe authority (epoch unreadable) must **suspend protected effect\n  1135\tcommitment** — fail closed — or the composition has chosen at-least-once\n  1136\tdelivery with after-the-fact dedup reconciliation; which one is a declared\n  1137\tproperty of the consumer, and a consumer that neither checks nor declares\n  1138\tis not exactly-once and may not claim to be. A holder partitioned from the\n  1139\tarbitration medium runs to its own lease bound and its effects are\n  1140\tafter-the-fact distinguishable, which is the guarantee `dao.lease.md`\n  1141\tactually makes; UCF does not promise more. A grantor that lost its ledger\n  1142\treclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`, *Restart*); **permanent** authority\n  1143\tloss breaks exclusive custody irreparably — the ledger was the resource —\n  1144\tand repair is a governance act outside this protocol. The arbitration\n  1145\tmedium's durability is therefore a composition duty owed before\n  1146\t`:yin.k/exclusive` is offered at all (`dao.lease.md`, *Composition\n  1147\tduties*: a judge whose durability matches the resource).\n  1148\t\n  1149\t### 7.7.6 Successors and chains\n  1150\t\n  1151\tA resumed continuation runs to its next safepoint and either halts or parks\n  1152\tagain. Either way it produces a successor value — a `:yin.k/continuation`\n  1153\tor a `:yin.k/result` — whose `:yin.k/origin` carries the predecessor's\n  1154\t`:yin.k/occurrence` and the lease it ran under. Each park is a **new\n  1155\toccurrence**: minted fresh by the running holder, chained to its\n  1156\tpredecessor, and itself the subject of the next custody cycle. The holder\n  1157\tappends the successor, records `:yin.k/resumed` with the successor's\n  1158\taddress, and releases. The chain of occurrences linked by `:yin.k/origin`\n  1159\tis the migratory computation's history, queryable on the arbitration\n  1160\tmedium: where it ran, under which grant, what it became. A rollback is a\n  1161\tre-offer of an earlier link; effects past that link need compensation, not\n  1162\trecomputation (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.4) — and §7.7.5's op-ids\n  1163\tare what compensation reads to find what was done.\n  1164\t\n  1165\t### 7.7.7 Restart\n  1166\t\n  1167\tA grantor that lost its ledger reclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`,\n  1168\t*Restart*: reclaim first, then grant afresh — a new grant alone does not\n  1169\tend the prior tenure). Its inventory of possessed resources is the set of\n  1170\toccurrence ledgers it transacts; its recoverable holder for each is the\n  1171\t`:dao.lease/holder` of the last grant, both of which are facts on (or the\n  1172\trecoverable state of) the space it possesses. Both are queries, which is\n  1173\twhy custody lives on a `dao.space` and not on the carrier stream.\n  1174\t\n  1175\t## §7.8 Lifecycle: lift on park, lower on resume\n  1176\t\n  1177\t**Lift** (emitter side; composition-driven, never automatic on every park):\n  1178\t\n  1179\t1. The machine is parked or blocked with a local record `r`, and its\n  1180\t   ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n  1181\t2. **Enter exporting** (§7.7.4): detach the wait set into the export\n  1182\t   record, neutralize direct `:resume`. From here the source is fenced; no\n  1183\t   poll, retry, or local resume can perform an effect.\n  1184\t3. `lift(export-record)`: confirm the record is at a safepoint (§7.4.1),\n  1185\t   else `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; canonicalize every referenced segment and\n  1186\t   compute its address (§7.3.2), else the refusal; encode (§7.5), else the\n  1187\t   refusal; compute `:yin.k/requires` and `:yin.k/store` by the fixed point\n  1188\t   (§7.6), recording the discovery status; stamp the contract (§7.3.3); on\n  1189\t   first export, mint the occurrence; mint `:yin.k/id`.\n  1190\t4. Append the canonical instruction vectors of every required segment to a\n  1191\t   `dao.jing` intake pool (the payloads *are* the vectors, §7.3.4), then\n  1192\t   the id-less value body to the carrier medium; observe `:dao.stream/ok`\n  1193\t   on each. `:yin.k/carried` may then be empty. Retry of any failed append\n  1194\t   reuses the occurrence (§7.7.4).\n  1195\t5. Under `:exclusive`, append `:yin.k/offered` to the arbitration medium.\n  1196\t   The export record stays for recovery; the emitter is now a candidate\n  1197\t   like any other, and wakes only on custody (a grant to itself), never on\n  1198\t   data.\n  1199\t\n  1200\t**Lower** (resumer side):\n  1201\t\n  1202\t1. Read the value; check `:yin.k/contract`, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\n  1203\t2. Verify `:yin.k/id` by re-hashing the body, else `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`.\n  1204\t3. Decode against the §7.5.1 grammar with full structural validation, else\n  1205\t   `:yin.k/undecodable` — a correct hash is not structural validation, and\n  1206\t   both checks always run.\n  1207\t4. Evaluate `:yin.k/requires` (§7.6.5); fetch what may be fetched (segments\n  1208\t   by address, verified by re-canonicalization); else\n  1209\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with the discovery status.\n  1210\t5. Under `:exclusive`, attach to the arbitration medium (an attachment\n  1211\t   failure here is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it — the value declares its\n  1212\t   authority, §7.7.3), propose, then observe the grant, else\n  1213\t   `:yin.k/not-holder` with the lease state observed; while a proposal is\n  1214\t   unanswered the driver's step answers `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` — a\n  1215\t   grantor owes no answer and no bound applies (`dao.lease.md`,\n  1216\t   *Authority*).\n  1217\t6. Load every required segment by address into the per-stamp index,\n  1218\t   verifying each (§7.3.4).\n  1219\t7. **Restore into an isolated store** (§7.6.2): the slice, the remapped\n  1220\t   cells, the resumer's FFI pair. Attach to every stream descriptor in the\n  1221\t   frame, pending waits, and cells (§7.4.3); an attachment `not-found` is\n  1222\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream. **Any failure from this step\n  1223\t   onward — after custody was acquired — releases first**: the resumer\n  1224\t   appends `:dao.lease/released`, the occurrence returns to offered, and\n  1225\t   the failure outcome is returned. A resumer that dies without releasing\n  1226\t   is recovered by the lease's own lapse; nothing else is owed.\n  1227\t8. `lower(frame, layout)` into engine registers; re-mint every pending wait\n  1228\t   in the local wait set; seed `:id-counter` per §7.6.3; run — through a\n  1229\t   fenced writer under `:exclusive` (§7.7.5).\n  1230\t9. At the next safepoint or halt, produce the successor occurrence\n  1231\t   (§7.7.6), record `:yin.k/resumed`, release.\n  1232\t\n  1233\tNeither sequence contains a callback. Each is a step function a\n  1234\tcomposition-supplied driver repeats, as for every other interpreter in the\n  1235\tsystem.\n  1236\t\n  1237\t## §7.9 Outcome algebra\n  1238\t\n  1239\tEvery UCF operation returns one map dispatching on `:yin.k/status`; the set\n  1240\tis closed and every non-`ok` outcome carries the data needed to act on it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
