{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,210p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | nl -ba\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# yin.vm.linker: the universal code linker over dao.stream\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design, proposed (2026-09-24). Supersedes the namespace placement\n     4\tand the two-backend scope of `yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` (Phase B6); that\n     5\tdocument's fetch pipeline, format records, refusal vocabulary, and\n     6\tsame-root pairing are carried here unchanged in substance and extended to\n     7\tall four `yin.vm` execution backends. Subordinate to `datom.world.md`,\n     8\t`dao.stream.md`, and `dao.jing.md`. Builds on the code identity rules of\n     9\t`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` (UCF) section 7.3 and the\n    10\taddressing rules of `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 4.\n    11\t\n    12\tRelated documents:\n    13\t\n    14\t- `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` - the B6 predecessor (two\n    15\t  formats, one synchronous fetch)\n    16\t- `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` - governing decisions D5 to D16\n    17\t- `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` - AST rows and segment vectors as\n    18\t  content\n    19\t- `docs/design/yin.vm.dependency-completion.md` - the work-item fixed\n    20\t  point that computes a program's requirements\n    21\t- `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` - code identity,\n    22\t  contract stamp, safepoints, pending waits\n    23\t- `docs/design/dao.jing.md` - content-addressed storage and the stepped\n    24\t  remote client\n    25\t- `docs/design/dao.stream.md` - the stream contract, in particular OD-5\n    26\t\n    27\t## 1. Objective and architectural position\n    28\t\n    29\t`yin.vm` has four execution backends, all reachable from `yin.repl`\n    30\t(`src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc`, `vm-constructors`):\n    31\t\n    32\t| Backend       | Kernel                    | Code shape                   |\n    33\t|---------------|---------------------------|------------------------------|\n    34\t| `:ast-walker` | `yin.vm.ast-walker`       | universal AST row set        |\n    35\t| `:semantic`   | `yin.vm.semantic`         | positional instruction vector|\n    36\t| `:stack`      | `yin.vm.debruijn.stack`   | de Bruijn stack image (H)    |\n    37\t| `:register`   | `yin.vm.debruijn.register`| de Bruijn register image (R) |\n    38\t\n    39\tEach backend needs the same service: given an identity for some code, and\n    40\tnothing else, obtain the code, prove it is the code the identity names,\n    41\tprove it is well formed, prove the receiver can bind its free names\n    42\tidentically to the publisher, and hand it over for loading. Today that\n    43\tservice exists once, for two of the four backends, under a name that says\n    44\tit belongs to one of them: `yin.vm.debruijn-linker`.\n    45\t\n    46\tThis document makes two corrections and one unification.\n    47\t\n    48\t**Correction 1: the linker is universal.** The B6 fetch function has no\n    49\tde Bruijn branch in it. It is parameterized by a format record and runs\n    50\tthe same six steps for a stack image and a register image. The namespace\n    51\t`yin.vm.debruijn-linker` is therefore a misnomer that invites a second\n    52\tlinker for the semantic VM and a third for the walker. There is one\n    53\tlinker, `yin.vm.linker`, and every backend is a format record it takes.\n    54\t\n    55\t**Correction 2: local and remote linking are one mechanism.** B6 already\n    56\tstates this (`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` section 7.2: \"the boundary is\n    57\t`dao.stream`; local and remote resolution are the same transport-neutral\n    58\tmechanism\"). But `fetch` today reads through a synchronous `dao.jing`\n    59\thandle, and a local composition hands it an in-memory store whose `get` is\n    60\ta map lookup. That is a function call across what the axioms say is a\n    61\tstream boundary. This document makes the request and response a stream\n    62\texchange in every composition; what varies is the transport underneath\n    63\t(ring buffer, file, WebSocket, DHT), never the linker's shape.\n    64\t\n    65\t**Unification: `require` is linking.** Module loading in `yin.vm.module`\n    66\tresolves `(require 'foo)` against a dictionary the composition built by\n    67\thand at wiring time. Linking resolves an identity to content over a stream.\n    68\tThese are the same operation at two grains: a module name is an entry in a\n    69\tname environment whose value is a manifest address, and a manifest names\n    70\tthe images the linker fetches. `(require 'foo)` lowers to an effect that\n    71\tparks the VM and emits a link request; the module arrives as verified\n    72\tcontent on a response stream. The dictionary becomes a manifest registry:\n    73\ta value naming content addresses, never holding code.\n    74\t\n    75\t### 1.1 What this document adds over B6\n    76\t\n    77\t1. The namespace `yin.vm.linker` and its migration from\n    78\t   `yin.vm.debruijn-linker` (section 9).\n    79\t2. Two new format records, `:yin.ast/code` and `:yin.semantic/code`, beside\n    80\t   the two B6 records (section 5).\n    81\t3. A `:parts-fn` slot in the format record so that a multi-row payload\n    82\t   (an AST tree) fetches through the same pipeline as a single vector\n    83\t   (section 4.2, step 2).\n    84\t4. The stepped link interface (`link-state`, `request-link`, `step`,\n    85\t   `abandon`) as the portable shape, with the synchronous `fetch`\n    86\t   reclassified as host policy (section 6).\n    87\t5. The `:module/require` effect lowered onto the linker: park, request,\n    88\t   response, install (section 7).\n    89\t6. The module manifest and the manifest registry that replaces the\n    90\t   in-memory module dictionary (section 8).\n    91\t7. Three additional refusals: `:unsupported-format`, `:contract-mismatch`,\n    92\t   `:export-missing` (section 4.3).\n    93\t\n    94\t### 1.2 What this document does not add\n    95\t\n    96\t- No transport. `dao.stream` and its transports are the only medium.\n    97\t- No peer routing, caching, or responder. `dao.jing.remote` serves\n    98\t  content; `dao.jing.dht` finds it.\n    99\t- No global registry, loader, or callback. Every handle, index, stream,\n   100\t  and format record is an explicit argument or a value in VM state.\n   101\t- No execution. The linker fetches and verifies. Loading an image into a\n   102\t  kernel and running a module body belong to the VM.\n   103\t- No change to `image-hash`, `register-hash`, `segment-key`, the opcode\n   104\t  tables, or any kernel's semantics.\n   105\t\n   106\t## 2. Governing invariants\n   107\t\n   108\tEvery rule below derives from `datom.world.md`. The derivations are named\n   109\tonce here so later sections can cite them.\n   110\t\n   111\t**I1. Code at rest is content in `dao.jing`.** An image, a row, a vector,\n   112\ta manifest: each is an opaque value at its `segment-key`. `dao.jing`\n   113\tassigns identity and retrieval and nothing else (`dao.jing.md`,\n   114\t*Definition*). The linker never asks the store what a value means.\n   115\t\n   116\t**I2. Code in motion is a stream in `dao.stream`.** A request for code, a\n   117\tpayload answering it, a refusal, an absence, a timeout: each is a value\n   118\tat a position in an append-only sequence. No function call crosses a host\n   119\tboundary (`datom.world.md`, *Host Boundaries*).\n   120\t\n   121\t**I3. Location is not identity.** Whether the content is in this process,\n   122\tin a file, behind a socket, or on a DHT peer changes how bytes are\n   123\tobtained, not what the linker checks (`dao.jing.md`, *Reads*). A local\n   124\tcomposition and a remote one differ in the creation specification of the\n   125\tstreams they wire, and in nothing the linker can observe.\n   126\t\n   127\t**I4. No operation waits.** The portable linker interface is a step that\n   128\treturns what is true now, including \"not yet\" (`dao.stream.md`, OD-5). A\n   129\twaiting `fetch` may wrap the step as host policy on hosts that can afford\n   130\tit and is never the interface another interpreter is written against.\n   131\t\n   132\t**I5. Verification is address-directed and identity-directed, and both\n   133\trun.** The storage address and the format identity have different\n   134\tpreimages for two of the four formats and the same preimage for the other\n   135\ttwo; in every case both checks execute, because an index entry is a claim\n   136\tand never a proof (`yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` section 6; D14).\n   137\t\n   138\t**I6. The VM never invokes a loader.** Absence of code is a park plus a\n   139\trequest emission; the loaded image arrives as a value in VM state\n   140\t(`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` section 7.2). The linker is an observer on a\n   141\trequest stream, not a function the kernel calls.\n   142\t\n   143\t**I7. The registry is a value.** `yin.vm.module` already keeps the\n   144\tregistry as a map in VM state. This document keeps that and changes what\n   145\tthe map holds: content addresses and manifests, never code.\n   146\t\n   147\t## 3. Identities: three kinds, one address space\n   148\t\n   149\tThe linker moves between three kinds of name. Confusing them was the\n   150\tdefect B6 guarded against with D9, and the unification here adds a third.\n   151\t\n   152\t| Kind             | Example                              | Owner            |\n   153\t|------------------|--------------------------------------|------------------|\n   154\t| storage address  | `:segment/blake3-...`                | `dao.jing`       |\n   155\t| format identity  | H, R, a vector address, a root row id| the format       |\n   156\t| module name      | `'my.lib`                            | name environment |\n   157\t\n   158\t**Storage address.** `(jing/segment-key payload)` under the storage\n   159\tencoder in force. Changes when the encoder changes (the CBOR landing,\n   160\t`dao.jing.md` *Open items*). Carries its own hash algorithm.\n   161\t\n   162\t**Format identity.** What the VM contract calls the code. There are two\n   163\tfamilies, and the linker treats them differently at one point only,\n   164\tsection 4.2 step 3:\n   165\t\n   166\t- *Contract-pinned identities* (H, R): computed by the format's own hash\n   167\t  function over a descriptor that embeds the lowering contract version,\n   168\t  pinned to SHA-256 by contract freeze, independent of the storage\n   169\t  encoder. Their preimage differs from the storage address's preimage.\n   170\t- *Storage-derived identities* (`:yin.ast/code`, `:yin.semantic/code`):\n   171\t  UCF section 7.3.2 defines a segment's `:yin.code/hash` as the\n   172\t  `segment-key` of its canonical instruction vector, and\n   173\t  `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 4.1 defines a tree's address as its\n   174\t  root row's id, itself a `segment-key`. Here the identity's preimage is\n   175\t  the payload, so the identity equals the storage address of the payload\n   176\t  today. When the storage encoder changes, these identities change with\n   177\t  it. UCF accepts that (\"UCF must not mint a second addressing scheme\");\n   178\t  this document inherits it and does not repair it.\n   179\t\n   180\t**Module name.** A symbol resolved through a name environment to a\n   181\tmanifest address (section 8). A name is a claim of the composition that\n   182\tpublished it; the manifest it resolves to is content and is verified like\n   183\tany other.\n   184\t\n   185\tThe relation among them, for one module and one backend:\n   186\t\n   187\t```text\n   188\tname 'my.lib\n   189\t  --(name environment)-->  manifest address  :segment/...\n   190\t  --(fetch, verify)-->     manifest value\n   191\t  --(:yin.module/images)-> format identity   H | R | vector addr | root id\n   192\t  --(format index)-->      storage address   :segment/...\n   193\t  --(fetch, verify)-->     image value\n   194\t```\n   195\t\n   196\tTwo indexes appear: the name environment (name to manifest address) and\n   197\tthe format index (identity to storage address). For the storage-derived\n   198\tformats the second index is the identity function and may be omitted; the\n   199\tlinker still runs step 3 against it.\n   200\t\n   201\t## 4. The fetch pipeline\n   202\t\n   203\t### 4.1 The format record\n   204\t\n   205\tA format record is a plain map. B6 fixed three function slots; this\n   206\tdocument adds one and a contract slot:\n   207\t\n   208\t```clojure\n   209\t{:format        :yin.debruijn.code          ; the dispatch key\n   210\t :contract      \"b1\"                        ; the contract revision name\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
