{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '211,420p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | nl -ba -v 211\"","aggregated_output":"   211\t :hash-fn       (fn [value] identity)       ; format identity of a payload\n   212\t :validate-fn   (fn [value] defect-or-nil)  ; structural + liveness rules\n   213\t :free-names-fn (fn [value] [sym ...])      ; names the receiver must bind\n   214\t :parts-fn      (fn [value] [address ...])} ; addresses the payload needs\n   215\t```\n   216\t\n   217\t`:parts-fn` returns the storage addresses a fetched payload references and\n   218\twithout which it is incomplete. For the three single-payload formats it\n   219\treturns nothing. For `:yin.ast/code` it returns the child row ids of a\n   220\trow body, which is how one pipeline fetches a whole tree (step 2 below).\n   221\t\n   222\t`fetch` contains no branch on `:format`. Adding a fifth backend is one\n   223\tmore record.\n   224\t\n   225\t### 4.2 The six steps\n   226\t\n   227\tThe order is B6's, unchanged, and remains load-bearing for the reasons\n   228\tB6 gives. What changes is that step 2 is a worklist and that steps 1 and\n   229\t2 are stream exchanges (section 6).\n   230\t\n   231\t```text\n   232\t(link handle index format identity receiver)\n   233\t\n   234\t1. address    <- (index identity)\n   235\t                 absent entry                       -> :absent\n   236\t2. payload    <- fetch address over dao.stream\n   237\t                 absent payload                     -> :absent\n   238\t                 (not (segment-matches? address v)) -> :address-mismatch\n   239\t                 for each a in ((:parts-fn format) v):\n   240\t                   fetch a the same way; repeat until no new address\n   241\t                 (an absent or mismatched part refuses, naming it)\n   242\t3.               (not= identity ((:hash-fn format) root-value))\n   243\t                                                    -> :hash-mismatch\n   244\t4.               ((:validate-fn format) value) returns a defect\n   245\t                                                    -> :descriptor-defect\n   246\t                 the format's contract disagrees with the request's\n   247\t                                                    -> :contract-mismatch\n   248\t5.               ((:free-names-fn format) value) against receiver\n   249\t                 unresolved name                    -> :unresolved-free\n   250\t                 name shadowed by free env or store -> :shadowed-free\n   251\t6. return the verified image as data\n   252\t```\n   253\t\n   254\tStep by step, for all four formats:\n   255\t\n   256\t1. **Resolve identity to storage address.** The index is composition\n   257\t   data: a map, a function, or datoms `[identity attribute address]`\n   258\t   read through `index-from-datoms`. For the storage-derived formats the\n   259\t   composition may pass `identity` as the index; the linker does not\n   260\t   special-case this.\n   261\t2. **Fetch payload over `dao.stream`.** Each address is a\n   262\t   `:jing/get-content` request on the link request stream and a\n   263\t   correlated response on the link response stream (section 6). The\n   264\t   value is verified against the address it was requested at, using the\n   265\t   algorithm the address carries (`jing/segment-matches?`, a Class 3\n   266\t   validation site under `dao.jing.call-site-classification.md`). Then\n   267\t   `:parts-fn` names further addresses; each is fetched and verified the\n   268\t   same way; the worklist is breadth-first from the root and visits\n   269\t   each address once. The result of step 2 is the root value plus, for a\n   270\t   multi-part format, the map of every fetched part by address.\n   271\t3. **Verify format identity.** `(:hash-fn format)` over the root value\n   272\t   must equal the requested identity. For H and R this is a different\n   273\t   hash under a different preimage from step 2 and catches a stale or\n   274\t   swapped index entry, a descriptor disagreement, or a contract version\n   275\t   disagreement (all `:hash-mismatch`, as in B6). For the storage-derived\n   276\t   formats it is a second `segment-key` over the same bytes; it still\n   277\t   runs, because it is the only check that the *index* did not point the\n   278\t   identity at a different, correctly stored payload.\n   279\t4. **Validate structural and liveness invariants.** The format's\n   280\t   validator runs over the complete value: for a tree, over the\n   281\t   assembled row set; for a vector, over the vector; for a register\n   282\t   image, including the ascending, bounded, exact live-set rules. A\n   283\t   validator that throws is `:descriptor-defect` with rule\n   284\t   `:validator-refused`. If the request named a contract revision and the\n   285\t   format record's `:contract` differs, the refusal is\n   286\t   `:contract-mismatch`; this is UCF section 7.3.3's\n   287\t   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` at the linker's grain, raised before any\n   288\t   grammar check that would be run under the wrong table.\n   289\t5. **Verify free-name closure against the receiver.** The receiver is\n   290\t   `{:free-env :store :primitives :modules}`, every key optional. A name\n   291\t   bound in the receiver's free env or store is `:shadowed-free`; a name\n   292\t   that resolves through neither primitives nor the module registry is\n   293\t   `:unresolved-free`. Resolution order is `resolve-var`'s: env, store,\n   294\t   primitives, modules. D11 requires refusal, not execution, for an image\n   295\t   the receiver cannot bind identically. What counts as a free name is\n   296\t   the format's business (section 5).\n   297\t6. **Return the verified image.** `{:status :ok :format f :identity i\n   298\t   :address a :value v}` plus `:parts {address value}` for a multi-part\n   299\t   format. Nothing partially verified is ever returned.\n   300\t\n   301\t### 4.3 Refusal vocabulary\n   302\t\n   303\tEvery refusal is `{:status :refused :reason r}` merged with the step's own\n   304\tevidence, exactly as `yin.vm.linker/refused` builds it.\n   305\t\n   306\t| Reason                | Step   | Meaning                                    |\n   307\t|-----------------------|--------|--------------------------------------------|\n   308\t| `:absent`             | 1 or 2 | no index entry, or no payload at the       |\n   309\t|                       |        | address (root or any part)                 |\n   310\t| `:address-mismatch`   | 2      | payload does not hash to its address       |\n   311\t| `:hash-mismatch`      | 3      | root value does not hash to the identity   |\n   312\t| `:descriptor-defect`  | 4      | structural or liveness validation failed   |\n   313\t| `:contract-mismatch`  | 4      | request names a contract the format record |\n   314\t|                       |        | does not implement                         |\n   315\t| `:unresolved-free`    | 5      | a free name binds nowhere in the receiver  |\n   316\t| `:shadowed-free`      | 5      | a free name is bound by the receiver's     |\n   317\t|                       |        | free env or store                          |\n   318\t| `:unsupported-format` | before | the receiver holds no kernel or format     |\n   319\t|                       | 1      | record for the requested format            |\n   320\t| `:export-missing`     | after  | a module manifest names an export the      |\n   321\t|                       | 6      | evaluated module did not bind (section 8)  |\n   322\t| `:pairing-mismatch`   | verify | re-lowered H and R disagree with the       |\n   323\t|                       |        | claimed root pairing (B6 section 7)        |\n   324\t\n   325\t`:unsupported-format` is the linker's instance of the host-boundary rule\n   326\tthat a host with no implementation for an effect emits a qualified\n   327\tunsupported result, \"a correct outcome, not a gap to be filled\"\n   328\t(`datom.world.md`). It lets a receiver decline an R request and, where\n   329\tthe composition permits, fall back to H by same-root pairing.\n   330\t\n   331\tA stepped link additionally reports `:pending` (section 6.2), which is\n   332\tnot a refusal: it is the \"not yet\" of a non-blocking read.\n   333\t\n   334\t## 5. Format records for the four backends\n   335\t\n   336\tThe four records, with the functions they bind. Three are existing code;\n   337\tthe AST record's free-name scanner is the section 4.5 query of\n   338\t`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`, already implemented as `yin.vm/free-names`.\n   339\t\n   340\t### 5.1 `:yin.ast/code` (the AST walker)\n   341\t\n   342\t```clojure\n   343\t{:format        :yin.ast/code\n   344\t :contract      \"v2\"\n   345\t :hash-fn       (fn [root-row] (jing/segment-key (subvec root-row 1)))\n   346\t :validate-fn   (fn [tree] (yin.vm/validate-rows tree))\n   347\t :free-names-fn (fn [tree] (yin.vm/free-names (relation tree)\n   348\t                                              (occurrences tree)\n   349\t                                              (:root tree)))\n   350\t :parts-fn      (fn [row] (body-child-ids row))}\n   351\t```\n   352\t\n   353\tPayload: one row body `[tag & slots]` per address, exactly as\n   354\t`yin.vm.content/materialize-tree!` stores it (D3: individual rows, no pack\n   355\tformat). The identity is the root row's id. Step 2's worklist follows\n   356\t`node` and `nodes` slots by the grammar's positions, the walk\n   357\t`yin.vm.content/load-rows` performs today; that function's throwing\n   358\tsurface is retired in favor of the linker's refusals (section 9). The\n   359\tassembled value handed to steps 3 to 6 is `{:root id :rows {id row}}`.\n   360\tFree names are the conservative, occurrence-scoped set: a `:variable`\n   361\tname is an obligation unless an enclosing `:lambda` binds it on that\n   362\toccurrence's own path; a name bound at one occurrence and free at another\n   363\tis included. Loading is `yin.vm.ast-walker/vm-load-rows`.\n   364\t\n   365\t### 5.2 `:yin.semantic/code` (the semantic VM)\n   366\t\n   367\t```clojure\n   368\t{:format        :yin.semantic/code\n   369\t :contract      \"v2\"\n   370\t :hash-fn       jing/segment-key\n   371\t :validate-fn   yin.vm.code/well-formed-vector?\n   372\t :free-names-fn semantic-free-names\n   373\t :parts-fn      (constantly nil)}\n   374\t```\n   375\t\n   376\tPayload: the canonical positional instruction tuple vector (UCF section\n   377\t7.3.2), stored as the exact vector at its own `segment-key`; nothing\n   378\telse may hash there (UCF section 7.3.4). The identity is that address.\n   379\t`well-formed-vector?` is the section 7.5 grammar of\n   380\t`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`: mnemonic set, arity, operand kinds,\n   381\tsaturation, target bounds, terminator, argc. `semantic-free-names` scans\n   382\t`:var` operands not bound by an enclosing closure's params, the `$code`\n   383\tquery of section 7.7; the linker module defines it, as B6 defined the\n   384\ttwo de Bruijn scanners, so no existing file changes. The contract stamp\n   385\tis UCF's `{:yin.code/contract \"v2\"}`; a link request for this format\n   386\tcarries the stamp, and the receiver's index is keyed per stamp (UCF\n   387\tsection 7.3.3). Loading is `yin.vm.semantic/load-vector`, which writes\n   388\tthe address to the image and the alias column.\n   389\t\n   390\t### 5.3 `:yin.debruijn.code` (the stack VM)\n   391\t\n   392\t```clojure\n   393\t{:format        :yin.debruijn.code\n   394\t :contract      \"b1\"\n   395\t :hash-fn       yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-hash\n   396\t :validate-fn   yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-defect\n   397\t :free-names-fn stack-free-names\n   398\t :parts-fn      (constantly nil)}\n   399\t```\n   400\t\n   401\tUnchanged from B6 section 5.1. The identity H is `image-hash` over the\n   402\tdescriptor hash (which embeds the lowering contract version) and the\n   403\tcanonical vector. Free names are `:load-free` operands. Loading is\n   404\t`yin.vm.debruijn.stack/load-image`; `yin.repl`'s `append-stack-image`\n   405\tshows the relocation a second image needs beside a held one.\n   406\t\n   407\t### 5.4 `:yin.debruijn.register` (the register VM)\n   408\t\n   409\t```clojure\n   410\t{:format        :yin.debruijn.register\n   411\t :contract      \"r1\"\n   412\t :hash-fn       yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-hash\n   413\t :validate-fn   yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-image-defect\n   414\t :free-names-fn register-free-names\n   415\t :parts-fn      (constantly nil)}\n   416\t```\n   417\t\n   418\tUnchanged from B6 section 5.2. The payload is the register image map\n   419\t`{:bodies ... :instructions ...}`; validation includes the live-set\n   420\trules; free names are `[:load-free rd name]` operands across all bodies.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '421,630p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | nl -ba -v 421\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '421,630p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | nl -ba -v 421\"","aggregated_output":"   421\tLoading is `yin.vm.debruijn.register/load-image`.\n   422\t\n   423\t### 5.5 Same-root pairing\n   424\t\n   425\tB6 section 7 stands: a root's H and R are recorded as pairing datoms at\n   426\tmint time, and a receiver without a register kernel may fall back from R\n   427\tto H on composition trust or after re-lowering the named datoms. With\n   428\tfour formats the pairing generalizes to a per-root record of every\n   429\tidentity minted from the same tree, which is exactly what a module\n   430\tmanifest's `:yin.module/images` holds (section 8.1). `trusted-fallback`\n   431\tand `verifying-fallback` keep their B6 shapes and read the pair from the\n   432\tmanifest where one exists.\n   433\t\n   434\t## 6. Linking is a stream exchange\n   435\t\n   436\t### 6.1 The topology\n   437\t\n   438\t```mermaid\n   439\tflowchart LR\n   440\t  subgraph VM[\"yin.vm kernel (any backend)\"]\n   441\t    K[park on :link wait entry]\n   442\t  end\n   443\t  subgraph LNK[\"yin.vm.linker interpreter\"]\n   444\t    L[link-state / step]\n   445\t  end\n   446\t  subgraph JING[\"dao.jing content server\"]\n   447\t    J[serve-content!]\n   448\t  end\n   449\t  K -- \"link request stream\" --> L\n   450\t  L -- \"link response stream\" --> K\n   451\t  L -- \"content request stream\" --> J\n   452\t  J -- \"content response stream\" --> L\n   453\t```\n   454\t\n   455\tFour streams, two pairs. The VM and the linker share the link pair; the\n   456\tlinker and the content server share the content pair. Each pair is\n   457\twired by the composition, and its transport is the composition's choice:\n   458\t\n   459\t| Composition           | Link pair            | Content pair             |\n   460\t|-----------------------|----------------------|--------------------------|\n   461\t| single process, tests | ring buffer          | ring buffer              |\n   462\t| durable local         | ring buffer          | memory-log or file       |\n   463\t| remote content        | ring buffer          | `dao.stream.ws` + RPC    |\n   464\t| peer network          | ring buffer          | DHT (`dao.jing.dht`)     |\n   465\t\n   466\tNothing in the linker or the VM changes across rows. This is I3 made\n   467\toperational: a \"local\" require and a \"remote\" require are the same four\n   468\tappends and four reads.\n   469\t\n   470\tThe content pair is `dao.jing.remote`'s existing wire vocabulary:\n   471\t`:jing/get-content` answering `{:found? boolean :value v}` over\n   472\t`dao.stream.rpc`. The linker writes no protocol of its own for content.\n   473\t\n   474\t### 6.2 The stepped interface\n   475\t\n   476\tFollowing `dao.stream.md` OD-5 and the shape `dao.jing.remote.step`\n   477\talready has, the portable linker is a pure function over explicit state.\n   478\t\n   479\t```clojure\n   480\t(link-state rpc-state)          ; -> state; no socket, atom, or scheduler\n   481\t(request-link state request)    ; -> [state link-id]\n   482\t(step state budget)             ; -> {:state s :completions [...]\n   483\t                                ;     :diagnostics [...]}\n   484\t(abandon state link-id reason)  ; -> state\n   485\t```\n   486\t\n   487\tA `request` is plain data:\n   488\t\n   489\t```clojure\n   490\t{:yin.link/format   :yin.debruijn.code\n   491\t :yin.link/identity H\n   492\t :yin.link/contract \"b1\"            ; optional; checked at step 4\n   493\t :yin.link/index    index-or-nil    ; composition data, or absent when the\n   494\t                                    ;   identity is its own address\n   495\t :yin.link/receiver {...}}          ; the step-5 environment\n   496\t```\n   497\t\n   498\t`step` advances every in-flight link in a fixed order: re-attempt\n   499\tunsent content requests, poll the content response medium at most\n   500\t`budget` elements, route each correlated response to its link, run the\n   501\tverification step that response enables, issue the next content request\n   502\tthe worklist needs (a part, under `:parts-fn`), and take completions.\n   503\tEach link is a small state machine over the six steps; steps 3 to 6\n   504\tnever touch a stream and run the moment step 2 completes.\n   505\t\n   506\tA completion is the step-6 result or a refusal, tagged with its link id:\n   507\t\n   508\t```clojure\n   509\t{:yin.link/id 7 :status :ok ...}\n   510\t{:yin.link/id 7 :status :refused :reason :absent :address a}\n   511\t```\n   512\t\n   513\tA link with no completion yet is `:pending`; the caller steps again\n   514\twhen it chooses. Retry cadence, deadlines, and permanent-absence policy\n   515\tare the composition's, as D6 states: streams report, they do not decide.\n   516\t\n   517\t`abandon` retires a link's bookkeeping and completes it `:lost` with the\n   518\tgiven reason, so a caller that gives up still receives exactly one\n   519\tcompletion and never reuses the id.\n   520\t\n   521\t### 6.3 `fetch` is host policy\n   522\t\n   523\tThe B6 signature survives:\n   524\t\n   525\t```clojure\n   526\t(fetch handle index format identity)\n   527\t(fetch handle index format identity receiver)\n   528\t```\n   529\t\n   530\tIt is reclassified as what it is: a blocking driver over the stepped\n   531\tcore, valid where a host can wait and the content handle answers at\n   532\tonce (an in-memory store, a file store, the JVM `connect-content!`\n   533\tclient). Its implementation becomes `request-link`, a loop of `step`\n   534\tuntil the one link completes, and the completion's body. `verify`, the\n   535\tpure steps 3 to 6 over values already in hand, is exported so tests and\n   536\tcompositions holding a payload can run the checks without a stream.\n   537\t\n   538\tNo new interpreter may be written against `fetch`. The VM path of\n   539\tsection 7 is written against `step`.\n   540\t\n   541\t## 7. `(require 'foo)` lowers to the linker\n   542\t\n   543\t### 7.1 Today\n   544\t\n   545\t`yin.vm/primitives` binds `require` to an effectful primitive that\n   546\treturns `{:effect :module/require :module ns-sym}`.\n   547\t`yin.vm.engine/handle-effect` dispatches it to the registry's effect\n   548\thandler, and `yin.vm.module/require-handler` answers from\n   549\t`(:modules state)` alone: present, the module name; absent, a thrown\n   550\t`ex-info`. The registry was populated by `register-module` at wiring\n   551\ttime with host functions. This is the \"static ad-hoc in-memory\n   552\tdictionary lookup\" the critique names. Its two defects: a module that is\n   553\tnot in the dictionary cannot be obtained at all, and the dictionary holds\n   554\tcode (host functions) rather than naming it.\n   555\t\n   556\t### 7.2 The unified flow\n   557\t\n   558\t```mermaid\n   559\tsequenceDiagram\n   560\t  participant P as program\n   561\t  participant V as VM kernel\n   562\t  participant L as yin.vm.linker\n   563\t  participant J as dao.jing (over dao.stream)\n   564\t  P->>V: (require 'foo)\n   565\t  V->>V: :module/require effect\n   566\t  alt manifest already linked in registry value\n   567\t    V-->>P: 'foo\n   568\t  else not linked\n   569\t    V->>V: park; wait entry {:reason :link}\n   570\t    V->>L: append link request {name 'foo, format f}\n   571\t    L->>L: name env: 'foo -> manifest address\n   572\t    L->>J: get-content manifest address\n   573\t    J-->>L: manifest value (verified)\n   574\t    L->>J: get-content image(s) for format f\n   575\t    J-->>L: image value(s) (verified, 6 steps)\n   576\t    L->>V: append link response {:ok image, manifest}\n   577\t    V->>V: wake; load image; evaluate module body\n   578\t    V->>V: registry value += {'foo manifest, exports}\n   579\t    V-->>P: 'foo\n   580\t  end\n   581\t```\n   582\t\n   583\tThe steps, as rules:\n   584\t\n   585\t1. **The effect is unchanged.** `require` still returns\n   586\t   `{:effect :module/require :module 'foo}`. Nothing upstream of the\n   587\t   engine learns anything.\n   588\t2. **The handler checks the registry value first.** A manifest already\n   589\t   linked in `(:modules state)` answers now, as `next` answers `ok` when\n   590\t   the value is there. This is not the dictionary lookup surviving: the\n   591\t   registry holds manifests and export bindings produced by an earlier\n   592\t   link, and every entry got there by the flow below or by the\n   593\t   composition's host-module declaration (section 8.3).\n   594\t3. **A miss parks.** The handler returns `{:park true ...}` and the\n   595\t   engine's existing `handle-stream-block` path adds a wait entry:\n   596\t\n   597\t   ```clojure\n   598\t   {:reason   :link\n   599\t    :link-id  7                     ; the request's correlation id\n   600\t    :name     'foo\n   601\t    :format   :yin.debruijn.code    ; this kernel's format\n   602\t    :cursor   <kept response cursor>}\n   603\t   ```\n   604\t\n   605\t   The entry is registers and resource ids only, as every wait entry is;\n   606\t   no handle and no closure is attached. It is a safepoint kind that UCF\n   607\t   section 7.4.1's table must gain (`:yin.k/reason :link`, resume pc\n   608\t   `pc+1`, accumulator the module name), and a pending variant for UCF\n   609\t   section 7.4.3 carrying the link id, the name, the format, and the\n   610\t   response cell at its kept position. Ratifying those two rows is an\n   611\t   amendment to UCF, listed in section 11.\n   612\t4. **The request is appended** to the link request stream by the\n   613\t   handler, through the writer surface the composition supplied in\n   614\t   `opts`, exactly as `:stream/put` appends through a stored handle. A\n   615\t   `full` outcome retains the request on the wait entry and retries on\n   616\t   the next poll, the `:ffi-request` discipline.\n   617\t5. **The linker interpreter observes the request stream** with its own\n   618\t   cursor, in its own control flow. It resolves the name through the\n   619\t   name environment it was composed with (section 8.2), fetches the\n   620\t   manifest by address (a `:yin.module/manifest` format record, section\n   621\t   8.1, so the manifest itself passes steps 2 to 4), chooses the\n   622\t   identity under `:yin.module/images` for the requested format, and\n   623\t   runs the six steps for that identity through the stepped core. A\n   624\t   manifest with no image for the requested format is\n   625\t   `:unsupported-format`, and the composition's fallback policy (section\n   626\t   5.5) applies before a refusal is appended.\n   627\t6. **The response is appended** to the link response stream:\n   628\t   `{:yin.link/id 7 :status :ok :manifest m :image {...}}` or the\n   629\t   refusal. The linker holds nothing afterwards.\n   630\t7. **`check-wait-set` polls the kept cursor** for the `:link` entry as\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
