{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '631,840p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | nl -ba -v 631\"","aggregated_output":"   631\t   it polls a `:next` entry. On `:ok` the scheduler dispatches\n   632\t   restoration: the kernel's loader installs the image (section 7.3),\n   633\t   the module body is evaluated, the registry value gains the manifest\n   634\t   and the export bindings, and the program resumes at `pc+1` with the\n   635\t   module name as its value. On a refusal the program resumes with the\n   636\t   refusal raised as the effect's error, the same way an `:ffi` `error`\n   637\t   raises. On `gap` the honest outcome is a `gap`, and the composition's\n   638\t   policy decides.\n   639\t\n   640\tSteps 3 to 7 require no new engine machinery: park entries, kept\n   641\tcursors, `handle-stream-block`, `check-wait-set`, and restoration by\n   642\tthe scheduler already exist for streams and FFI. The `:link` entry is\n   643\tone more reason those paths carry.\n   644\t\n   645\t### 7.3 Installing a module\n   646\t\n   647\tThe linker delivers a verified image. Installing it is the kernel's, and\n   648\teach backend already has the loader (`yin.repl`'s `program-loaders`\n   649\tnames all four). Two rules make installation the same for every backend:\n   650\t\n   651\t**Evaluate in an isolated store.** A module body is a program; its\n   652\tdefinitions are `yin/def` effects that write the store. The kernel runs\n   653\tthe image to halt in a fresh store containing nothing of the requiring\n   654\tprogram's state (UCF section 7.6.2's isolated execution store, applied\n   655\tto loading). The resulting store slice is the module's bindings. The\n   656\tmanifest's `:yin.module/exports` names which keys must be present; a\n   657\tmissing one is `:export-missing`, reported as the require's error, and\n   658\tnothing from the slice is installed.\n   659\t\n   660\t**Install is a registry update, not a code copy.** The registry value\n   661\tgains `{'foo {:manifest m :address a :bindings slice}}`. `resolve-var`'s\n   662\tmodule step reads `:bindings`. The image itself stays where the kernel\n   663\tloaded it (the semantic VM's per-stamp address index, the stack VM's\n   664\theld segment); the registry names it and does not duplicate it.\n   665\t\n   666\tThe alternative, per-format export locators in the manifest (a body pc\n   667\tfor stack images, a segment and pc for semantic ones), was considered\n   668\tand rejected: it persists four descriptions of a fact that one\n   669\tevaluation derives, and it would make the manifest's shape depend on\n   670\twhich backends exist. This is *derive, don't persist* at the module\n   671\tgrain.\n   672\t\n   673\t### 7.4 Transitive requires\n   674\t\n   675\tA module body may itself `(require 'bar)`. Under the flow above that is\n   676\ta nested park inside the isolated evaluation, and it works with no\n   677\tfurther rule. The linker may also resolve `:yin.module/requires` ahead\n   678\tof time from the manifest and deliver every image of the closure in one\n   679\tresponse, ordered so that dependencies precede dependents and a\n   680\tstrongly connected component arrives as one unit. This is Phase B7's\n   681\tdependency closure (`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` D5, D6); this document\n   682\tfixes only that the response is a vector of `[manifest image]` pairs in\n   683\tinstall order and that a cycle is one unit hashed with canonical member\n   684\tordering. How SCC identity is minted stays open (section 12).\n   685\t\n   686\t## 8. The manifest registry\n   687\t\n   688\t### 8.1 The module manifest\n   689\t\n   690\tA manifest is content: one map, materialized in `dao.jing`, named by its\n   691\taddress. It replaces the `{sym fn}` dictionary entry.\n   692\t\n   693\t```clojure\n   694\t{:yin.module/name      'my.lib\n   695\t :yin.module/contract  \"v2\"\n   696\t :yin.module/images    {:yin.ast/code          :segment/...   ; root row\n   697\t                        :yin.semantic/code     :segment/...   ; vector\n   698\t                        :yin.debruijn.code     H\n   699\t                        :yin.debruijn.register R}\n   700\t :yin.module/index     {H :segment/... R :segment/...}        ; optional\n   701\t :yin.module/exports   #{'foo 'bar}\n   702\t :yin.module/requires  #{'other.lib}\n   703\t :yin.module/primitives {'println {:yin.k/profile ... }}     ; UCF 7.5.2\n   704\t :yin.module/footprint {:store-keys #{} :effects #{}}}       ; UCF 7.6.1\n   705\t```\n   706\t\n   707\tRules:\n   708\t\n   709\t- `:yin.module/images` holds one identity per format the publisher\n   710\t  lowered. A format absent here is `:unsupported-format` for a kernel\n   711\t  that needs it; pairing fallback (section 5.5) reads this map.\n   712\t- `:yin.module/index` maps contract-pinned identities to their storage\n   713\t  addresses, the H and R indexes of B6 section 6 carried beside the\n   714\t  identities they resolve. A publisher may omit it when the composition\n   715\t  holds the index elsewhere. Storage-derived identities need no entry.\n   716\t- Every identity in `:yin.module/images` was minted from the same tree;\n   717\t  the manifest is the per-root pairing record.\n   718\t- `:yin.module/exports` are names, checked after evaluation (section\n   719\t  7.3). `:yin.module/primitives` declares host functions the module's\n   720\t  code assumes, by profile, so step 5 can refuse a receiver that binds a\n   721\t  same-named primitive of different profile (UCF section 7.5.2).\n   722\t- `:yin.module/footprint` is what `yin.vm.dependency-completion.md`\n   723\t  needs to report `:complete`; an undeclared footprint is `:incomplete`,\n   724\t  never a silent under-approximation.\n   725\t- The manifest is verified like any content: a `:yin.module/manifest`\n   726\t  format record with `:hash-fn jing/segment-key`, a validator over the\n   727\t  keys above, no free names, no parts. It passes steps 2 to 4 before\n   728\t  any image it names is requested.\n   729\t\n   730\t### 8.2 The name environment\n   731\t\n   732\tNames live outside the manifest. A name environment maps a module name\n   733\tto a manifest address, and it is composition data in one of two shapes:\n   734\t\n   735\t- a plain map `{'my.lib :segment/...}`, for tests and single-process\n   736\t  compositions;\n   737\t- datoms `[name :yin.module/manifest address]` in a `dao.space`\n   738\t  index the composition reads, so that publication, retraction, trust,\n   739\t  and provenance are ordinary facts (D6: \"a name environment is a value\n   740\t  or stream with a declared ledger\").\n   741\t\n   742\tAn entry is a claim. The manifest it names is verified; the name is\n   743\tnot, because a name has no hash. Who may assert a name for whom is the\n   744\tauthority question section 12 leaves open.\n   745\t\n   746\t### 8.3 What `yin.vm.module` becomes\n   747\t\n   748\tThe namespace keeps its shape and changes its contents.\n   749\t\n   750\t| Today                        | After                                       |\n   751\t|------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|\n   752\t| `register-module r name fns` | `register-host-module r name fns profiles`: |\n   753\t|                              | a host module, declared at wiring, entered  |\n   754\t|                              | as an already-linked manifest whose exports |\n   755\t|                              | are primitives by profile; still no code    |\n   756\t|                              | fetched, because the host is the boundary   |\n   757\t| `resolve-module r sym`       | unchanged signature; reads `:bindings`      |\n   758\t| `require-handler`            | registry hit answers; miss parks and emits  |\n   759\t|                              | (section 7.2); never throws for absence     |\n   760\t| `register-effect-handler`    | unchanged                                   |\n   761\t| `stream-module`              | a host module; `register-stream-module`     |\n   762\t|                              | calls `register-host-module`                |\n   763\t| `default-registry`           | unchanged                                   |\n   764\t| (new) `link-module r m img`  | the install step of section 7.3             |\n   765\t\n   766\tThe registry value's `:modules` map changes from `{name {sym fn}}` to\n   767\t`{name {:manifest m :address a :bindings {sym value}}}`. The stream\n   768\tmodule and every other host module get a synthesized manifest with\n   769\t`:yin.module/images {}` and their exports listed as primitives with\n   770\tprofiles; they are \"linked\" by declaration. No shim keeps the old entry\n   771\tshape: this is a dev-only repository with no deployed registries, and a\n   772\tclean break is the default here.\n   773\t\n   774\t## 9. Migration from `yin.vm.debruijn-linker`\n   775\t\n   776\tFive steps, each landing green on all three hosts before the next. The\n   777\tpredecessor namespace is renamed, not aliased.\n   778\t\n   779\t```mermaid\n   780\tflowchart TD\n   781\t  M1[\"M1 rename\\nyin.vm.debruijn-linker -> yin.vm.linker\"]\n   782\t  M2[\"M2 four format records\\n+ :parts-fn worklist\"]\n   783\t  M3[\"M3 stepped core\\nlink-state/step; fetch = host policy\"]\n   784\t  M4[\"M4 require lowers to the linker\\nmanifests, registry, :link wait\"]\n   785\t  M5[\"M5 yin.repl wires the four streams\\nper backend\"]\n   786\t  M1 --> M2 --> M3 --> M4 --> M5\n   787\t```\n   788\t\n   789\t**M1. Rename.** Move `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc` to\n   790\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc` with namespace `yin.vm.linker`; move the\n   791\ttest likewise. Update the four design documents that name the old\n   792\tnamespace (`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`, `yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`,\n   793\t`dao.agent.md`, `dao.agent.harness.md`, `dao.agent.mcp.server.md`) and\n   794\tthe orchestrator log. Mark `yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` as superseded by\n   795\tthis document in its status line. No behavior changes. The B6 test\n   796\tsuite passes unchanged apart from the require alias.\n   797\t\n   798\t**M2. Four format records.** Add `:contract` and `:parts-fn` to the two\n   799\texisting records; add `ast-format` and `semantic-format` (section 5)\n   800\tand `semantic-free-names`. Make step 2 the worklist. `yin.vm.content`\n   801\tkeeps `materialize-tree!` and `materialize-vector!` as the mint side\n   802\t(they are `publish!` for those formats); `load-rows` and `fetch-vector`\n   803\tare retired, and their two callers move to `fetch` with the new\n   804\trecords. The tri-host tests of B6 section 11 gain the same refusal\n   805\tmatrix for the two new formats, plus: a tree with one absent child row\n   806\tis `:absent` naming that row; a tree with one corrupt child row is\n   807\t`:address-mismatch` naming it; a vector whose index entry points at a\n   808\tdifferent valid vector is `:hash-mismatch`.\n   809\t\n   810\t**M3. Stepped core.** Implement `link-state`, `request-link`, `step`,\n   811\t`abandon` over `dao.jing.remote.step` (or, for a purely local\n   812\tcomposition, over `dao.stream.rpc` on a ring-buffer pair served by\n   813\t`dao.jing.remote/serve-content!`'s handlers). Reimplement `fetch` as the\n   814\tblocking driver over it and export `verify`. Tests: the full B6 refusal\n   815\tmatrix through `step` over ring buffers on all three hosts; the JVM\n   816\tWebSocket path from B6 completion criterion 1; a `:pending` sequence\n   817\twhere the content server answers one part per step.\n   818\t\n   819\t**M4. `require` lowers to the linker.** The manifest record and its\n   820\tformat record; `register-host-module`; `require-handler` parks and\n   821\temits; the `:link` wait entry through `check-wait-set`; `link-module`\n   822\twith isolated evaluation and `:export-missing`; the UCF table\n   823\tamendments. Tests, per backend: a require of an unlinked module over\n   824\tring buffers completes and the program resumes with its exports bound;\n   825\ta missing export refuses; a shadowed export name in the requiring\n   826\tprogram's store is `:shadowed-free`; a module requiring another links\n   827\ttransitively; `(vm :stack)` and `(vm :register)` in `yin.repl` link the\n   828\tsame manifest by H and R respectively and produce B0-equal results.\n   829\t\n   830\t**M5. `yin.repl` wiring.** The REPL composes the link pair per VM and\n   831\tthe content pair per connection (`connect` already opens the RPC\n   832\tclient). `(require 'foo)` at the prompt exercises the whole path.\n   833\t\n   834\tCoexistence during M4: host modules are entered by\n   835\t`register-host-module` from the first commit of M4, so every existing\n   836\tcomposition keeps working while linked modules are added. There is no\n   837\tperiod in which two registry shapes coexist.\n   838\t\n   839\t## 10. File box\n   840\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
