/jing/dht.cljc:270:  \"Wrap an IDhtNet transport and a local content handle as a DHT\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:275:      :put-content-fn f, :get-content-fn g, :close-fn c}\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:278:   :net is an IDhtNet transport; :local is a content handle carrying\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:279:   :put-content-fn and :get-content-fn (dao.jing.mem/create-content-mem or\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:293:  (when-not (fn? (:get-content-fn local))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:294:    (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing.dht local requires :get-content-fn\"\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:300:           :get-content-fn (make-get handle)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '35,170p' docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md | nl -ba -v 35\nsed -n '200,315p' src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc | nl -ba -v 200\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '35,170p' docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md | nl -ba -v 35\nsed -n '200,315p' src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc | nl -ba -v 200\"","aggregated_output":"    35\t| Local-first durable write, best-effort replication | Implemented |\n    36\t| Hash-verified fetch with local caching | Implemented |\n    37\t\n    38\t## The store handle\n    39\t\n    40\t`create-content-dht` wraps an `IDhtNet` transport and a local content handle\n    41\t(e.g. `dao.jing.mem/create-content-mem`) into a plain-data content-store\n    42\thandle map:\n    43\t\n    44\t```clojure\n    45\t{:net net, :local local, :closed-atom a,\n    46\t :put-content-fn f, :get-content-fn g, :close-fn c}\n    47\t```\n    48\t\n    49\tIt is consumed by `dao.jing/materialize!`, `dao.jing/get`, and\n    50\t`dao.jing/close!` exactly like any other backend. The local handle is this\n    51\tnode's own copy of the content it serves and caches; an acknowledged write\n    52\talways lands locally first (see *Writes* below).\n    53\t\n    54\t`create-content-dht-udp` (in `dao.jing.dht.node`) is the convenience\n    55\tconstructor: open a UDP node and wrap it as a DHT content-store handle. The\n    56\tnode and the store share `local`, so the peer serves the same bytes it reads.\n    57\t\n    58\t## Writes: local durable insert, then best-effort replication\n    59\t\n    60\t`put` validates the address/payload pair, writes the local backend first, and\n    61\tonly after the local write durably succeeds replicates to the `k` nearest\n    62\tnon-self peers:\n    63\t\n    64\t1. **Local durable insert.** The payload is inserted into the local content\n    65\t   handle (insert-if-absent). The backend verdict must be an explicit\n    66\t   `:inserted` or `:present`; a local collision throws. The address is never\n    67\t   acknowledged before this write lands.\n    68\t2. **Best-effort replication.** The `k` nearest non-self peers to the content\n    69\t   hash are located by an iterative lookup, and `store-content!` is sent to\n    70\t   each. Replication is best-effort: a transport timeout, refusal, or fault\n    71\t   simply means that peer is not replicated to. Nothing in the local\n    72\t   acknowledgement depends on the replication outcome.\n    73\t\n    74\tThe acknowledgement's **value** is independent of replication, but its\n    75\t**timing** is not. The current content handle completes the bounded replication\n    76\tattempts before returning the local `:inserted` or `:present` verdict. On the\n    77\tJVM the peer calls run in parallel and the caller waits for all of them, so the\n    78\tnetwork portion is bounded by the slowest peer attempt; other hosts run the\n    79\tattempts synchronously in peer order. An asynchronous outbox would be a\n    80\tdifferent lifecycle and causality contract, and is not implicit in\n    81\t\"best-effort.\"\n    82\t\n    83\tBecause payloads are content-addressed, replication is idempotent and\n    84\tconvergent: every copy of a value is byte-identical to every other, so no\n    85\tordering or reconciliation is required between the local copy and remote\n    86\tcopies.\n    87\t\n    88\t## Reads: local first, hash-verified fetch, cached\n    89\t\n    90\t`get` reads the local backend first. On a hit it returns the value directly,\n    91\tno network. On a miss it:\n    92\t\n    93\t1. runs the iterative lookup to converge on the `k` nearest known peers to\n    94\t   the content hash;\n    95\t2. asks candidates with `fetch-content` until one answers; every fetch is\n    96\t   bounded by the transport's timeout and treated as unreachable when it\n    97\t   expires;\n    98\t3. verifies the fetched payload by hashing it against the requested address\n    99\t   (a mismatch is discarded and the next candidate tried);\n   100\t4. caches the verified value through the local backend (`materialize!`), so a\n   101\t   second read of the same segment is a local hit; and\n   102\t5. returns not-found when no candidate produced a verifiable value.\n   103\t\n   104\tThe verification step is what makes `get` safe against a peer that claims to\n   105\thold content it does not: the address is the digest of the payload, so a\n   106\tlying peer fails the check without ceremony.\n   107\t\n   108\t## The transport protocol (`IDhtNet`)\n   109\t\n   110\t`IDhtNet` is the per-peer RPC surface the store requires of a transport. It\n   111\thas exactly six methods:\n   112\t\n   113\t- `self-peer [net]` — this node's own `{:id :host :port}` peer map.\n   114\t- `known-peers [net target-id n]` — the `n` locally-known peers nearest\n   115\t  `target-id`, nearest first; no IO.\n   116\t- `find-closer [net peer target-id]` — ask `peer` for the peers it knows\n   117\t  nearest `target-id`; nil when unreachable.\n   118\t- `store-content! [net peer address payload]` — ask `peer` to hold content;\n   119\t  a boolean acknowledgement, false/nil when the peer is unreachable or\n   120\t  refuses. Implementations must own bounded transport timeouts.\n   121\t- `fetch-content [net peer address]` — ask `peer` for content;\n   122\t  `{:found? bool :value v}` when reachable, nil when unreachable.\n   123\t  Implementations must own bounded transport timeouts.\n   124\t- `close-net! [net]` — release the transport's local resources (socket,\n   125\t  threads).\n   126\t\n   127\tThe store's iterative lookup (`dao.jing.dht/lookup`) uses only these methods:\n   128\tit converges on the `k` nearest known peers to a target id, deduplicating\n   129\tpeer ids so a peer is never asked twice in one lookup.\n   130\t\n   131\t## The UDP wire (`dao.jing.dht.node`)\n   132\t\n   133\tThe reference transport is a UDP Kademlia peer (`create-node`). Its wire ops\n   134\tare exactly five: `:ping`, `:find-node`, `:store-content`, `:fetch-content`,\n   135\tand `:reply`. One Transit-JSON message map per datagram: requests carry\n   136\t`{:op ... :rpc n :from peer}`; replies carry\n   137\t`{:op :reply :rpc n :from peer :value ...}`.\n   138\t\n   139\t- **Request-response over fire-and-forget UDP.** Reliability is owned by the\n   140\t  client as per-attempt timeouts and retries (`:timeout-ms` default 500,\n   141\t  `:tries` default 3); a peer that stays unreachable is answered as nil by the\n   142\t  `IDhtNet` contract, never thrown.\n   143\t- **Routing knowledge accretes from traffic.** Every received `:from` is\n   144\t  observed into the local k-bucket table, so peers learn each other from the\n   145\t  messages they exchange, not from a separate discovery service.\n   146\t- **Peers are untrusted.** An incoming `:store-content` re-verifies the\n   147\t  content address against the payload before writing; a mismatch is refused.\n   148\t- **Replies go to the datagram's source address**, not the claimed `:from`.\n   149\t\n   150\t## Operational limits (current)\n   151\t\n   152\t- **JVM only.** The UDP node uses `java.net.DatagramSocket`; there is no\n   153\t  browser or Node peer. Browsers cannot send or receive raw UDP — the closest\n   154\t  reachable primitives (WebRTC DataChannel, WebTransport) are outside this\n   155\t  transport. The file stays `.cljc` to leave room for non-JVM branches; on\n   156\t  other platforms it defines nothing.\n   157\t- **One datagram per message.** Anything beyond the 1200-byte datagram budget\n   158\t  is refused on send and dropped on reply, never torn. Segments that exceed\n   159\t  the budget stay local-only until fragmentation (DRDS) exists: store degrades\n   160\t  to best-effort, fetch times out.\n   161\t- **No peer-returnability validation or rate limiting yet.** Incoming\n   162\t  `:store-content` is content-address verified, but is not guarded by\n   163\t  returnability cookies or per-peer limits. UDP amplification hardening is\n   164\t  open.\n   165\t- **NAT traversal is unaddressed.** A global peer-to-peer grid behind NAT\n   166\t  requires hole-punching, relay/TURN, and bootstrap discovery. Until then the\n   167\t  DHT works where peers can reach each other's UDP sockets directly.\n   168\t- **Storage economics / GC.** Unbounded caching is unbounded storage growth.\n   169\t  There is no pinning, eviction, or reclamation policy, so `get` is best-effort\n   170\t  by default and superseded content accumulates.\n   200\t                              (future\n   201\t                                (safe-store-content! net peer address payload)))\n   202\t                            peers))\n   203\t           :default (run! (fn [peer]\n   204\t                            (safe-store-content! net peer address payload))\n   205\t                          peers)))\n   206\t      result)))\n   207\t\n   208\t\n   209\t(defn- make-get\n   210\t  [{:keys [net local], :as handle}]\n   211\t  (fn [address not-found]\n   212\t    (ensure-open handle)\n   213\t    (let [v (jing/get local address content-missing)]\n   214\t      (if (not (identical? v content-missing))\n   215\t        v\n   216\t        (let [self-id (:id (self-peer net))\n   217\t              fetched\n   218\t              (some (fn [peer]\n   219\t                      (when (not= self-id (:id peer))\n   220\t                        (when-let [res (safe-fetch-content net peer address)]\n   221\t                          (when (and (:found? res)\n   222\t                                     (jing/segment-matches? address\n   223\t                                                            (:value res)))\n   224\t                            [(:value res)]))))\n   225\t                    (lookup net (content-target address)))]\n   226\t          (if fetched\n   227\t            (let [value (first fetched)\n   228\t                  algo (jing/segment-algorithm address)\n   229\t                  cached (jing/materialize! local value {:algorithm algo})]\n   230\t              (when-not (= address cached)\n   231\t                (throw\n   232\t                  (ex-info\n   233\t                    \"dao.jing.dht: fetched content cached under the wrong address\"\n   234\t                    {:address address, :cached cached})))\n   235\t              value)\n   236\t            not-found))))))\n   237\t\n   238\t\n   239\t(defn- make-close\n   240\t  [{:keys [net local closed-atom]}]\n   241\t  (fn []\n   242\t    (with-lock closed-atom\n   243\t      (fn []\n   244\t        (when-not @closed-atom\n   245\t          (let [err-net (try (close-net! net)\n   246\t                             nil\n   247\t                             (catch #?(:clj Throwable\n   248\t                                       :cljs :default\n   249\t                                       :cljd Object)\n   250\t                                    e\n   251\t                               e))\n   252\t                err-local (try (jing/close! local)\n   253\t                               nil\n   254\t                               (catch #?(:clj Throwable\n   255\t                                         :cljs :default\n   256\t                                         :cljd Object)\n   257\t                                      e\n   258\t                                 e))]\n   259\t            (if (or err-net err-local)\n   260\t              (throw (or err-net err-local))\n   261\t              (reset! closed-atom true))))\n   262\t        nil))))\n   263\t\n   264\t\n   265\t;; =============================================================================\n   266\t;; Handle constructor\n   267\t;; =============================================================================\n   268\t\n   269\t(defn create-content-dht\n   270\t  \"Wrap an IDhtNet transport and a local content handle as a DHT\n   271\t   content-store handle.\n   272\t\n   273\t   The returned handle is plain data:\n   274\t     {:net net, :local local, :closed-atom a,\n   275\t      :put-content-fn f, :get-content-fn g, :close-fn c}\n   276\t\n   277\t   and works with dao.jing/materialize!, dao.jing/get, and dao.jing/close!.\n   278\t   :net is an IDhtNet transport; :local is a content handle carrying\n   279\t   :put-content-fn and :get-content-fn (dao.jing.mem/create-content-mem or\n   280\t   equivalent); :closed-atom is the store's explicit private state and close\n   281\t   lock.\n   282\t\n   283\t   The DHT routes only registered :segment/<algorithm>-... content addresses\n   284\t   and records no source identity: there are no roots, CAS records, deletes,\n   285\t   or intake streams.\"\n   286\t  [{:keys [net local]}]\n   287\t  (when-not (and net local)\n   288\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing.dht requires :net and :local\"\n   289\t                    {:net net, :local local})))\n   290\t  (when-not (fn? (:put-content-fn local))\n   291\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing.dht local requires :put-content-fn\"\n   292\t                    {:local local})))\n   293\t  (when-not (fn? (:get-content-fn local))\n   294\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing.dht local requires :get-content-fn\"\n   295\t                    {:local local})))\n   296\t  (let [closed-atom (atom false)\n   297\t        handle {:net net, :local local, :closed-atom closed-atom}]\n   298\t    (assoc handle\n   299\t           :put-content-fn (make-put handle)\n   300\t           :get-content-fn (make-get handle)\n   301\t           :close-fn (make-close handle))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
