{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1295,1390p' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc | nl -ba -v 1295\nrg -n \\\"defn relation|defn occurrences|defn free-names\\\" src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"  1295\t                  :node (when v [[pos v]])\n  1296\t                  :nodes (map-indexed (fn [i cid] [[pos i] cid]) v)\n  1297\t                  nil)))\n  1298\t            (range (count slots)) slots (drop 2 row))))\n  1299\t\n  1300\t\n  1301\t(defn occurrences\n  1302\t  \"§6.1: the occurrence relation of a projected row set — one\n  1303\t   `[root path node]` tuple per place, so a structurally shared subtree\n  1304\t   (§4.4) yields one tuple per place and one node id. Computed by the same\n  1305\t   walk as the projection, so it is generic over every §2.3 tag. It is a\n  1306\t   pure function of the tree and carries no origin; a query fixes origin\n  1307\t   from the composition's side and joins on `[root path]` (§6.1). Assumes\n  1308\t   the walk terminates: projected ids are content addresses of their\n  1309\t   children, so a projected set is acyclic by construction (§4.1).\"\n  1310\t  [{:keys [root rows]}]\n  1311\t  (loop [frontier [[[] root]]\n  1312\t         tuples []]\n  1313\t    (if (empty? frontier)\n  1314\t      (set tuples)\n  1315\t      (let [children (mapcat (fn [[path id]]\n  1316\t                               (let [row (get rows id)]\n  1317\t                                 (map (fn [[step cid]] [(conj path step) cid])\n  1318\t                                      (when row (occurrence-child-places row)))))\n  1319\t                             frontier)]\n  1320\t        (recur children\n  1321\t               (into tuples (map (fn [[path id]] [root path id]) frontier)))))))\n  1322\t\n  1323\t\n  1324\t(def occurrence-rules\n  1325\t  \"§4.5/§7.7: root-scoped occurrence rules. `?root` is threaded through\n  1326\t   every head (`p-up`/`occ-anc`/`occ-bound?`) and the `[$occ …]` join is\n  1327\t   constrained to one root, so a binder in another tree of the same\n  1328\t   relation can never classify this tree's occurrence — the gap §4.5 names\n  1329\t   in an unscoped rule set. The ancestor walk is over path prefixes\n  1330\t   (`path-pop`), not row edges, so it is generic over every §2.3 tag. `$`\n  1331\t   must be the row relation and `$occ` the occurrence relation; the rules\n  1332\t   need `member?` and `path-pop` under `:fns` — see `occurrence-fns`.\n  1333\t   `?root` must be bound at every call: the bodies never bind it, and an\n  1334\t   unscoped invocation fails loudly rather than enumerate.\"\n  1335\t  '[[(p-up ?root ?child ?parent) [(path-pop ?child) ?parent]]\n  1336\t    [(occ-anc ?root ?a ?d) (p-up ?root ?d ?a)]\n  1337\t    [(occ-anc ?root ?a ?d) (p-up ?root ?d ?m) (occ-anc ?root ?a ?m)]\n  1338\t    [(occ-bound? ?root ?path ?name)\n  1339\t     (occ-anc ?root ?lam-path ?path)\n  1340\t     [$occ ?root ?lam-path ?lam]\n  1341\t     [?lam :lambda ?params _]\n  1342\t     [(member? ?params ?name)]]])\n  1343\t\n  1344\t\n  1345\t(def occurrence-fns\n  1346\t  \"The `:fns` map `occurrence-rules` requires (§6.3: quoted-symbol keys):\n  1347\t   membership over a `:syms`/`:nodes` vector, and one step up a §2.5 path\n  1348\t   (nil at the root, where no occurrence tuple carries the answer anyway).\"\n  1349\t  {'member? (fn [coll x] (boolean (some #(= % x) coll))),\n  1350\t   'path-pop (fn [p] (when (pos? (count p)) (subvec p 0 (dec (count p)))))})\n  1351\t\n  1352\t\n  1353\t(defn free-names\n  1354\t  \"§7.7 free-name extraction as a function: every `:variable` name free at\n  1355\t   some occurrence of `root`'s tree. `db` is the row relation\n  1356\t   (`query/relation` over the rows) and `occ` the occurrence relation\n  1357\t   (`occurrences`); both may hold several trees unioned (§6.1) — the rules\n  1358\t   are root-scoped, so only `root`'s binder classifies `root`'s\n  1359\t   occurrences. Conservative by construction: a name whose row is bound at\n  1360\t   one occurrence and free at another is included, never silently dropped\n  1361\t   (§7.6.1).\"\n  1362\t  [db occ root]\n  1363\t  (set (map first\n  1364\t            (query/collect\n  1365\t              (query/q '[:find ?name :in $ $occ % ?root\n  1366\t                         :where\n  1367\t                         [$occ ?root ?path ?v]\n  1368\t                         [?v :variable ?name]\n  1369\t                         (not (occ-bound? ?root ?path ?name))]\n  1370\t                       db occ occurrence-rules root\n  1371\t                       {:fns occurrence-fns})))))\n  1372\t\n  1373\t\n  1374\t;; =============================================================================\n  1375\t;; The footprint table and the §7.7 syntactic extraction queries\n  1376\t;; =============================================================================\n  1377\t;; §7.7: the code part of UCF §7.6.1's conservative fixed point is the\n  1378\t;; queries below, run over the flat rows of every reachable tree ($ast) and\n  1379\t;; the segment-qualified rows of every reachable segment ($code). They\n  1380\t;; supply the fixed point's inputs; the value, name-satisfaction, and\n  1381\t;; module parts remain the walk UCF §7.6.1 specifies. Free names are\n  1382\t;; `free-names` above; everything else the syntactic queries fill is here.\n  1383\t\n  1384\t(def footprint-table\n  1385\t  \"§7.7.1's effect normalization as a data value, keyed by the execution\n  1386\t   contract stamp (UCF §7.3.3) — the effect a syntax tag or mnemonic raises\n  1387\t   is part of that contract, lifted out of the reference machine's hot loop\n  1388\t   (`semantic.cljc`). Two directions: `:tags` over every §2.3 syntax tag\n  1389\t   and `:mnemonics` over every §2.4 mnemonic, each to the set of effect\n  1390\t   identifiers it contributes. Every tag and every mnemonic has a row, so\n1301:(defn occurrences\n1353:(defn free-names\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
