{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-24 11:57:31 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-24 18:57:31 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\nNo P0 findings.\n\nP1 | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:345` | The AST hash function hashes `(subvec root-row 1)`, but lines 353–359 establish that the fetched root value is already the stored body `[tag & slots]`. This drops the tag and guarantees a false `:hash-mismatch`. | Use `jing/segment-key` directly on the fetched body; prepend the address only when assembling `{id [id tag & slots]}` for validation.\n\nP1 | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:256` | The index may be a function, while the supposedly plain-data request at lines 487–495 carries both `:yin.link/index` and a receiver containing stores, primitives, and modules. Such values may contain functions, handles, or ambient state and cannot safely cross the link stream. Step 1 also has no defined asynchronous index-resolution protocol. | Make link requests contain only portable identifiers, contracts, and profile requirements. Keep indexes and receiver capability tables in explicitly composed linker-local state, or define a separate stepped stream service for remote index resolution.\n\nP1 | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:289` | Free-name verification checks only whether a symbol is present. Yet lines 719–721 promise refusal when a same-named primitive has a different profile. Since `:free-names-fn` returns only symbols, the six-step pipeline has no publisher profile or manifest identity to compare. | Replace bare names with dependency obligations such as `{name expected-profile}` or `{module expected-manifest}` and require exact profile/address equality.\n\nP1 | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:593` | The proposed `:link` wait entry omits the exact request and request-stream reference, although line 615 requires retaining and retrying the request after `full`. It also never requires minting the response cursor before the append, permitting an immediate response to land before the cursor and be skipped. | Define separate `:link-request` and `:link-response` pending states, analogous to UCF’s `:ffi-request`/`:ffi`: mint the kept cursor first, retain the complete unsent envelope and request descriptor, and transition to response-wait only after append returns `ok`.\n\nP1 | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:627` | Responses carry `:yin.link/id`, but lines 630–638 wake a wait entry on any `ok` without specifying that the identifier must match. Multiple outstanding waits on one retained response stream can therefore consume or install another request’s result. | Specify one response router cursor that dispatches completions by ID, or require each independent cursor to advance past nonmatching responses and restore only on exact correlation. Define duplicate, late, unknown, and abandoned-ID handling.\n\nP1 | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:640` | The claim that installation requires no new machinery conflicts with lines 647–678: the VM must run a module in a separate isolated store, detect its halt, preserve the requiring continuation, and allow that child evaluation to park on another `require`. A synchronous call from wait restoration cannot satisfy these rules. | Specify installation as an explicit scheduler state machine or child task: `loading → parked/running → validated → linked/refused`, with its own VM/store and waiter set. Publish bindings and wake the parent only after successful halt and export validation.\n\nP1 | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:425` | Four-way same-root pairing is merely asserted through the manifest. The retained verifier proves only H/R by re-lowering; nothing prevents a manifest from combining individually valid AST, semantic, H, and R images from different source trees, producing backend-dependent behavior. | Pin one canonical tree identity and content-addressed, profile-stamped derivation records in the manifest. Verify every selected image against that derivation, with swapped-image tests for all formats.\n\nP1 | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:571` | The resolved manifest’s `:yin.module/name` is never compared with the requested name. A mapping for `'foo` can point to a valid manifest declaring `'bar`, which will still pass content verification and be installed under the requested registry key. | Add a name-binding verification immediately after manifest validation and refuse mismatches with a qualified `:module-name-mismatch`.\n\nP1 | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:911` | Authority and duplicate-name resolution remain open, yet M4 enables module loading from `dao.space` assertions without making this decision an entry criterion. Multiple or malicious assertions can yield nondeterministic or substituted module content. | Block M4 on an explicit fail-closed policy: authorized assertion provenance, ledger/snapshot scope, and either exactly one accepted manifest or an `:ambiguous-name` refusal.\n\nP2 | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:170` | AST and semantic VM identities are explicitly equal to Jing storage addresses. Storage-encoder migration therefore changes executable identity, so storage address is not strictly decoupled from VM identity across all four VMs. | Introduce contract-pinned AST and semantic identities plus explicit identity-to-storage indexes, amending UCF and code-as-tuples accordingly; otherwise state clearly that strict decoupling applies only to H/R.\n\nP2 | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:370` | The storage-derived formats recompute identity using unary `jing/segment-key`, which uses the current minting default. Existing addresses carry their own algorithm; a value minted under another registered algorithm or an earlier default can pass address verification and then fail identity verification spuriously. | Make identity verification identity-directed, e.g. an `:identity-matches-fn [identity value]` using `jing/segment-matches?` for storage-derived formats. Keep H/R’s contract-pinned hash functions separate.\n\nP2 | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:239` | `:parts-fn` follows child addresses before structural validation at step 4. An address-valid but malformed AST row can force an unbounded breadth-first fetch of attacker-selected addresses before being rejected. | Perform row-local tag, arity, slot-kind, and saturation validation before enqueueing children; impose composition-supplied limits on part count, depth, and total bytes.\n\nP2 | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:523` | The retained `fetch(handle, …)` signature does not provide the RPC state, stream pair, content-server driver, or cadence needed by the stepped core. Calling `jing/get` directly becomes a local fast path; merely looping `step` can deadlock unless another driver advances the server. | Accept an explicit link runtime/driver instead of a raw Jing handle, and specify who steps both sides. Add a test proving local fetch traverses the same request/response protocol and never invokes direct `jing/get`.\n\nP2 | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:459` | The topology labels DHT as a content pair, but the existing DHT is a synchronous `dao.jing` handle over `IDhtNet`, not a stepped DaoStream endpoint. No adapter or driver is specified, so peer-network equivalence is asserted rather than achieved. | Place the DHT handle behind an explicitly driven content-server stream boundary, or define a portable stepped DHT client. Add this topology and its non-blocking test to M3.\n\nP2 | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:750` | `register-host-module` accepts arbitrary functions and profiles and marks them already linked. The specification does not require functions to be pure constructors or declared effect producers, reject `:host`/captured-state profiles, or prevent direct host IO. | Enforce UCF profile classes at registration: accept only `:pure` or declared `:effectful` functions with `:host-state :none`; require effectful exports to return plain effect data. Keep `stream/make` as a pure effect constructor and `:make-stream` as the explicit engine boundary capability.\n\nHygiene: No actionable findings. `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md` contains no non-ASCII lines and no line exceeds 80 columns. No files were edited."}}
